(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1d.bae5eb8fd7.js"]={"47":[{"id":"sdl47_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which gene mutation underlies Marfan syndrome, and how do the resulting protein defects primarily contribute to connective tissue instability across multiple organ systems?","choices":{"A":"Mutations in FBN2 (chromosome 5q23) reduce fibrillin-2 production in elastic microfibrils, destabilizing connective tissue networks exclusively during embryonic development and producing arachnodactyly without cardiovascular involvement.","B":"Mutations in COL1A1 reduce type I collagen synthesis, weakening load-bearing connective tissues in the skeleton, eyes, and cardiovascular system through structural scaffold loss without TGF-beta dysregulation.","C":"Mutations in COL3A1 reduce type III collagen synthesis in vascular walls, producing aortic dilation and dissection along with joint laxity through impaired vascular matrix structural support and increased wall compliance.","D":"Mutations in TGFBR2 increase TGF-beta receptor signaling constitutively in all connective tissues, producing the full Marfan phenotype through overactivation of fibrotic and matrix metalloproteinase-mediated degradation pathways.","E":"Mutations in FBN1 (chromosome 15q21) reduce fibrillin-1 scaffolding in extracellular microfibrils and dysregulate TGF-beta bioavailability, together weakening microfibril networks in the skeleton, eyes, and cardiovascular system."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Marfan syndrome is caused by heterozygous FBN1 mutations on chromosome 15q21. Fibrillin-1 serves two roles: (1) structural scaffold for ECM microfibrils providing tensile strength to connective tissues in bone, eye, and aorta; (2) sequestration of latent TGF-beta complexes in the ECM. FBN1 mutations cause both structural scaffold loss AND excess TGF-beta release. FBN2 mutations cause congenital contractural arachnodactyly (CCA), not classic Marfan. COL1A1 mutations cause OI/EDS. COL3A1 mutations cause vascular EDS. TGFBR2 mutations cause Loeys-Dietz syndrome (similar to Marfan).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan = FBN1 (chr 15q21), fibrillin-1. Dual mechanism: structural loss + TGF-beta excess. Loeys-Dietz = TGFBR1/2 mutations (more aggressive aortic disease). COMLEX: Autosomal dominant, 75% familial, 25% de novo. Screen first-degree relatives.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Vascular remodeling from TGF-beta dysregulation in Marfan syndrome parallels the vasculopathy driving Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma renal crisis in systemic sclerosis.","whyNot":{"A":"FBN2 mutations cause congenita","B":"COL1A1 mutations cause OI/EDS.","C":"COL3A1 mutations cause vascula","D":"TGFBR2 mutations cause Loeys-D"}},{"id":"sdl47_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fibrillin-1 normally sequesters latent TGF-beta complexes in the extracellular matrix. How does loss-of-function FBN1 mutation lead to excess TGF-beta bioavailability, and what is the primary consequence of this dysregulation in the aortic wall?","choices":{"A":"Reduced fibrillin-1 releases sequestered TGF-beta from extracellular matrix storage sites, producing elevated free TGF-beta that drives smooth muscle cell apoptosis and cystic medial necrosis in the aortic wall, predisposing to aneurysm and dissection.","B":"Reduced fibrillin-1 prevents glycoprotein cross-linking in the extracellular matrix, exposing TGF-beta binding sites on collagen fibers that then constitutively activate adventitial fibroblasts through paracrine signaling and producing progressive aortic fibrosis.","C":"Reduced fibrillin-1 increases cleavage of latent TGF-beta binding proteins by matrix metalloproteinases, producing truncated TGF-beta precursors that paradoxically activate both pro-apoptotic and pro-survival pathways in aortic smooth muscle cells.","D":"Reduced fibrillin-1 promotes alternative splicing of TGF-beta receptor transcripts, creating constitutively active receptor isoforms that no longer require ligand binding to activate downstream SMAD2/3 signaling in aortic smooth muscle cells.","E":"Reduced fibrillin-1 activates plasminogen-to-plasmin conversion in aortic connective tissue, producing unregulated TGF-beta precursor proteolysis and releasing active TGF-beta independently of normal latent complex activation mechanisms."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fibrillin-1 normally sequesters latent TGF-beta by binding the large latent TGF-beta binding protein (LTBP) in the ECM. When fibrillin-1 is reduced, latent TGF-beta complexes are released from ECM storage, increasing bioavailable TGF-beta. In the aortic media, excess TGF-beta drives smooth muscle cell apoptosis, matrix metalloproteinase activation, and replacement of smooth muscle cells with pools of myxoid ground substance — the histologic lesion of cystic medial necrosis. This weakens the tunica media, predisposing to aortic root aneurysm at the sinuses of Valsalva, which can progress to Type A aortic dissection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FBN1 reduced → TGF-beta released from ECM → aortic smooth muscle apoptosis → cystic medial necrosis → aortic root aneurysm → Type A dissection. COMLEX: Losartan (AT1 receptor blocker) reduces TGF-beta signaling and slows aortic root dilation in Marfan syndrome clinical trials.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Vascular remodeling from TGF-beta dysregulation in Marfan syndrome parallels the vasculopathy driving Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma renal crisis in systemic sclerosis."},{"id":"sdl47_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Most FBN1 mutations in Marfan syndrome are missense mutations that produce a dominant negative effect rather than simple haploinsufficiency. Which molecular mechanism best explains dominant negative pathogenicity in fibrillin-1 disease?","choices":{"A":"Dominant negative FBN1 mutations insert premature stop codons that produce truncated fibrillin-1 proteins, which are secreted into the ECM and competitively displace full-length fibrillin-1 from microfibril assembly sites through competitive receptor binding.","B":"Dominant negative FBN1 missense mutations reduce fibrillin-1 gene transcription through gain-of-function promoter modification effects, producing haploinsufficiency that accounts for most Marfan cases rather than the minority claimed in older literature.","C":"Dominant negative FBN1 mutations produce fibrillin-1 lacking the RGD integrin-binding motif, preventing smooth muscle cell attachment to microfibrils and causing aortic media apoptosis through anoikis-mediated cell death at the integrin-fibrillin interface.","D":"Dominant negative FBN1 missense mutations produce structurally abnormal fibrillin-1 that co-assembles with normal subunits in forming microfibrils, disrupting the whole polymer and causing more damage than halving normal protein production would cause","E":"Dominant negative FBN1 mutations delete the calcium-binding EGF-like domains of fibrillin-1, preventing calcium-dependent protein folding and thereby inactivating all fibrillin-1 subunits including those encoded by the intact wild-type allele."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fibrillin-1 assembles into head-to-tail microfibrils with multiple subunits. When a missense FBN1 mutation produces structurally abnormal fibrillin-1, these abnormal subunits incorporate into the microfibril polymer alongside normal subunits. Because microfibrils are long-chain polymers, even a minority of abnormal subunits can disrupt the structural integrity of entire polymer chains — this is the dominant negative mechanism. In contrast, haploinsufficiency (simply reduced protein production) accounts for a minority of cases; most FBN1 mutations are missense mutations that give rise to abnormal fibrillin-1 (Robbins Ch 5). Premature stop codons typically cause haploinsufficiency via nonsense-mediated decay rather than dominant negative effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dominant negative = abnormal protein incorporates into and disrupts the polymer; worse than simple haploinsufficiency. Haploinsufficiency (reduced gene dosage) = minority of Marfan. Most FBN1 mutations = missense, dominant negative. COMLEX: Distinguish haploinsufficiency from dominant negative — key concept in genetic disease mechanisms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Vascular remodeling from TGF-beta dysregulation in Marfan syndrome parallels the vasculopathy driving Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma renal crisis in systemic sclerosis.","whyNot":{"B":"In contrast, haploinsufficienc"}},{"id":"sdl47_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old tall male with Marfan syndrome undergoes echocardiography showing mitral valve prolapse and aortic root dilation at the sinuses of Valsalva. He asks which cardiovascular complication represents the leading cause of premature death in Marfan syndrome.","choices":{"A":"Mitral valve prolapse with progressive mitral regurgitation leading to left ventricular volume overload and eventual systolic dysfunction represents the leading cause of premature cardiovascular death in Marfan syndrome patients.","B":"Pulmonary artery aneurysm and rupture due to fibrillin-1 deficiency in the pulmonary arterial media represents the most common lethal cardiovascular complication in Marfan syndrome, occurring before aortic disease in most patients.","C":"Spontaneous pneumothorax from bilateral apical pulmonary bleb rupture represents the leading cause of premature death in tall, thin Marfan syndrome patients and carries higher mortality risk than the cardiovascular complications.","D":"Progressive aortic regurgitation from annular dilation causing acute left ventricular failure represents the single most common immediate cause of death in Marfan syndrome, occurring before dissection in most untreated patients.","E":"Aortic dissection originating at the aortic root (Type A) due to cystic medial necrosis in the tunica media is the leading cause of premature death in Marfan syndrome, arising where fibrillin-1-deficient media is most vulnerable"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aortic dissection (Type A, originating at the aortic root) is the leading cause of premature death in Marfan syndrome. Cystic medial necrosis weakens the aortic root at the sinuses of Valsalva, leading to progressive dilation and eventually dissection. Untreated, aortic dissection is fatal. Prophylactic aortic root replacement is recommended once the root is significantly dilated. Mitral valve prolapse is common (40-50%) but rarely causes death. Aortic regurgitation is a consequence of aortic root dilation but not the primary cause of death. Spontaneous pneumothorax occurs but is not the leading cause of death.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leading cause of death in Marfan = Type A aortic dissection. Surgery once the aortic root is significantly dilated. Beta-blockers slow dilation rate. COMLEX: Marfan screening: annual echo for aortic root diameter. Also screen first-degree family members.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 2 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon occurs in connective tissue diseases including Marfan syndrome, linking its multi-organ vascular phenotype to the broader spectrum of secondary vasomotor disorders.","whyNot":{"A":"Mitral valve prolapse is commo","C":"Spontaneous pneumothorax occur","D":"Aortic regurgitation is a cons"}},{"id":"sdl47_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female with Marfan syndrome is referred to ophthalmology. Slit-lamp examination reveals bilateral lens subluxation. In which direction does ectopia lentis characteristically displace in Marfan syndrome, and which structural element is primarily deficient?","choices":{"A":"The lens displaces inferiorly and nasally in Marfan syndrome due to zonular fiber weakness caused by fibrillin-1 deficiency, in contrast to homocystinuria where the lens also displaces inferiorly but from cystathionine accumulation in ciliary muscle.","B":"The lens displaces anteriorly into the anterior chamber in Marfan syndrome as fibrillin-1-deficient zonular fibers elongate under intraocular pressure, producing acute angle-closure glaucoma as the characteristic primary ocular emergency.","C":"The lens displaces laterally toward the temporal visual field in Marfan syndrome due to asymmetric zonular fiber weakness on the nasal side, reflecting the mosaic distribution of fibrillin-1 expression across the ciliary body circumference.","D":"The lens displaces superiorly and temporally in Marfan syndrome due to weakness of fibrillin-1-containing zonular fibers that suspend the lens from the ciliary body, contrasting with the inferior and nasal displacement seen in homocystinuria.","E":"The lens displaces posteriorly into the vitreous humor in Marfan syndrome as fibrillin-1-deficient posterior zonular fibers undergo progressive TGF-beta-mediated degeneration from chronically elevated TGF-beta in the vitreous compartment."},"correct":"D","explanation":"In Marfan syndrome, ectopia lentis (lens subluxation) is bilateral and displaces superiorly and temporally (upward and outward), reflecting weakness in the superior zonular fibers (suspensory ligaments of the lens) that contain fibrillin-1. In homocystinuria, lens dislocation is classically inferior and nasal (downward and inward) due to homocysteine-mediated damage to zonular fibers — the classic board comparison. Bilateral ectopia lentis in a young patient warrants evaluation for both Marfan syndrome and homocystinuria. The mnemonic: Marfan = Up and Out; Homocystinuria = Down and In.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan ectopia lentis = bilateral, superior-temporal (up and out). Homocystinuria = inferior-nasal (down and in). COMLEX: Marfan vs homocystinuria: both tall + lens dislocation, but Marfan = normal intelligence + up/out; homocystinuria = intellectual disability + thrombosis + down/in.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 2 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon occurs in connective tissue diseases including Marfan syndrome, linking its multi-organ vascular phenotype to the broader spectrum of secondary vasomotor disorders.","whyNot":{"A":"In homocystinuria, lens disloc"}},{"id":"sdl47_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old male with Marfan syndrome has a thoracolumbar scoliosis of 38 degrees. His parents ask if this skeletal finding shares the same molecular basis as his concurrent aortic root dilation. Which statement correctly explains the shared molecular underpinning of both manifestations?","choices":{"A":"Both scoliosis and aortic dilation arise from TGF-beta-driven fibrosis of paraspinal muscles and aortic adventitia through independent secondary mutations that each occur against the background of the primary FBN1 haploinsufficiency.","B":"Both scoliosis and aortic dilation arise from the same FBN1 mutation, which reduces fibrillin-1 in the microfibrils of both the spinal ligaments and the aortic tunica media, reflecting connective tissue-wide vulnerability from one gene defect","C":"Scoliosis in Marfan syndrome results from TGF-beta-mediated paraspinal myopathy that is independent of fibrillin-1 scaffold loss, whereas aortic dilation results exclusively from structural fibrillin-1 scaffold loss without TGF-beta involvement.","D":"Scoliosis and aortic root dilation arise from distinct secondary genes that co-segregate with FBN1 mutations in familial Marfan rather than from the primary FBN1 defect, which affects only ocular and cardiovascular connective tissues.","E":"Both complications arise from fibrillin-1-deficient microfibrils that activate the alternative complement pathway in connective tissue, generating C3a and C5a that recruit macrophages to degrade vertebral ligament collagen and aortic elastin."},"correct":"B","explanation":"FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, a ubiquitous ECM microfibril component present in elastic and non-elastic connective tissues throughout the body. Because fibrillin-1 is present in periosteum, ligaments, and cartilage of the spine as well as in the aortic media, a single FBN1 mutation produces multi-system disease. The skeletal manifestations (scoliosis, pectus, arachnodactyly, tall stature) and cardiovascular manifestations (aortic root dilation, MVP) all arise from the same underlying fibrillin-1 microfibril weakness plus TGF-beta dysregulation — the dual mechanism operating in all affected tissues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan is a systemic connective tissue disorder — all phenotypes arise from the single FBN1 defect: skeletal + ocular + cardiovascular. COMLEX: Ghent nosology for Marfan diagnosis uses major and minor criteria across these three systems; aortic root Z-score + ectopia lentis are the cardinal features.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 2 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon occurs in connective tissue diseases including Marfan syndrome, linking its multi-organ vascular phenotype to the broader spectrum of secondary vasomotor disorders."},{"id":"sdl47_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"All subtypes of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome share a common molecular theme in their pathogenesis. Which statement best characterizes the unifying defect underlying all EDS subtypes?","choices":{"A":"All EDS subtypes result from mutations in genes encoding fibronectin, laminin, or perlecan, the three principal basement membrane proteins that maintain structural integrity of skin, joints, and vascular tissue in connective tissues.","B":"All EDS subtypes result from mutations disrupting elastin fiber assembly, either through direct mutations in the elastin gene or through defects in lysyl oxidase enzymes that cross-link elastin precursors in dermal and vascular connective tissue.","C":"All EDS subtypes result from complement activation against extracellular matrix collagen components, producing autoantibody-mediated progressive destruction of collagen fibers in skin, joints, and blood vessel walls through immune complex deposition.","D":"All EDS subtypes result from defects in collagen synthesis, structure, or assembly, either through direct mutations in collagen structural genes or through mutations in enzymes and accessory proteins required for collagen post-translational processing and fibril formation.","E":"All EDS subtypes result from mutations disrupting fibrillin microfibril assembly and resulting in excessive TGF-beta bioavailability that drives matrix metalloproteinase-mediated collagen degradation in affected skin, joint, and vascular connective tissues."},"correct":"D","explanation":"The unifying molecular thread in all EDS subtypes is some form of collagen abnormality. The defects span three categories: (1) mutations in structural collagen genes (COL1A1/A2, COL3A1, COL5A1/A2, COL12A1); (2) mutations in enzymes for collagen post-translational modification (PLOD1 = lysyl hydroxylase; ADAMTS2 = procollagen N-peptidase); (3) mutations in accessory ECM proteins that regulate collagen assembly (TNXB = tenascin X). Only hypermobile EDS lacks an identified causative gene. FBN1/fibrillin mutations cause Marfan, not EDS. Elastin mutations cause cutis laxa. The 2017 classification recognizes 13 EDS subtypes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EDS common thread = collagen defect (synthesis, structure, or assembly). 13 subtypes in 2017 classification. COMLEX: Clinical features common to most EDS: hyperextensible skin, hypermobile joints, fragile skin with poor wound healing, and joint dislocation risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint hypermobility and recurrent effusions in EDS reflect the collagen structural defects that alter synovial joint architecture, placing EDS in the differential for class I non-inflammatory joint effusions.","whyNot":{"B":"Elastin mutations cause cutis ","E":"FBN1/fibrillin mutations cause"}},{"id":"sdl47_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Kyphoscoliotic EDS is caused by mutations in the PLOD1 gene and is the most common autosomal recessive EDS subtype. Through which mechanism do PLOD1 mutations impair collagen structural integrity?","choices":{"A":"PLOD1 mutations disrupt the signal peptide of procollagen alpha chains, preventing their entry into the endoplasmic reticulum and causing cytosolic procollagen accumulation that activates the UPR and cannot undergo the folding required for fibril formation.","B":"PLOD1 mutations reduce lysyl hydroxylase activity, impairing hydroxylation of lysine residues in newly synthesized collagen chains and preventing formation of the hydroxylysine-derived cross-links essential for collagen fibril tensile strength","C":"PLOD1 mutations impair prolyl-4-hydroxylase enzyme activity, preventing hydroxyproline formation in the Gly-X-Pro collagen repeating sequence and destabilizing the triple helix at normal body temperature in all affected collagen types.","D":"PLOD1 mutations eliminate the collagen-specific chaperone HSP47 binding function, preventing proper triple-helix stabilization in the ER and causing collagen misfolding that triggers UPR and ER-associated degradation of the misfolded chains.","E":"PLOD1 mutations impair procollagen N-peptidase function, preventing removal of N-terminal propeptide domains from newly synthesized procollagen and leading to dermal procollagen accumulation that cannot assemble into normal extracellular fibrils."},"correct":"B","explanation":"PLOD1 encodes lysyl hydroxylase 1 (LH1), the enzyme that hydroxylates lysine residues at the Y position of Gly-X-Y repeats in collagen. Hydroxylysine serves as the substrate for glycosylation AND as the attachment point for intermolecular aldol cross-links (allysine-hydroxylysine cross-links = pyridinoline cross-links). Without hydroxylysine, collagen fibrils lack structural cross-links, reducing tensile strength. Diagnostic clue: reduced urinary hydroxylysylpyridinoline:lysylpyridinoline ratio. PLOD1 ≠ prolyl hydroxylase (P4H). PLOD1 ≠ procollagen peptidase. Hydroxyproline (from P4H) stabilizes triple helix; hydroxylysine (from LH) enables cross-links.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PLOD1 = lysyl hydroxylase → hydroxylysine → cross-links. Kyphoscoliotic EDS = autosomal recessive, PLOD1. Features: kyphoscoliosis, ocular fragility, hypermobility. COMLEX: Distinguish lysyl hydroxylase (cross-links, PLOD1) from prolyl hydroxylase (triple helix stability; both prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase require vitamin C).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint hypermobility and recurrent effusions in EDS reflect the collagen structural defects that alter synovial joint architecture, placing EDS in the differential for class I non-inflammatory joint effusions.","whyNot":{"C":"PLOD1 ≠ prolyl hydroxylase (P4","E":"PLOD1 ≠ procollagen peptidase."}},{"id":"sdl47_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Vascular EDS differs fundamentally from other EDS subtypes in its clinical presentation and underlying molecular defect. Which statement best contrasts the features of vascular EDS with classic EDS?","choices":{"A":"Vascular EDS results from COL3A1 mutations affecting type III collagen in vessel walls and intestines, causing life-threatening spontaneous arterial rupture and bowel perforation without the skin hyperextensibility that characterizes classic EDS.","B":"Vascular EDS results from COL5A1 mutations affecting type V collagen in large vessel media, causing aortic dissection and arterial aneurysm formation while also producing the characteristic skin hyperextensibility and fragility of classic EDS subtypes.","C":"Vascular EDS results from ADAMTS2 mutations impairing procollagen-N-peptidase function in vascular smooth muscle cells, causing defective procollagen conversion that specifically weakens arterial walls without significant joint or skin involvement.","D":"Vascular EDS results from PLOD1 mutations reducing lysyl hydroxylase activity selectively in vascular collagen, impairing fibril cross-linking in arteries and bowel walls while leaving dermal and joint collagen structurally and functionally intact.","E":"Vascular EDS results from TNXB mutations eliminating tenascin X function in large vessel adventitia, disrupting the fibrillin-collagen scaffold that normally prevents arterial wall dilation and aneurysm formation in medium and large vessels."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vascular EDS (COL3A1 mutations, type III collagen) is the most life-threatening EDS subtype. Type III collagen is the predominant collagen in vessel walls and intestinal walls. COL3A1 mutations cause spontaneous rupture of medium and large arteries (especially splenic, renal, iliac) and spontaneous bowel perforation. Unlike other EDS subtypes, SKIN IS NOT HYPEREXTENSIBLE because skin hyperextensibility requires type V collagen deficiency (classic EDS, COL5A1). Vascular EDS can be identified by characteristic facies (thin nose, prominent eyes, lobeless ears, thin lips). Diagnosis confirmed by COL3A1 sequencing and dermal fibroblast type III collagen studies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vascular EDS = COL3A1, type III collagen, spontaneous arterial/bowel rupture, NO skin hyperextensibility. Classic EDS = COL5A1, type V collagen, skin hyperextensibility + joint hypermobility. COMLEX: Most dangerous EDS subtype = vascular (bowel and arterial rupture). Advise against contact sports, heavy lifting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — Joint hypermobility and recurrent effusions in EDS reflect the collagen structural defects that alter synovial joint architecture, placing EDS in the differential for class I non-inflammatory joint effusions.","whyNot":{"B":"Unlike other EDS subtypes, SKI"}},{"id":"sdl47_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A medical student reviewing the 2017 EDS classification notes that one subtype is the most commonly diagnosed form but lacks an identified causative gene. Which EDS subtype has no known causative gene, and what is its primary clinical feature?","choices":{"A":"Classical EDS is the most commonly diagnosed subtype with an unknown causative gene, presenting primarily with skin fragility, papyraceous scarring, and poor wound healing rather than joint hypermobility as the defining feature.","B":"Vascular EDS is the most common form with a currently unidentified genetic etiology, presenting primarily with spontaneous large-artery rupture and bowel perforation without significant skin or joint manifestations in most patients.","C":"Hypermobile EDS is the most commonly diagnosed subtype, with no identified causative gene; it presents with generalized joint hypermobility and musculoskeletal pain without major skin fragility or vascular complications","D":"Kyphoscoliotic EDS is the most commonly diagnosed subtype with an unknown causative gene, presenting primarily with progressive scoliosis and ocular fragility without appreciable skin hyperextensibility or joint laxity features.","E":"Periodontal EDS is the most common subtype with an unidentified causative gene, presenting primarily with early-onset severe periodontitis and premature tooth loss without significant joint or skin manifestations."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypermobile EDS (hEDS) is the most prevalent EDS subtype and the ONLY subtype without an identified causative gene as of the 2017 classification. It presents with generalized joint hypermobility, chronic musculoskeletal pain, and often dysautonomia. Skin findings are mild (velvet texture, mild hyperextensibility) but skin is NOT fragile. Vascular and visceral complications are absent. Many patients also carry a diagnosis of postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS). Because no genetic test exists, diagnosis is clinical using the 2017 hEDS criteria. All other 12 EDS subtypes have identified genetic defects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypermobile EDS = most common EDS, no known gene, joint hypermobility + chronic pain, no skin fragility. All other subtypes have known genes. COMLEX: hEDS + POTS + mast cell activation syndrome = frequently co-occurring triad in clinical practice.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 2 — Skin changes in systemic sclerosis reflect collagen overproduction, whereas EDS subtypes reflect collagen structural or assembly defects; both conditions demonstrate tissue-specific collagen pathology.","whyNot":{"A":"All other 12 EDS subtypes have","B":"All other 12 EDS subtypes have","D":"All other 12 EDS subtypes have","E":"All other 12 EDS subtypes have"}},{"id":"sdl47_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman presents with marked skin hyperextensibility, skin that tears with minor trauma, and wide papyraceous scars from prior wounds. Genetic testing reveals a heterozygous COL5A1 mutation. Which EDS subtype does this represent, and what is its inheritance pattern?","choices":{"A":"This represents classical EDS caused by COL5A1 mutations affecting type V collagen, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and characterized by skin hyperextensibility, skin fragility with poor wound healing, and joint hypermobility.","B":"This represents classical-like EDS caused by TNXB mutations affecting tenascin X, inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern and presenting identically to classical EDS with skin hyperextensibility, papyraceous scarring, and joint hypermobility.","C":"This represents dermatosparaxis EDS caused by ADAMTS2 mutations impairing procollagen-N-peptidase, inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern and producing the characteristic skin fragility and poor wound healing described in this patient.","D":"This represents arthrochalasia EDS caused by COL1A1 mutations affecting the pro-alpha-1 chain of type I collagen, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and producing the skin fragility, hyperextensibility, and wound healing defects described.","E":"This represents cardiac-valvular EDS caused by homozygous COL1A2 mutations affecting the pro-alpha-2 chain of type I collagen, inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern and presenting with progressive skin and joint manifestations."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Classical EDS is caused by heterozygous COL5A1 (or rarely COL1A1) mutations, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Type V collagen (alpha1, alpha2, alpha3 chains) is a fibrillar collagen that regulates type I collagen fibril diameter during fibrillogenesis. When type V collagen is deficient, type I collagen fibrils become abnormally large and irregular, reducing tensile strength. Classical EDS features: hyperextensible skin, velvety texture, papyraceous (cigarette paper) scars, joint hypermobility, molluscoid pseudotumors. Classical-like EDS (TNXB) is autosomal recessive with similar phenotype but no scarring. Dermatosparaxis EDS skin is extremely fragile with sagging and severe bruising.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classical EDS = COL5A1, AD, skin hyperextensibility + papyraceous scars + joint hypermobility. Classical-like = TNXB, AR, similar phenotype but no scars. COMLEX: Type V collagen regulates type I collagen fibril diameter — loss = large disorganized fibrils = weak skin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 2 — Skin changes in systemic sclerosis reflect collagen overproduction, whereas EDS subtypes reflect collagen structural or assembly defects; both conditions demonstrate tissue-specific collagen pathology.","whyNot":{"C":"Dermatosparaxis EDS skin is ex"}},{"id":"sdl47_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"The arthrochalasia and dermatosparaxis EDS subtypes both involve defective conversion of procollagen to mature collagen but differ in their molecular mechanism and inheritance pattern. Which statement correctly distinguishes these two subtypes?","choices":{"A":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from recessive ADAMTS2 mutations impairing procollagen-N-peptidase enzyme activity, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from dominant COL1A1/COL1A2 mutations producing a procollagen substrate resistant to peptidase cleavage.","B":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from recessive COL5A1 mutations producing type V procollagen that resists C-terminal peptidase cleavage, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from dominant PLOD1 mutations that produce a gain-of-function lysyl hydroxylase.","C":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from recessive PLOD1 mutations impairing lysine hydroxylation that is required for N-terminal peptidase recognition of procollagen, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from dominant COL1A2 frameshift mutations causing enzyme deficiency.","D":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from recessive TNXB mutations disrupting tenascin X-mediated procollagen assembly at the cell surface, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from dominant ADAMTS2 gain-of-function mutations producing ectopic peptidase activity.","E":"Arthrochalasia EDS results from dominant COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations producing procollagen chains that resist N-terminal peptidase cleavage, while dermatosparaxis EDS results from recessive ADAMTS2 mutations eliminating procollagen-N-peptidase enzyme activity itself."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both subtypes share the final common pathway of defective procollagen N-terminal processing, but through different mechanisms. Arthrochalasia EDS: dominant mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2 produce structurally abnormal pro-alpha chains that RESIST N-peptidase cleavage — the substrate is defective. Only 50% of chains are abnormal, but heterozygotes manifest disease because abnormal chains incorporate into triple helices. Dermatosparaxis EDS: recessive mutations in ADAMTS2 eliminate procollagen-N-peptidase ENZYME activity — the enzyme is absent. Since this is enzyme deficiency, both alleles must be non-functional (autosomal recessive). Both result in retention of N-propeptide domains and structurally deficient collagen fibrils.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arthrochalasia = dominant COL1A1/2 (resistant substrate). Dermatosparaxis = recessive ADAMTS2 (absent enzyme). Both = defective procollagen N-terminal cleavage. COMLEX: Enzyme deficiency diseases are usually recessive; structural protein diseases are often dominant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 2 — Skin changes in systemic sclerosis reflect collagen overproduction, whereas EDS subtypes reflect collagen structural or assembly defects; both conditions demonstrate tissue-specific collagen pathology."},{"id":"sdl47_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old male presents with tall stature, arm span exceeding height, arachnodactyly, high-arched palate, and pectus excavatum. Echocardiography shows aortic root dilation at 4.1 cm. Neither parent demonstrates Marfan features, and genetic testing confirms neither carries the identified FBN1 missense variant. Which statement best characterizes this patient's genetic presentation?","choices":{"A":"This patient's presentation reflects gonadal mosaicism in one parent, where a proportion of germline cells carry the FBN1 mutation below somatic cell detection threshold, producing the characteristic Marfan phenotype from partial germline transmission.","B":"This patient's de novo FBN1 mutation likely arose from mitotic recombination during early embryogenesis, producing segmental Marfan syndrome where only tissues derived from the affected cell lineage are phenotypically involved.","C":"Approximately 25% of Marfan syndrome cases arise from de novo FBN1 mutations not inherited from either parent; this patient's full phenotypic expression from a single mutant FBN1 allele reflects the autosomal dominant nature of the disease with complete penetrance.","D":"This patient's de novo presentation requires homozygous FBN1 mutations at both alleles for phenotypic expression, since de novo mutations arising during meiosis typically produce haploinsufficiency insufficient to cause disease in heterozygotes.","E":"This patient's de novo mutation originated from an epigenetic modification of the FBN1 promoter rather than a coding sequence change, producing an environmentally acquired phenotype with reduced heritability compared to germline missense mutations."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Marfan syndrome follows autosomal dominant inheritance. Approximately 75% of cases are inherited from an affected parent; ~25% arise from de novo FBN1 mutations that occurred in the germline of one unaffected parent. De novo mutations carry the same pathogenicity and follow the same autosomal dominant inheritance going forward — this patient has a 50% chance of transmitting the mutation to each child. Full penetrance means all carriers develop some degree of Marfan features, though expressivity (severity) is variable. Gonadal mosaicism is possible but would not be detectable by standard somatic tissue testing and is not the primary explanation for the ~25% de novo frequency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 75% inherited, 25% de novo in Marfan. De novo = new germline mutation, not inherited. Still AD with 50% transmission risk. COMLEX: Counsel all Marfan patients: 50% risk to each child. Recommend genetic testing of children and siblings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy and mechanism of organ damage in Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl47_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher studies a haploinsufficient FBN1 mouse model with 50% fibrillin-1 reduction. The mice develop progressive aortic root dilation similar to human Marfan syndrome. When treated with a TGF-beta neutralizing antibody, dilation is significantly attenuated. What does this experiment support about the dual pathogenic mechanism of FBN1 mutations?","choices":{"A":"This experiment supports haploinsufficiency as the exclusive mechanism, confirming that structural scaffold loss alone drives the full cardiovascular phenotype and that TGF-beta antibody acts through an off-target effect on aortic smooth muscle contractility.","B":"This experiment supports the arthritogenic peptide hypothesis by showing that fibrillin-1 peptide fragments bind HLA molecules on aortic endothelium, and that TGF-beta antibody blocks TCR engagement with fibrillin-1 neoantigens at the aortic root.","C":"This experiment supports a TGF-beta-exclusive mechanism in fibrillin haploinsufficiency, implying that the structural scaffold loss is irrelevant to cardiovascular disease and that all Marfan manifestations are driven by TGF-beta signaling alone.","D":"This experiment supports excess TGF-beta bioavailability from reduced fibrillin-1 sequestration as a primary disease driver, with TGF-beta-mediated smooth muscle apoptosis and matrix remodeling adding to the direct structural scaffold loss in the aorta","E":"This experiment supports neomorphic gain-of-function as the mechanism in fibrillin haploinsufficiency, where the single remaining FBN1 allele upregulates TGF-beta synthesis through a compensatory feedback mechanism that is blocked by the antibody."},"correct":"D","explanation":"This classic experiment (Habashi et al., Science 2006) demonstrated that TGF-beta neutralization with a pan-TGF-beta antibody or with losartan (an AT1R antagonist that reduces TGF-beta signaling) attenuated aortic root dilation in the Marfan mouse model. This established that excess TGF-beta — not just structural scaffold loss — is a primary driver of Marfan aortic pathology. This has clinical translation: losartan is used adjunctively to beta-blockers in Marfan syndrome management to reduce aortic root dilation progression. The experiment does not refute the scaffold hypothesis but demonstrates that TGF-beta is independently pathogenic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Losartan (ARB) reduces TGF-beta signaling and slows aortic dilation in Marfan — addresses the TGF-beta arm of the dual mechanism. Beta-blockers address hemodynamic stress. COMLEX: Both beta-blockers and ARBs are used to slow aortic dilation in Marfan; surgery (aortic root replacement) once the root is significantly dilated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy and mechanism of organ damage in Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma.","whyNot":{"A":"The experiment does not refute","C":"The experiment does not refute"}},{"id":"sdl47_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with Marfan syndrome caused by a de novo FBN1 missense mutation asks about the risk of transmitting the condition to her children. Her partner does not carry FBN1 mutations. Which statement most accurately describes the inheritance risk for her offspring?","choices":{"A":"Because FBN1 mutations cause autosomal dominant Marfan syndrome with complete penetrance, this patient has a 50% probability of transmitting the mutation to each child, regardless of her partner's genotype, and all carriers can be expected to develop Marfan features.","B":"Because this patient's FBN1 mutation arose de novo, it carries a significantly lower recurrence risk in her offspring compared to inherited mutations, as de novo origins are associated with reduced germline transmission efficiency in subsequent generations.","C":"Because FBN1 missense mutations act through dominant negative mechanisms, the risk to offspring is 75% rather than 50%, since one mutant and one normal allele together produce sufficient abnormal fibrillin-1 polymer disruption in nearly all children.","D":"Because FBN1 mutations show variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance, the transmission risk to each child is 50% but only 30-40% of children who inherit the mutation will develop clinically significant Marfan syndrome requiring medical management.","E":"Because this mutation arose de novo in somatic cells during embryogenesis producing tissue mosaicism, the actual germline transmission risk is lower than 50% and depends on the proportion of germline cells carrying the mutation."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autosomal dominant inheritance with complete penetrance: once a person carries a disease-causing FBN1 allele, regardless of whether it arose de novo or was inherited, the transmission risk to each child is 50% (Mendelian segregation). De novo origin does NOT reduce transmission risk in subsequent generations — the mutation is fully heritable once established in the germline. Complete penetrance means all carriers manifest some features (though expressivity varies). Variable expressivity is present in Marfan but incomplete penetrance is not — all FBN1 mutation carriers develop some Marfan features. The dominant negative mechanism does not change the 50% transmission probability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AD inheritance = 50% risk per child regardless of de novo or inherited origin. De novo in generation 1 = fully heritable in generation 2. COMLEX: Marfan genetic counseling: 50% child risk, recommend prenatal/preimplantation genetic testing if desired.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy and mechanism of organ damage in Raynaud phenomenon and scleroderma.","whyNot":{"C":"The dominant negative mechanis"}},{"id":"sdl47_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with Marfan syndrome has a new early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border and a wide pulse pressure. Echocardiography shows severe aortic regurgitation with a markedly dilated aortic root, no intimal flap, and aortic cusps of normal thickness. Which mechanism best explains his regurgitation?","choices":{"A":"Myxoid floppy change of the aortic cusps, the most frequent cardiac lesion of Marfan syndrome, causing the cusps to prolapse","B":"Infective endocarditis on a bicuspid aortic valve, a congenital defect that is inherited together with the FBN1 mutation","C":"Medial degeneration of the ascending aorta lets the root and valve ring dilate progressively, causing aortic incompetence","D":"Fibrous fusion of the aortic cusps after rheumatic carditis, an infection to which fibrillin-deficient valves are prone","E":"A retrograde aortic dissection tearing the attachments of the cusps, the usual first cardiovascular event in Marfan syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cardiovascular lesions are the most life-threatening features of Marfan syndrome. Medial degeneration (cystic medial necrosis) of the ascending aorta removes medial support, so the aortic valve ring and aortic root dilate progressively, giving severe aortic incompetence. The same weakening predisposes to intimal tears and aortic dissection, but no flap is seen here. Mitral valve prolapse is the more frequent lesion (40% to 50% of cases) but is clinically less important than the aortic lesions. The floppy, myxoid change of Marfan syndrome mainly affects the mitral valve, also the tricuspid valve, and only rarely the aortic valve. Bicuspid valves and rheumatic cusp fusion are not among the Marfan lesions, and dissection is a complication of the dilated, weakened aorta rather than its usual first event. Most deaths are caused by rupture of aortic dissections, followed by cardiac failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan cardiovascular disease: MVP (40% to 50%, most frequent) and aortic root dilation from cystic medial necrosis (most important), leading to aortic regurgitation and dissection; death is most often from aortic dissection or rupture. COMLEX: Aortic regurgitation gives an early diastolic decrescendo murmur with a wide pulse pressure; follow the aortic root with serial echocardiography.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 1 — Pathogenesis of Marfan syndrome","whyNot":{"A":"The floppy, myxoid change of M","B":"Bicuspid valves and rheumatic "}},{"id":"sdl47_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two unrelated patients each carry a pathogenic FBN1 mutation. The first has bilateral ectopia lentis and aortic root dilation but an ordinary body build. The second is very tall with arachnodactyly, pectus excavatum and scoliosis but has normal eyes and a normal aorta. Which statement best explains the difference between them?","choices":{"A":"The second patient has homocystinuria rather than Marfan syndrome, because lens dislocation is required for a Marfan diagnosis","B":"The second patient must carry an FBN2 mutation as well, because FBN1 mutations always affect the eye before the skeleton","C":"Skeletal and ocular features arise from two separate genes, so each patient has inherited a different one of two linked loci","D":"Phenotype depends on the parent of origin, so FBN1 from the father causes skeletal disease and FBN1 from the mother eye disease","E":"Expression varies widely between families, partly because about 1000 different FBN1 mutations alter fibrillin differently"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Marfan syndrome shows great variation in clinical expression. Patients with prominent eye or cardiovascular changes may have few skeletal abnormalities, whereas others with striking changes in body habitus have no eye changes. Variability within a family occurs, but variability between families is much more common and extensive, partly because close to 1000 different FBN1 mutations have been found. Because of this variability, diagnosis uses the revised Ghent criteria, which combine family history, cardinal clinical signs and FBN1 testing; in general, major involvement of two of the four organ systems (skeletal, cardiovascular, ocular and skin) and minor involvement of another are required. Lens dislocation is therefore not required. Homocystinuria is autosomal recessive and has downward and nasal lens dislocation. FBN2 mutations cause congenital contractural arachnodactyly, a separate disorder. The variability is attributed to the many different FBN1 mutations, not to two linked loci or to which parent transmits the gene.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Marfan syndrome = autosomal dominant FBN1 mutation (chromosome 15) with variable expression due to pleiotropy; about 75% familial, 25% de novo. COMLEX: Revised Ghent criteria: major involvement of two of four systems (skeletal, cardiovascular, ocular, skin) plus minor involvement of another, with family history and FBN1 testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 2 — Multi-organ phenotypes of Marfan syndrome","whyNot":{"A":"Lens dislocation is therefore ","D":"The variability is attributed "}},{"id":"sdl47_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the potential multi-organ phenotypes seen in Marfan's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with Marfan syndrome has been on atenolol for 10 years. Her 8-year-old daughter, who has some Marfan features, is referred for cardiac evaluation. Echocardiography of the daughter reveals mitral valve prolapse with mild regurgitation and mild aortic root dilation. Which cardiac manifestation is most prevalent in pediatric Marfan syndrome, often preceding significant aortic root dilation?","choices":{"A":"Aortic regurgitation from progressive aortic annular dilation is the most common initial cardiac finding in pediatric Marfan syndrome, preceding the aortic root dilation that develops predominantly in late adolescence and early adulthood.","B":"Mitral valve prolapse is the most common cardiac manifestation in pediatric Marfan syndrome, present in 40-50% of patients, resulting from fibrillin-1-deficient myxomatous changes in the mitral valve leaflets and chordae tendineae.","C":"Pulmonary artery dilation is the most common initial cardiac finding in pediatric Marfan syndrome, reflecting fibrillin-1 deficiency in the pulmonary arterial media that appears radiographically before systemic arterial involvement.","D":"Tricuspid valve regurgitation from papillary muscle elongation is the most common early cardiac finding in pediatric Marfan syndrome, reflecting the disproportionate chordal growth in tall patients before annular dilation becomes evident.","E":"Ventricular septal defect from fibrillin-1 deficiency at the interventricular junction is the most common cardiac manifestation in pediatric Marfan syndrome, arising during cardiogenesis and identified on newborn echo screening."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) is the most common cardiac finding in Marfan syndrome, present in approximately 40-50% of patients. Fibrillin-1 deficiency leads to myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve leaflets and chordae tendineae (accumulation of proteoglycans, collagen disorganization), causing leaflet billowing and prolapse into the left atrium during systole. MVP can cause mitral regurgitation and, rarely, is associated with sudden cardiac death from arrhythmia. Aortic root dilation (at sinuses of Valsalva) also develops but may be less severe and less commonly symptomatic in younger pediatric patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MVP in Marfan = 40-50% prevalence, myxomatous valve degeneration. Aortic root dilation = more serious long-term. Both require echo monitoring. COMLEX: Annual echo in Marfan; beta-blockers initiated when aortic root begins to dilate or MVP causes symptomatic regurgitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 2 — Primary vs secondary Raynaud and underlying diseases associated with secondary causes."},{"id":"sdl47_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman with classical Ehlers-Danlos syndrome needs repair of a forehead laceration after a minor fall. The surgeon notes that the wound gaped widely after a small impact and that sutures tear through the wound edges easily. She can bend her thumb back to touch her forearm. Which property of her connective tissue best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Her elastic fibers are overabundant, so the skin recoils strongly away from the wound edges and pulls the sutures out","B":"Her fibrillin-1 microfibrils release excess TGF-beta, which activates proteases that digest the dermis around sutures","C":"Her collagen fibers lack normal tensile strength, so collagen-rich skin, ligaments and joints stretch and tear","D":"Her platelets cannot adhere to subendothelial collagen, so wound hematomas push the edges apart and loosen sutures","E":"Her fibroblasts cannot divide after injury, so the wound edges stay thin even though her dermal collagen is normal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tissues rich in collagen, such as skin, ligaments and joints, are involved in most variants of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. Because the abnormal collagen fibers lack adequate tensile strength, the skin is hyperextensible and the joints are hypermobile; bending the thumb back to touch the forearm is a classic example. The skin is extraordinarily stretchable, extremely fragile and easily bruised. Minor injuries produce gaping defects, and surgical repair is accomplished with great difficulty because the tissue lacks normal tensile strength. The defect lies in collagen, not elastin. Release of TGF-beta from abnormal fibrillin-1 microfibrils is the mechanism of Marfan syndrome. The sources attribute the easy bruising of EDS to fragile tissue, not to a platelet defect. The problem is the quality of the collagen fibers, not a failure of fibroblast division.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EDS = faulty collagen synthesis: hyperextensible, fragile skin; hypermobile joints with dislocations; easy bruising; may be associated with berry and aortic aneurysms and organ rupture. COMLEX: Expect gaping wounds and poor suture holding; close wounds and plan surgery with care.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 4 — Types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome classified by phenotype","whyNot":{"A":"The defect lies in collagen, n","D":"The sources attribute the easy","E":"The problem is the quality of "}},{"id":"sdl47_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In one family with vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, every affected member is heterozygous for the same COL3A1 missense mutation, yet each has severe arterial fragility. In a second family, a different COL3A1 mutation reduces secretion of type III procollagen. Which statement best describes the genetics of vascular EDS?","choices":{"A":"Vascular EDS is caused by COL5A1 mutations, and skin hyperextensibility is its usual first sign, well before any arterial rupture","B":"Vascular EDS is autosomal recessive, so heterozygous relatives are healthy carriers and severe disease requires two mutant alleles","C":"Vascular EDS results from loss of lysyl hydroxylase, so all of its mutations act by blocking hydroxylysine cross-links in collagen","D":"Vascular EDS is caused by one recurrent COL3A1 mutation, so every affected family shares the same molecular disease mechanism","E":"COL3A1 mutations can slow chain synthesis, block secretion or make abnormal chains, and dominant-negative alleles are severe"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vascular EDS typically results from abnormalities of type III collagen and is genetically heterogeneous: at least three distinct types of COL3A1 mutation can cause it. Some affect the rate of synthesis of pro-alpha-1(III) chains, some affect secretion of type III procollagen, and others produce structurally abnormal type III collagen. Some mutant alleles behave as dominant negatives and therefore produce severe phenotypes in heterozygotes. Because blood vessels and intestines are rich in type III collagen, these mutations make the organs vulnerable to spontaneous rupture; unlike other subtypes, the skin is not usually hyperextensible. Inheritance is autosomal dominant. COL5A1 mutations cause classical EDS. Lysyl hydroxylase deficiency (PLOD1) causes kyphoscoliotic EDS, an autosomal recessive form.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vascular EDS = COL3A1 (type III procollagen), autosomal dominant; fragile arteries, intestine and gravid uterus prone to rupture; skin usually not hyperextensible. COMLEX: Dominant-negative mutant chains explain why one abnormal allele can cause severe connective tissue disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 4 — Types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome classified by phenotype"},{"id":"sdl47_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the underlying defect contributing to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-month-old boy has growth and developmental delay, hypotonia, and sparse, brittle, kinky hair. A maternal uncle died in infancy with similar findings. His physician notes that collagen cross-linking is defective, as in some forms of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, but at a different step. Which defect is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Loss of lysyl hydroxylase from PLOD1 mutations, which prevents hydroxylation of lysine residues in the endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Loss of procollagen N-peptidase from ADAMTS2 mutations, which leaves the N-terminal propeptides attached to type I procollagen","C":"Mutation of ATP7B, which traps copper in the liver and leaves too little copper for the collagen-modifying enzymes of the skin","D":"Mutation of ATP7A, which impairs copper absorption and transport and lowers the activity of the copper-dependent lysyl oxidase","E":"Lack of vitamin C, which is needed to hydroxylate proline and lysine residues before three collagen chains form a triple helix"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is Menkes disease, an X-linked recessive connective tissue disease, which fits an affected boy and a maternal uncle. Defective Menkes protein (ATP7A) impairs copper absorption and transport. Copper is a necessary cofactor of lysyl oxidase, so its activity falls and collagen cross-linking is defective; patients have brittle, kinky hair, growth and developmental delay, hypotonia and an increased risk of cerebral aneurysms. In collagen synthesis, hydroxylation of proline and lysine occurs in the RER and requires vitamin C; cross-links between collagen molecules form outside the cell and are stabilized by lysyl oxidase. Lysyl hydroxylase deficiency (PLOD1) is kyphoscoliotic EDS, an autosomal recessive defect of the earlier hydroxylation step. ADAMTS2 deficiency is dermatosparaxis EDS, a defect of propeptide cleavage. ATP7B is the Wilson disease protein, which causes copper buildup. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, not an X-linked disease of infants.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collagen synthesis: hydroxylation of proline and lysine in the RER (vitamin C; lysyl hydroxylase is the PLOD1 enzyme) comes before secretion; propeptide cleavage and lysyl oxidase cross-linking (copper) occur outside the cell. Menkes = ATP7A, X-linked recessive, low lysyl oxidase activity. COMLEX: Kinky hair, hypotonia and developmental delay in an infant boy point to Menkes disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 3 — The underlying collagen defect in Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","whyNot":{"A":"Lysyl hydroxylase deficiency (","B":"ADAMTS2 deficiency is dermatos","C":"ATP7B is the Wilson disease pr"}},{"id":"sdl47_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A genetics resident reviews a pedigree showing three generations of autosomal dominant EDS. The proband has severe skin hyperextensibility and papyraceous scarring with a heterozygous COL5A1 mutation. A distant cousin has clinically similar features but a homozygous TNXB mutation. Which statement correctly explains why these genetically distinct mutations produce similar clinical presentations?","choices":{"A":"The COL5A1 and TNXB mutations produce identical clinical features because both genes encode identical molecular functions in type V collagen biosynthesis, with TNXB serving as the regulatory chaperone of the COL5A1 transcriptional activation complex.","B":"COL5A1 encodes structural type V collagen while TNXB encodes tenascin X, a matrix glycoprotein that interacts with collagens I, III, and V to regulate fibril assembly; both defects disrupt dermal collagen architecture by different routes","C":"The clinical similarity reflects phenotypic convergence in which any collagen-related gene mutation preferentially disrupts the COL5A1-rich papillary dermis regardless of the gene primarily affected, producing the same superficial skin phenotype across all genotypes.","D":"Both COL5A1 and TNXB encode enzymes required for the same N-hydroxylation step in type V collagen post-translational modification, producing equivalent collagen structural defects through convergent enzymatic dysfunction.","E":"Both mutations cause classical EDS through identical haploinsufficiency mechanisms, and the differing inheritance patterns reflect differences in gene copy number sensitivity between individuals rather than truly distinct molecular pathomechanisms."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Classic EDS (COL5A1 mutation) and classical-like EDS (TNXB mutation) produce similar clinical phenotypes through different but converging molecular mechanisms. Tenascin X (encoded by TNXB) is a large extracellular matrix glycoprotein that physically interacts with type I, III, and V collagens and regulates their lateral fibril assembly and fibril diameter. When tenascin X is absent (biallelic TNXB mutations — autosomal recessive), fibrillar collagen (especially type V-containing fibrils) fails to assemble normally, producing a phenotype similar to primary type V collagen deficiency. This illustrates how ECM accessory proteins that regulate collagen assembly can cause EDS subtypes even without direct collagen structural gene mutations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classical EDS = COL5A1 (AD). Classical-like EDS = TNXB (AR). Similar phenotype, different inheritance. Tenascin X regulates collagen fibril assembly. COMLEX: EDS subtype identification requires genetic testing to distinguish classical from classical-like and guide inheritance counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 2 — Typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes that cause them."},{"id":"sdl47_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 19-year-old man with joint hypermobility and kyphoscoliosis that has progressed since childhood has a corneal rupture after a minor blow to the eye and, a year later, a retinal detachment. His parents are unaffected first cousins. Which Ehlers-Danlos syndrome subtype best fits his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Kyphoscoliotic EDS","B":"Hypermobile EDS","C":"Vascular EDS","D":"Dermatosparaxis EDS","E":"Classical EDS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The defective connective tissue of EDS can cause serious internal complications that differ by subtype. Ocular fragility with rupture of the cornea and retinal detachment is the complication of the kyphoscoliosis type. The kyphoscoliotic type is the most common autosomal recessive form of EDS, which fits unaffected parents who are first cousins. It results from mutations in PLOD1, which encodes lysyl hydroxylase; without hydroxylysine, collagen cross-links and structural stability are deficient. Hypermobile EDS is the one subtype without a known gene, and its problems center on joint instability. Vascular EDS is autosomal dominant and causes rupture of the colon and large arteries. Dermatosparaxis EDS is an autosomal recessive defect of procollagen N-peptidase, without the ocular rupture described here. Classical EDS is an autosomal dominant type V collagen disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EDS internal complications by subtype: vascular = rupture of colon and large arteries; kyphoscoliotic = corneal rupture and retinal detachment; classic = diaphragmatic hernia. COMLEX: Kyphoscoliotic EDS is the most common autosomal recessive EDS (PLOD1, lysyl hydroxylase).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 3 — The underlying collagen defect in Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","whyNot":{"B":"Hypermobile EDS is the one sub"}},{"id":"sdl47_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the variety of types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome as classified by phenotype.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Beyond the skin and joint findings, the defective connective tissue of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome can cause serious internal complications that differ by subtype. Which pairing of EDS subtype with an internal complication is correct?","choices":{"A":"Hypermobile type with colonic rupture","B":"Vascular type with corneal rupture","C":"Classic type with diaphragmatic hernia","D":"Kyphoscoliotic type with arterial rupture","E":"Vascular type with diaphragmatic hernia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins lists the internal complications that the defective connective tissue of EDS can produce: rupture of the colon and large arteries in the vascular type, ocular fragility with rupture of the cornea and retinal detachment in the kyphoscoliosis type, and diaphragmatic hernia in the classic type. Classic EDS is usually caused by mutations in the type V collagen genes COL5A1 and COL5A2. Colonic and arterial rupture belong to the vascular type, which results from abnormal type III collagen; vessels and intestines are rich in this collagen. Corneal rupture belongs to the kyphoscoliotic type, not the vascular type, and diaphragmatic hernia belongs to the classic type. The hypermobile type is the most common subtype, has no identified gene, and is defined by joint instability rather than organ rupture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EDS subtype clues: classical = skin and joints, COL5A1/COL5A2, diaphragmatic hernia; vascular = COL3A1, arterial, colonic and uterine rupture; kyphoscoliotic = PLOD1, corneal rupture, retinal detachment; hypermobile = most common, no known gene. COMLEX: EDS may also be associated with berry and aortic aneurysms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 3 — The underlying collagen defect in Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","whyNot":{"A":"The hypermobile type is the mo","B":"Corneal rupture belongs to the","E":"Corneal rupture belongs to the"}}],"53":[{"id":"sdl53_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Alpha-2C adrenoceptors on vascular smooth muscle cells play a central role in cold-induced vasoconstriction in Raynaud phenomenon. Which statement best describes the mechanism by which cold selectively activates these receptors?","choices":{"A":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are expressed constitutively at high density on the plasma membrane of digital arterioles; cold exposure increases their affinity for norepinephrine through a temperature-dependent conformational change in the receptor ligand-binding domain.","B":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are stored silently within the Golgi apparatus; cold exposure activates the Rho/Rho kinase signaling pathway, triggering receptor translocation to the plasma membrane and augmenting vasoconstriction in response to norepinephrine.","C":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are degraded by a ubiquitin-proteasome mechanism; cold exposure stabilizes these receptors by inhibiting their ubiquitination, producing a net increase in surface receptor expression on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells.","D":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are phosphorylated and internalized via beta-arrestin; cold exposure dephosphorylates these receptors and promotes their recycling to the plasma membrane through a protein kinase A-dependent mechanism.","E":"At normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are sequestered by circulating catecholamines at baseline; cold exposure displaces bound norepinephrine from alpha-2C receptors through allosteric competition, creating a paradoxical increase in free receptor availability."},"correct":"B","explanation":"In cutaneous arterioles at normal temperature, alpha-2C adrenoceptors are stored silently in the Golgi apparatus and do not contribute to baseline vascular tone. Cold exposure triggers a rapid increase in reactive oxygen species in smooth muscle cells, activating the Rho/Rho kinase signaling pathway. This causes translocation of alpha-2C adrenoceptors from the Golgi to the plasma membrane, where they augment the contractile response to norepinephrine. This mechanism explains why alpha-2C receptors mediate cold-induced but not baseline vasoconstriction. Importantly, in primary Raynaud phenomenon, this pathway is exaggerated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alpha-2C adrenoceptors = cold-specific vasoconstriction via Golgi-to-membrane translocation through Rho/Rho kinase. Alpha-2A/B = baseline adrenergic tone. COMLEX: Rho kinase inhibitors are an emerging therapeutic target in Raynaud phenomenon and pulmonary arterial hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis reflects endothelial dysfunction with reduced nitric oxide and prostacyclin and elevated endothelin-1, directly linking RP pathogenesis to scleroderma vasculopathy."},{"id":"sdl53_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Arteriovenous anastomoses are the primary thermoregulatory vessels in glabrous skin. Which statement best describes their role in the pathogenesis of Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Arteriovenous anastomoses connect paired digital arteries and provide collateral flow between adjacent fingers; their constriction during cold exposure is a protective mechanism that preserves nutritional capillary flow and prevents the cyanosis characteristic of Raynaud phenomenon.","B":"Arteriovenous anastomoses are low-resistance conduits that connect cutaneous lymphatics to the deep venous plexus; their constriction during cold increases lymphatic pressure in the fingers, producing the edema and discoloration characteristic of severe Raynaud attacks.","C":"Arteriovenous anastomoses connect the superficial to the deep dermal plexus and are richly innervated by the sympathetic adrenergic system; their exaggerated constriction during cold exposure shunts blood away from digital arteries and cutaneous capillaries, producing the ischemic pallor of Raynaud phenomenon.","D":"Arteriovenous anastomoses function exclusively as baroreceptors that sense changes in digital perfusion pressure; their impaired mechanosensory function in primary Raynaud phenomenon reduces the normal vasodilatory reflex triggered by digital ischemia.","E":"Arteriovenous anastomoses are found predominantly in hairy non-glabrous skin of the forearm and trunk; their selective dysfunction in Raynaud phenomenon explains why episodes preferentially involve the non-glabrous skin of the dorsal hand rather than the palmar surface."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arteriovenous anastomoses are low-resistance shunt vessels concentrated in glabrous skin of fingers, toes, palms, soles, ears, and nose. They connect the superficial arteriolar plexus to the deep venous plexus, bypassing nutritional capillaries. They are richly innervated by the sympathetic adrenergic system and serve as the primary thermoregulatory vessels. During cold exposure, sympathetic activation constricts AVAs, shunting blood to the deep plexus for heat conservation. In Raynaud phenomenon, this response is exaggerated, reducing total digital blood flow. In primary RP, nutritional capillary flow is largely preserved despite AVA constriction; in secondary RP, nutritional flow is also impaired.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AVAs = thermoregulatory shunt vessels in glabrous digital skin, richly sympathetically innervated. AVA constriction = reduced total digital flow but nutritional capillaries preserved in primary RP. COMLEX: AVA dysfunction is central to RP pathogenesis; found in fingers, toes, ears, nose, lips.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis reflects endothelial dysfunction with reduced nitric oxide and prostacyclin and elevated endothelin-1, directly linking RP pathogenesis to scleroderma vasculopathy."},{"id":"sdl53_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Endothelin-1 is a potent vasoconstrictor produced by endothelial cells. Which statement best characterizes the role of endothelin-1 in Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Endothelin-1 is constitutively released by endothelial cells at high levels in all forms of Raynaud phenomenon; it acts on ETA receptors on smooth muscle cells to produce the sustained digital vasospasm and is the primary mediator of both primary and secondary forms.","B":"Endothelin-1 acts as a vasodilator in the digital microcirculation by activating ETB receptors on endothelial cells to release nitric oxide; its deficiency in secondary Raynaud phenomenon removes this protective vasodilatory signal and promotes vasospasm.","C":"Endothelin-1 is the primary mediator of cold-induced vasoconstriction in primary Raynaud phenomenon; its release from digital endothelial cells is directly triggered by cold temperature via TRPM8 receptor activation on endothelial cell cilia.","D":"Endothelin-1 is not released under normal vascular conditions but is produced when endothelial dysfunction occurs; elevated endothelin-1 contributes to the exaggerated vasoconstriction of secondary Raynaud phenomenon rather than primary disease","E":"Endothelin-1 competitively inhibits norepinephrine binding at alpha-2C adrenoceptors in normal conditions; its absence in Raynaud phenomenon removes this inhibitory mechanism and allows unrestrained norepinephrine-mediated vasoconstriction of digital arteries."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Endothelin-1 is a potent vasoconstrictor but is not released under normal conditions in primary Raynaud phenomenon, where endothelial function is generally preserved. It is elevated in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, especially systemic sclerosis, where endothelial injury and dysfunction are early and prominent features. In SSc, reduced nitric oxide and prostacyclin production combined with elevated endothelin-1 creates a pro-vasoconstrictive endothelial state. Endothelin-1 does act on both ETA receptors on smooth muscle cells to cause vasoconstriction AND ETB receptors on endothelial cells to release NO (vasodilation), making it a complex mediator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary RP = preserved endothelial function, normal endothelin-1. Secondary RP (especially SSc) = endothelial dysfunction, elevated endothelin-1, reduced NO/prostacyclin. COMLEX: Bosentan (endothelin receptor antagonist) is used for SSc-related digital ulcers and pulmonary arterial hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 3 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis reflects endothelial dysfunction with reduced nitric oxide and prostacyclin and elevated endothelin-1, directly linking RP pathogenesis to scleroderma vasculopathy.","whyNot":{"B":"Endothelin-1 does act on both "}},{"id":"sdl53_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best describes the features of primary Raynaud phenomenon that distinguish it from secondary Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects predominantly older males, presents with asymmetric extremity involvement that progressively worsens over time, and results from structural vascular changes including intimal fibrosis and luminal obliteration of digital arteries.","B":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects individuals over age 50 with equal sex distribution, presents with unilateral hand involvement that correlates with occupational cold exposure, and is characterized by endothelial dysfunction with elevated endothelin-1 and reduced nitric oxide.","C":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects 3-5% of the general population, predominantly young females, presents with symmetric extremity attacks that do not progress in severity, and reflects intrinsic smooth muscle cell hyperreactivity without structural vascular change","D":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects predominantly young males with a strong family history of connective tissue disease, presents with episodic toe involvement more commonly than finger involvement, and is distinguished by the presence of anti-centromere antibodies on serologic testing.","E":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon affects patients of any age with a prior history of digital ischemia, presents with bilateral asymmetric involvement that improves over time, and is characterized by structural intimal thickening of proximal palmar digital arteries at presentation."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon characteristics: 3-5% prevalence in the general population; predominantly young females; onset typically between age 15-30; symmetric bilateral involvement of fingers and sometimes toes; course is benign with stable or improving severity over time; no underlying disease; intrinsic smooth muscle hyperreactivity to cold/emotion; no structural arterial wall changes except in long-standing disease where mild intimal thickening can develop. Critical ischemia with ulceration or gangrene is rare. Autoantibody testing and nail fold capillaroscopy are normal in primary RP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary RP = young female, symmetric, benign course, no structural changes, no underlying disease. Secondary RP = asymmetric, progressive, underlying disease, structural changes, nutritional capillary involvement. COMLEX: Nailfold capillaroscopy: normal loops in primary RP; dilated/tortuous/avascular areas in secondary RP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 1 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon is the sentinel vascular manifestation of systemic sclerosis, preceding skin fibrosis and internal organ involvement in the majority of SSc patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which connective tissue disease is most commonly associated with secondary Raynaud phenomenon and how often does Raynaud phenomenon occur in it, often as the earliest clinical manifestation?","choices":{"A":"Systemic sclerosis is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in virtually all affected patients and frequently preceding skin fibrosis and internal organ involvement by months to years.","B":"Systemic lupus erythematosus is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in virtually all affected patients and representing the most common initial clinical manifestation that triggers autoantibody evaluation.","C":"Mixed connective tissue disease is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in virtually all affected patients because anti-U1 RNP antibodies directly activate alpha-2C adrenoceptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells.","D":"Rheumatoid arthritis is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in virtually all affected patients as a consequence of synovial pannus encasing and compressing digital artery walls during active joint inflammation.","E":"Sjogren syndrome is the connective tissue disease most strongly associated with Raynaud phenomenon, occurring in virtually all affected patients because aquaporin-3 deficiency in glandular and vascular tissue simultaneously impairs salivary secretion and digital vasodilation."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Systemic sclerosis has the strongest association with Raynaud phenomenon of all connective tissue diseases, with RP seen in virtually all SSc patients (Robbins Ch 6). Raynaud phenomenon is often the very first symptom of SSc, preceding skin thickening by months to years. The mechanism in SSc involves endothelial dysfunction (reduced NO and prostacyclin, elevated endothelin-1), intimal fibrosis, alpha-2C adrenoceptor upregulation, and structural microangiopathy. SLE, MCTD and Sjogren syndrome can also cause RP, but less consistently. Rheumatoid arthritis can cause RP but is not the most common association.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc + Raynaud phenomenon = virtually all patients (Robbins Ch 6); often first symptom years before skin disease. COMLEX: Any new Raynaud phenomenon in patient over 30 or with asymmetric/progressive features warrants evaluation for underlying connective tissue disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 1 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon is the sentinel vascular manifestation of systemic sclerosis, preceding skin fibrosis and internal organ involvement in the majority of SSc patients.","whyNot":{"B":"SLE, MCTD and Sjogren syndrome","D":"Rheumatoid arthritis can cause"}},{"id":"sdl53_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which distinction in the effect on digital blood flow best differentiates primary from secondary Raynaud phenomenon and explains why critical digital ischemia and gangrene occur in secondary but not primary forms?","choices":{"A":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction affects only the distal interphalangeal joint capillaries; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, the proximal digital arteries are also involved, producing retrograde ischemia that propagates from the palm to the fingertips.","B":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction is limited to the alpha-2A adrenoceptors on large digital arteries; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, alpha-2C adrenoceptors on the nutritional capillaries are additionally activated, producing complete microvascular arrest.","C":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction is fully reversed within 30 seconds of rewarming due to intact endothelial nitric oxide production; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, structural vascular changes prevent nitric oxide release and trap the digits in permanent vasospasm.","D":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction reduces arteriovenous anastomosis flow while nutritional capillary flow to the tissue is largely preserved; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon both flows are reduced, producing critical ischemia that permits tissue necrosis","E":"In primary Raynaud phenomenon, cold-induced constriction affects all digital vessels symmetrically but is limited in magnitude by normal Rho kinase activity; in secondary Raynaud phenomenon, activated Rho kinase produces an amplitude of constriction 10-fold greater than in primary disease."},"correct":"D","explanation":"The key pathophysiologic distinction: In primary RP, exaggerated AVA constriction reduces total digital blood flow but nutritional capillary flow is maintained at most temperatures. Without nutritional capillary involvement, tissue oxygenation is preserved and critical ischemic events (digital ulcers, pitting scars, gangrene) are rare. In secondary RP, the underlying vascular disease (structural microangiopathy, endothelial dysfunction, intimal fibrosis in SSc) also compromises nutritional capillary flow, leaving digits vulnerable to critical ischemia. This is why digital pitting, ulceration, and gangrene are features of secondary RP but essentially absent in uncomplicated primary RP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary RP = preserved nutritional capillary flow = no gangrene/ulcers. Secondary RP = nutritional capillary also compromised = digital ulcers, gangrene, pitting scars possible. COMLEX: Digital ulcers in Raynaud = secondary RP until proven otherwise; evaluate for SSc with nailfold capillaroscopy and ANA/anti-Scl-70/anticentromere.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 1 — Secondary Raynaud phenomenon is the sentinel vascular manifestation of systemic sclerosis, preceding skin fibrosis and internal organ involvement in the majority of SSc patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Cold exposure is the most common trigger of Raynaud phenomenon. Through which specific molecular mechanism does cold temperature initiate the exaggerated vasospastic response in affected patients?","choices":{"A":"Cold exposure directly depolarizes voltage-gated calcium channels on smooth muscle cell membranes, producing a sustained influx of extracellular calcium that triggers myosin light chain kinase phosphorylation and tonic contraction independent of adrenergic receptor activation.","B":"Cold exposure generates reactive oxygen species in digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells, activating the Rho/Rho kinase signaling pathway that triggers alpha-2C adrenoceptor translocation from the Golgi to the plasma membrane and augments calcium sensitivity of contractile proteins.","C":"Cold exposure inhibits endothelial nitric oxide synthase by reducing the availability of its cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin, producing endothelial uncoupling that generates superoxide instead of nitric oxide and precipitates vasospasm through oxidative stress.","D":"Cold exposure activates TRPM8 cold receptors on digital artery endothelial cells, triggering direct calcium influx into the endothelium that releases thromboxane A2 and serotonin from endothelial granules, which then activate smooth muscle vasoconstriction.","E":"Cold exposure directly activates the NLRP3 inflammasome in perivascular macrophages, releasing IL-1beta that acts on smooth muscle cells to upregulate endothelin-1 receptor expression and amplify endothelin-driven digital vasospasm in susceptible individuals."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cold triggers RP through the Rho/Rho kinase pathway: cold exposure causes a rapid increase in ROS in cutaneous smooth muscle cells, activating Rho/Rho kinase, which then (1) triggers alpha-2C adrenoceptor translocation from Golgi to plasma membrane and (2) augments calcium sensitivity of the contractile proteins (via myosin light chain phosphatase inhibition). This is distinct from direct calcium channel activation or endothelial mechanisms. TRPM8 is expressed on afferent sensory neurons to detect cold (not on endothelial cells). The ROS-Rho-alpha-2C pathway is a self-reinforcing cycle: ischemia from vasospasm generates more ROS, potentially perpetuating attacks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cold trigger mechanism: ROS → Rho/Rho kinase → alpha-2C translocation + augmented Ca sensitivity → vasoconstriction. TRPM8 = cold sensory receptor on afferent neurons, not the direct vasoconstriction trigger. COMLEX: Calcium channel blockers (nifedipine) are first-line treatment for RP, blocking voltage-gated calcium influx into vascular smooth muscle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 5 — Cold and emotional stress triggers Raynaud episodes in SLE patients, reflecting shared vascular reactivity abnormalities between primary RP and lupus-associated secondary Raynaud phenomenon.","whyNot":{"A":"This is distinct from direct c","C":"This is distinct from direct c","D":"TRPM8 is expressed on afferent"}},{"id":"sdl53_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Several drug classes are known to trigger or exacerbate Raynaud phenomenon. Which statement correctly identifies a drug class and its mechanism for worsening digital vasospasm?","choices":{"A":"Calcium channel blockers of the dihydropyridine class trigger Raynaud phenomenon by directly activating alpha-2C adrenoceptors through an off-target receptor interaction that selectively increases cold-induced vasoconstriction without affecting resting vascular tone.","B":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors trigger Raynaud phenomenon by increasing bradykinin levels in digital arterioles, which paradoxically activates B1 receptors on smooth muscle cells to produce vasoconstriction rather than the normal vasodilatory response.","C":"Proton pump inhibitors trigger Raynaud phenomenon by reducing magnesium absorption, lowering plasma magnesium levels and causing vascular smooth muscle membrane depolarization that increases the amplitude of cold-induced digital artery vasoconstriction.","D":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors trigger Raynaud phenomenon by depleting mevalonate pathway intermediates required for Rho protein prenylation, paradoxically activating constitutive Rho/Rho kinase signaling in digital smooth muscle cells through loss of a feedback inhibitory mechanism.","E":"Calcitonin gene-related peptide antagonist monoclonal antibodies, used for migraine prevention, remove the vasodilatory counterbalance provided by sensory nerve CGRP release in the digital microcirculation, unmasking or worsening existing Raynaud phenomenon."},"correct":"E","explanation":"CGRP antagonist monoclonal antibodies (erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab) are used for migraine prevention and are associated with new-onset or worsening Raynaud phenomenon. CGRP is a potent vasodilating neuropeptide released from sensory nerve fibers in digital skin; it normally counterbalances the sympathetic vasoconstriction. When CGRP signaling is blocked, this protective vasodilatory mechanism is removed, leaving alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction unopposed. Other drug triggers: beta-blockers (block compensatory beta-2-mediated vasodilation), ergotamines (direct vasoconstriction), bleomycin (vascular endothelial injury → secondary RP), interferon-alpha, cocaine, sympathomimetics. Calcium channel blockers (nifedipine, amlodipine) TREAT RP, they do not trigger it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CGRP antagonists (for migraine) → worsen/trigger RP by removing vasodilatory counterbalance. Beta-blockers → worsen RP by blocking compensatory beta-2 vasodilation. Bleomycin → secondary RP via vascular injury. COMLEX: First-line RP treatment = calcium channel blockers. Nonselective beta-blockers can worsen RP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 5 — Cold and emotional stress triggers Raynaud episodes in SLE patients, reflecting shared vascular reactivity abnormalities between primary RP and lupus-associated secondary Raynaud phenomenon.","whyNot":{"A":"Calcium channel blockers (nife"}},{"id":"sdl53_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Emotional stress is a recognized non-cold trigger of Raynaud phenomenon. Through which pathway does psychological stress initiate digital vasospasm?","choices":{"A":"Emotional stress activates hypothalamic circuits, particularly the dorsomedial hypothalamus, releasing norepinephrine from sympathetic nerve terminals that innervate the digital arterioles and arteriovenous anastomoses, triggering alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction","B":"Emotional stress activates the parasympathetic nervous system via the dorsal vagal nucleus, releasing acetylcholine from digital arteriolar nerve terminals that paradoxically activates muscarinic M3 receptors on smooth muscle cells rather than the expected vasodilatory response.","C":"Emotional stress directly activates circulating NK cells that release cytotoxic granule proteins into the digital microvasculature, producing endothelial injury and transient platelet aggregation that mechanically obstructs digital capillary flow during stress episodes.","D":"Emotional stress activates the renin-angiotensin system through amygdala-hypothalamic-pituitary axis signaling, releasing angiotensin II that directly activates AT2 receptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells to produce vasospasm during psychological stress.","E":"Emotional stress activates mast cells in digital skin through substance P release from central projections, producing histamine and prostaglandin D2 that paradoxically constrict digital arterioles through H2 receptor activation rather than producing the expected vasodilation."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Emotional stress activates the central nervous system, specifically hypothalamic circuits including the dorsomedial hypothalamus, which coordinates cutaneous vasoconstriction as part of the overall sympathetic response to perceived threat. This leads to increased sympathetic output, releasing norepinephrine from adrenergic nerve terminals that innervate digital arterioles and AVAs. In individuals with Raynaud phenomenon, the exaggerated alpha-2-adrenergic response amplifies the normal vasoconstriction to produce frank digital ischemia. Both cold-induced and emotionally-induced attacks share the same final common pathway of alpha-2 adrenoceptor-mediated smooth muscle contraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two RP triggers: cold (most common) and emotional stress. Both trigger via sympathetic norepinephrine → alpha-2 adrenoceptor activation. COMLEX: Non-pharmacologic RP management includes cold avoidance, stress reduction, smoking cessation, avoiding sympathomimetic drugs and beta-blockers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 5 — Cold and emotional stress triggers Raynaud episodes in SLE patients, reflecting shared vascular reactivity abnormalities between primary RP and lupus-associated secondary Raynaud phenomenon."},{"id":"sdl53_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with episodic migraine is started on fremanezumab for migraine prevention. Six weeks later she reports new episodes of bilateral finger blanching followed by cyanosis and redness triggered by cold exposure. Her past medical history is otherwise unremarkable and ANA is negative. Which mechanism best explains this adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Fremanezumab cross-reacts with alpha-2C adrenoceptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells due to structural homology between the CGRP receptor and the alpha-2C receptor extracellular domain, directly triggering cold-induced Golgi-to-membrane receptor translocation.","B":"Fremanezumab blocks calcitonin gene-related peptide, a vasodilating neuropeptide released from sensory nerve fibers in digital skin that normally counteracts sympathetic vasoconstriction, so its blockade unmasks latent Raynaud phenomenon in susceptible people","C":"Fremanezumab reduces CGRP signaling in the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center, impairing central temperature sensing and causing the central nervous system to misinterpret neutral temperatures as cold, triggering inappropriate sympathetic vasoconstriction of digital vessels.","D":"Fremanezumab activates complement C3 via the classical pathway through its Fc region, producing circulating C3a anaphylatoxin that selectively acts on digital arteriolar mast cells to release histamine-driven vasoconstriction independent of adrenergic mechanisms.","E":"Fremanezumab inhibits prostacyclin production by endothelial cells through a CGRP-receptor-independent off-target effect on COX-2 in the digital microvascular endothelium, removing a key vasodilatory counterbalance against alpha-adrenergic constriction."},"correct":"B","explanation":"CGRP is a 37-amino-acid vasodilating neuropeptide released from sensory nerve fibers in the skin, including digital tissue. It normally provides a vasodilatory counterbalance to sympathetic noradrenergic vasoconstriction. Case series report that CGRP antagonist monoclonal antibodies used for migraine prevention are associated with onset or worsening of Raynaud phenomenon. This represents a pharmacologically predicted adverse effect given CGRP's role in maintaining basal digital vasodilatory tone. Evidence also shows reduced CGRP-immunoreactive nerve fibers in skin biopsies of primary RP patients, further implicating CGRP deficiency in RP pathogenesis. This patient has primary RP triggered by drug-induced CGRP blockade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CGRP = vasodilatory sensory neuropeptide in digital skin. CGRP antagonist antibodies (for migraine) → worsen/trigger RP. Reduced CGRP fibers in primary RP skin biopsies also implicated in disease. COMLEX: Assess all RP patients for medication triggers including CGRP antagonists, beta-blockers, ergots, and bleomycin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy in Raynaud phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl53_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with primary Raynaud phenomenon undergoes skin biopsy of a digital lesion. Immunohistochemical staining shows a marked reduction in calcitonin gene-related peptide-immunoreactive nerve fibers in the dermis compared to normal controls. Which mechanism best explains how this finding contributes to her exaggerated vasospastic attacks?","choices":{"A":"Reduced CGRP-containing nerve fibers impair the afferent sensory signaling that detects finger temperature, preventing the central thermoregulatory reflex from activating compensatory systemic warming responses that would normally abort the vasospastic episode.","B":"Reduced CGRP-containing nerve fibers decrease substance P co-release from nociceptive fibers, impairing the axon reflex vasodilation that normally recruits additional blood flow to inflamed tissue adjacent to ischemic digital arteries during vasospastic attacks.","C":"Reduced CGRP-containing nerve fibers decrease neuropeptide Y co-release from sympathetic terminals, removing the autocrine inhibition that normally limits norepinephrine release from digital adrenergic nerve endings and prevents excessive alpha-2C receptor activation.","D":"Reduced CGRP-containing nerve fibers impair the perivascular anti-inflammatory signal that normally prevents mast cell degranulation in digital skin, enabling histamine-driven platelet aggregation and thromboxane A2 release during cold-induced vasospastic episodes.","E":"Reduced sensory nerve CGRP-containing fibers remove a critical vasodilatory counterbalance in digital skin; without adequate CGRP release during cold exposure, the sympathetic alpha-2-adrenergic vasoconstriction is unopposed, producing exaggerated and prolonged digital vasospasm."},"correct":"E","explanation":"CGRP-containing sensory nerve fibers in digital skin release CGRP in response to tissue stimuli, providing vasodilatory tone that counterbalances sympathetic vasoconstriction. A reduction in these fibers removes this protective anti-vasospastic signal. Without adequate CGRP release, the alpha-2-adrenergic vasoconstriction from sympathetic nerve stimulation is not opposed by sensory nerve vasodilation. This contributes to the exaggerated and prolonged vasoconstriction of Raynaud attacks. This mechanism is supported by the clinical observation that migraine treatments blocking CGRP trigger Raynaud episodes. CGRP also has NO-releasing effects on endothelium, further contributing to its vasodilatory role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CGRP from sensory nerve fibers = vasodilatory counterbalance. Reduced CGRP fibers in primary RP skin = unopposed alpha-2 vasoconstriction. COMLEX: Both the sensory nervous system and the sympathetic nervous system regulate digital vascular tone in opposing directions; imbalance toward sympathetic predominance drives RP.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy in Raynaud phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl53_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis of Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman who has had primary Raynaud phenomenon since age 18 reports that her episodes improved markedly after menopause at age 52. Which mechanism best explains the estrogen-related female predominance and the improvement of primary Raynaud phenomenon after menopause?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen upregulates alpha-2C adrenoceptor expression on cutaneous arteriolar smooth muscle cells, augmenting cold-induced vasoconstriction; when estrogen levels fall at menopause, alpha-2C receptor density decreases and cold-induced vasoconstriction lessens, explaining the female predominance","B":"Estrogen increases sympathetic nerve fiber density in digital arteriolar smooth muscle, creating more adrenergic terminals per vessel and amplifying norepinephrine release during cold; menopause reduces nerve fiber density and attenuates the noradrenergic trigger of vasospastic attacks.","C":"Estrogen directly activates TRPM8 cold receptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells through a genomic receptor mechanism, increasing the cells sensitivity to cold temperatures; when estrogen declines at menopause, TRPM8 receptor sensitivity decreases and vasospastic attacks become less frequent.","D":"Estrogen suppresses endothelial nitric oxide synthase transcription in digital arteriolar endothelium, reducing the vasodilatory counterbalance against sympathetic vasoconstriction; menopause restores eNOS expression and increases nitric oxide production, reducing vasospastic episodes.","E":"Estrogen promotes mast cell accumulation in digital skin through recruitment signals from estrogen receptor-expressing perivascular stromal cells; at menopause, reduced mast cell density decreases histamine-driven platelet activation that amplifies alpha-adrenergic digital vasoconstriction."},"correct":"A","explanation":"In vitro studies demonstrate that estrogen upregulates alpha-2C adrenoceptor expression on cutaneous vessels through the Epac/JNK/AP-1 signaling pathway. Experimental estrogen administration increases alpha-2C adrenoceptor density, leading to potentiated cold-induced vasoconstriction in mouse tail arteries. Epidemiologically, primary RP is more common in age-matched females than males (course SDL 53), and symptoms frequently improve after menopause when estrogen levels fall. Unopposed estrogen use in postmenopausal women is also associated with increased RP risk in epidemiologic studies. This explains why RP is more prevalent in young women during their reproductive years.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen upregulates alpha-2C adrenoceptors → augments cold-induced vasoconstriction → explains female predominance and menopausal improvement of primary RP. COMLEX: Primary RP = young female, onset age 15-30, symmetric, benign, improves with age/menopause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 3 — Pathology of vasculopathy in Raynaud phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma."},{"id":"sdl53_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman presents with progressive bilateral finger color changes triggered by cold and asymmetric severity between her hands. Nailfold capillaroscopy shows dilated, distorted capillary loops, avascular areas, and giant capillaries. ANA is positive with anti-Scl-70 detected. Which statement best explains what the capillaroscopy findings indicate about her Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Dilated capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy indicate excessive arteriovenous anastomosis dilation from endothelial prostacyclin overproduction in primary Raynaud phenomenon, representing a compensatory response to recurrent vasospastic ischemia.","B":"Dilated capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy indicate complement-mediated destruction of the deep dermal venous plexus, producing the same pattern regardless of whether the Raynaud phenomenon is primary or secondary to connective tissue disease.","C":"Dilated, tortuous, and disrupted capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy indicate structural microangiopathy of the nutritional capillary bed, characteristic of secondary Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis and of impaired tissue perfusion","D":"Dilated capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy represent a normal physiologic response to chronic repetitive cold exposure in any Raynaud patient and do not distinguish primary from secondary Raynaud phenomenon in clinical evaluation.","E":"Dilated capillary loops with avascular areas on nailfold capillaroscopy indicate excessive alpha-2C adrenoceptor density on nutritional capillary pericytes, producing a pattern specific to primary Raynaud phenomenon where the local defect is most pronounced in small nutritional capillaries."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nailfold capillaroscopy is the key test for distinguishing primary from secondary Raynaud phenomenon. Normal: regular, parallel capillary loops. Primary RP: normal capillaroscopy. Secondary RP (SSc pattern): dilated capillary loops, giant capillaries, avascular areas, hemorrhages, and disorganized architecture. These findings reflect structural microvascular damage with intimal fibrosis and capillary loss that characterizes SSc microangiopathy. Anti-Scl-70 confirms SSc. The avascular areas indicate capillary loss/dropout, meaning nutritional tissue flow is impaired even at rest, explaining the risk of digital ulceration and critical ischemia that distinguishes secondary RP from primary RP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nailfold capillaroscopy: normal = primary RP; abnormal (giant capillaries, avascular areas, dropout) = secondary RP, especially SSc. Giant capillaries are pathognomonic for SSc. COMLEX: Anti-Scl-70 = diffuse SSc + ILD; anticentromere = limited SSc + CREST + PAH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — Contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","whyNot":{"A":"Primary RP: normal capillarosc","B":"Primary RP: normal capillarosc","D":"Primary RP: normal capillarosc","E":"Primary RP: normal capillarosc"}},{"id":"sdl53_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with longstanding hypothyroidism and poor medication compliance presents with bilateral symmetric finger pallor and cyanosis triggered by modest cold exposure that developed over the past year. His thyroid-stimulating hormone is markedly elevated. Which mechanism best explains hypothyroidism as a cause of secondary Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism directly upregulates TRPM8 cold receptor expression on digital arteriolar endothelial cells through thyroid hormone response element-mediated suppression, causing these vessels to respond to normal ambient temperatures as if exposed to extreme cold.","B":"Hypothyroidism elevates circulating thyroid-stimulating hormone levels, which acts directly on TSH receptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells to activate G-protein-mediated phospholipase C, increasing intracellular calcium and producing sustained vasospasm.","C":"Hypothyroidism decreases alpha-2C adrenoceptor expression through reduced thyroid hormone-mediated receptor gene transcription, producing a paradoxical state where cold stimulation induces massive receptor upregulation from a lower baseline, generating an exaggerated vasospastic response.","D":"Hypothyroidism reduces basal metabolic rate, cardiac output, and peripheral tissue perfusion, lowering digital blood flow so that the normal cold-induced vasoconstrictive response more readily produces digital ischemia with the observed pallor and cyanosis","E":"Hypothyroidism increases plasma fibrinogen and blood viscosity through reduced hepatic fibrinogen clearance, producing sluggish digital capillary flow that slows local temperature equilibration and amplifies the duration of cold-induced vasoconstriction in affected fingers."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hypothyroidism causes secondary Raynaud phenomenon through multiple mechanisms: reduced cardiac output and peripheral perfusion at baseline; increased peripheral vascular resistance; and reduced digital blood flow, so an ordinary cold-induced vasoconstrictive response more readily produces ischemia. Elevated TSH does not directly act on digital vessels. Treatment of hypothyroidism often improves RP. Other endocrine causes of secondary RP include pheochromocytoma (excess catecholamines) and carcinoid syndrome. The important clinical point is that secondary RP may reveal an underlying treatable systemic condition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroidism → secondary RP via reduced cardiac output and peripheral perfusion. Screen any new secondary RP for thyroid disease with TSH. COMLEX: Secondary RP evaluation: ANA, anti-Scl-70, anticentromere, CBC, CMP, TSH, urinalysis, nailfold capillaroscopy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — Contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","whyNot":{"B":"Elevated TSH does not directly"}},{"id":"sdl53_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary Raynaud's as well as underlying diseases associated with secondary causes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old man presents with finger blanching and cyanosis involving predominantly the first two fingers of his right hand but sparing his left hand. He has no known connective tissue disease. His presentation is being evaluated for primary versus secondary Raynaud phenomenon. Which feature of his presentation most strongly suggests secondary rather than primary Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"The male sex of this patient strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon, as primary Raynaud phenomenon almost exclusively affects females and the occurrence in males should always prompt evaluation for an underlying systemic vascular disease.","B":"The involvement of only two fingers rather than all fingers of both hands strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon, as primary Raynaud phenomenon requires involvement of all ten digits to fulfill the diagnostic criteria established by international classification guidelines.","C":"The age of onset at 45 years rather than during adolescence strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon, as primary Raynaud phenomenon exclusively occurs before age 30 and any new-onset vasospastic disease in adults over 40 is by definition secondary.","D":"The occurrence of isolated finger blanching without the complete triphasic white-blue-red color change strongly suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon, as primary Raynaud phenomenon requires all three color changes to be present on physical examination for diagnosis.","E":"The asymmetric distribution, with involvement confined to specific fingers of one hand rather than bilateral symmetric involvement, suggests secondary Raynaud phenomenon from localized vascular disease rather than systemic sympathetic hyperreactivity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Asymmetric involvement is a key clinical feature favoring secondary over primary Raynaud phenomenon. Primary RP typically causes symmetric bilateral involvement of both hands with similar digit distribution. Asymmetric or unilateral Raynaud suggests localized vascular pathology such as atherosclerosis, Buerger disease, thoracic outlet syndrome, emboli, or local injury to specific vessels. Male sex is a relative indicator for secondary RP (primary is more common in females, but males do get primary RP). Onset after age 30 suggests secondary RP. The complete triphasic color change is not required for diagnosis of either form. Any of these features — asymmetry, male sex, older onset, progressive worsening — should prompt evaluation for secondary causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Red flags for secondary RP: asymmetric/unilateral involvement, age >30 at onset, male sex, progressive worsening, abnormal nailfold capillaroscopy, positive ANA or specific autoantibodies. COMLEX: Thoracic outlet syndrome can cause unilateral upper extremity RP from compression of the subclavian artery or brachial plexus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — Contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","whyNot":{"A":"Male sex is a relative indicat"}},{"id":"sdl53_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 52-year-old male construction worker who has operated pneumatic jackhammers for 20 years presents with bilateral finger blanching and numbness triggered by cold, which has progressively worsened over the past 5 years. He denies joint symptoms, skin changes, or systemic symptoms. Which mechanism best explains his occupationally acquired Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure causes direct structural damage to digital arteriolar walls and neural dysfunction in digital nerves, producing both a fixed vasculopathic component from vessel wall injury and abnormal vascular reactivity from impaired vasomotor nerve function.","B":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure selectively activates TRPM8 cold receptors in digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells, producing permanent upregulation of these receptors that causes inappropriate cold perception at normal temperatures in occupationally exposed workers.","C":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure causes repetitive micro-fractures of the periosteal attachments of digital flexor tendons, releasing bone morphogenetic protein-2 that stimulates digital arteriolar smooth muscle cell transdifferentiation into bone-forming cells and reduces vessel lumen diameter.","D":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure activates the NLRP3 inflammasome in digital skin macrophages through mechanical DAMP signals, releasing IL-1beta that promotes alpha-2C adrenoceptor overexpression on smooth muscle cells through an NFkB-mediated transcriptional mechanism.","E":"Chronic repetitive vibration exposure produces supraphysiologic norepinephrine release from digital sympathetic nerve terminals, exhausting norepinephrine stores and causing paradoxical denervation hypersensitivity of alpha-2C adrenoceptors on smooth muscle cells."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vibration-associated Raynaud phenomenon, also called hand-arm vibration syndrome or vibration white finger, is an occupational secondary RP caused by long-term use of vibrating tools such as jackhammers, chainsaws, and grinding tools. The mechanism involves direct vascular injury to digital artery walls and neural dysfunction from mechanical trauma to digital nerves. Biomarkers of endothelial injury and dysfunction, inflammation, and neuronal injury are elevated in affected workers. This results in both structural vessel wall damage and abnormal vascular reactivity. The condition is progressive with continued exposure and may not fully reverse with job change. Recognition is important for occupational health.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vibration white finger = occupational secondary RP from pneumatic tools, chainsaws. Direct vessel wall and nerve injury. Progressive with continued exposure. COMLEX: Occupational history is essential in RP evaluation. Hand-arm vibration syndrome is a reportable occupational disease in some jurisdictions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 5 — Broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations in SLE patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman with hepatitis C virus infection and mixed cryoglobulinemia develops episodic finger blanching, cyanosis, and purpuric lesions that are triggered by cold exposure. Her symptoms are more severe and persistent than typical vasospasm. Which mechanism explains cryoglobulinemia as a cause of Raynaud-like digital ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Cryoglobulins act as autoantibodies directed against alpha-2C adrenoceptors on digital arteriolar smooth muscle cells, activating these receptors at cold temperatures through a conformational change in the immunoglobulin Fc region that mimics norepinephrine binding.","B":"Cryoglobulins deposit in digital arteriolar walls as immune complexes that activate complement exclusively through the alternative pathway, producing membrane attack complex formation in smooth muscle cells and causing irreversible lysis of digital arteriolar smooth muscle.","C":"Cryoglobulins are immunoglobulins that reversibly precipitate in cold temperatures, physically obstructing digital microvascular lumens during cold exposure and activating complement and platelet aggregation, producing a vasoconstrictive-thrombotic combined mechanism of digital ischemia.","D":"Cryoglobulins bind directly to endothelial TRPM8 cold receptors on digital capillaries, acting as permanent cold receptor agonists that maintain TRPM8 in a constitutively active state even at normal ambient temperature and producing temperature-independent vasospasm.","E":"Cryoglobulins are released from activated mast cells during cold-induced degranulation in digital skin, providing an IgE-mediated triggering mechanism that produces histamine-driven digital arteriolar constriction rather than the classic adrenergic mechanism of primary Raynaud phenomenon."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cryoglobulins are immunoglobulins that reversibly precipitate at temperatures below 37°C (98.6°F). In cold conditions, cryoglobulins form insoluble aggregates that can physically obstruct digital microvascular lumens, triggering complement activation and platelet aggregation at the site of precipitation. This creates a unique combined vasoconstrictive-thrombotic mechanism distinct from primary RP. HCV-associated mixed cryoglobulinemia (type II) involves monoclonal IgM with rheumatoid factor activity complexed with polyclonal IgG. Clinical features include palpable purpura, arthralgias, neuropathy, and Raynaud-like symptoms. Treatment targets the underlying HCV infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryoglobulinemia + cold = precipitated Ig → microvascular obstruction + complement activation. HCV = most common cause of mixed cryoglobulinemia. Triad: purpura + arthralgia + neuropathy. COMLEX: Secondary RP hematologic causes: cryoglobulinemia, polycythemia vera, paraproteinemia (increased blood viscosity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 5 — Broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations in SLE patients."},{"id":"sdl53_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss triggers for Raynaud's phenomenon.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with hypertension and primary Raynaud phenomenon has his antihypertensive regimen changed from amlodipine to propranolol by his cardiologist. Within three weeks his Raynaud episodes become significantly more frequent and severe. Which mechanism explains why beta-blockers worsen Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Propranolol crosses the blood-brain barrier and directly activates alpha-2C adrenoceptor transcription in the hypothalamus, increasing the central sympathetic output to digital arterioles and amplifying the cold-induced vasoconstriction of Raynaud phenomenon through a central mechanism.","B":"Beta-adrenergic receptor blockade prevents beta-2-mediated compensatory vasodilation in the peripheral cutaneous vessels, leaving the alpha-adrenergic vasoconstrictive effect of norepinephrine unopposed during cold exposure and emotional stress, worsening attacks","C":"Propranolol causes peripheral edema by reducing cardiac output, increasing venous hydrostatic pressure in digital capillaries and displacing erythrocytes from the microcirculation into the interstitium, producing a mechanical reduction in digital capillary blood flow that mimics vasospasm.","D":"Propranolol reduces renal prostaglandin synthesis by blocking beta-1 receptors on juxtaglomerular cells, decreasing renin-mediated prostaglandin E2 production that would otherwise provide vasodilatory protection to digital arterioles during cold-induced vasospastic attacks.","E":"Propranolol directly activates TRPM8 cold receptors in digital arteriolar smooth muscle through an off-target ion channel mechanism, lowering the temperature threshold for cold-sensing and causing vasospastic attacks to be triggered by warmer ambient temperatures than before."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta-2 adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth muscle mediate vasodilation when activated by circulating epinephrine or local beta-adrenergic agonists. This beta-2-mediated vasodilation acts as a physiologic counterbalance to alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction. When beta-blockers are administered, this compensatory vasodilatory mechanism is blocked, leaving alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction unopposed. In patients with Raynaud phenomenon, this unmasking of alpha-adrenergic dominance worsens vasospastic attacks. Propranolol is nonselective and blocks beta-2 receptors directly; beta-1-selective agents are generally better tolerated, but caution is still advised. Calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine or nifedipine are preferred antihypertensives in these patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beta-blockers worsen RP by blocking beta-2-mediated compensatory vasodilation, leaving alpha-adrenergic constriction unopposed. Nonselective beta-blockers are best avoided in RP. COMLEX: Preferred antihypertensives in RP: calcium channel blockers (nifedipine, amlodipine) which also treat RP. Avoid: beta-blockers, sympathomimetics, ergots.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 5 — Broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations in SLE patients."}],"54":[{"id":"sdl54_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which bone tumor is most commonly found in the metaphysis of long bones and characteristically contains a cartilage cap overlying a bony exostosis?","choices":{"A":"Osteochondroma, the most common benign bone tumor, arises at the metaphysis near the growth plate and is capped by a layer of hyaline cartilage","B":"Enchondroma, which arises in the medullary cavity of small tubular bones in the hands and feet and is not capped by external cartilage","C":"Chondroblastoma, which arises in the epiphysis of long bones in skeletally immature patients and does not project externally from cortical bone","D":"Giant cell tumor, which arises in the epiphysis of skeletally mature long bones and is not associated with a cartilage cap or cortical exostosis","E":"Ewing sarcoma, which arises in the diaphysis of long bones and presents as an aggressive lytic lesion rather than a cartilage-capped exostosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteochondroma is the most common benign bone tumor and arises at the metaphysis adjacent to the growth plate. It projects outward from the cortex as a bony stalk capped by hyaline cartilage. The cartilage cap is the key distinguishing feature; secondary chondrosarcoma occurs rarely, usually with multiple hereditary exostoses (Robbins Ch 26; FA p470 \"Rarely transforms to chondrosarcoma\"). Enchondromas occur in medullary bone of small tubular bones. Chondroblastomas and giant cell tumors are epiphyseal. Ewing sarcoma is diaphyseal and aggressive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteochondroma is THE most common benign bone tumor — metaphysis, cartilage cap, near growth plate. HME (hereditary multiple exostoses) caused by EXT1/EXT2 loss-of-function mutations; increased malignant transformation risk. COMLEX: Palpable painless mass near a joint in a teenager — think osteochondroma first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development and location","whyNot":{"C":"Chondroblastomas and giant cel"}},{"id":"sdl54_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-year-old male presents with worsening knee pain and a palpable mass. X-ray shows a sunburst pattern and Codman triangle at the distal femur. Which of the following best describes the location rule for this tumor type?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the epiphysis of long bones, explaining its association with the subchondral bone and articular surface","B":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the diaphysis of long bones, where Haversian canal disruption provides the initial nidus for tumor growth","C":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the vertebral posterior elements, which is why it frequently causes neurologic compromise at presentation","D":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in flat bones of the axial skeleton, particularly the pelvis, as the site of highest red marrow activity in adolescents","E":"Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the metaphysis of long bones, with the distal femur and proximal tibia around the knee being the most common sites"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor in adolescents and arises in the metaphysis of long bones — distal femur (most common), proximal tibia, and proximal humerus together account for the majority of cases. The metaphysis is the fastest-growing region of long bones, explaining the predilection. Periosteal elevation creates the Codman triangle, and tumor extension into soft tissue produces the sunburst pattern on X-ray. Ewing sarcoma is the diaphyseal malignancy in this age group. Chondrosarcoma affects the axial skeleton in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board vignette — teenage male, knee pain, sunburst/Codman triangle, distal femur = osteosarcoma until proven otherwise. Bimodal distribution: 75% before age 20 (de novo) and a smaller second peak in older adults (secondary to Paget's, radiation, bone infarct) (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: Bone pain at night that is NOT relieved by aspirin in a teenager near the knee = osteosarcoma (contrast with osteoid osteoma, which IS aspirin-responsive).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development and location","whyNot":{"B":"Ewing sarcoma is the diaphysea"}},{"id":"sdl54_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old man presents with dull aching pain in the pelvis. MRI reveals a lobulated intramedullary lesion with a chondroid matrix in the iliac bone. Biopsy shows atypical chondrocytes in lacunae without osteoid production. Which tumor type does this lesion most likely represent?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma, which produces malignant osteoid as its defining histologic feature and primarily affects the metaphysis of long bones in adolescents","B":"Enchondroma, which is a benign cartilage tumor of the medullary cavity that most commonly affects the small tubular bones of the hands and feet","C":"Osteochondroma, which is a cartilage-capped exostosis arising at the metaphysis and is the most common benign bone tumor overall","D":"Chondrosarcoma, which preferentially involves the axial skeleton and pelvis in middle-aged to older adults and does not produce osteoid","E":"Ewing sarcoma, which involves the diaphysis of long bones and flat bones and histologically shows small round blue cells rather than atypical chondrocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chondrosarcoma is the second most common primary malignant bone tumor, following osteosarcoma. It preferentially arises in the axial skeleton — pelvis, shoulder girdle, and ribs — in adults aged 40-60 years (Robbins Ch 26 table; FA p471: most common over 50 years). Histologically, atypical chondrocytes in lacunae are the defining feature; no osteoid is produced. Chondrosarcoma is largely chemotherapy-resistant, making wide surgical resection the primary treatment. Osteosarcoma requires osteoid production for diagnosis. Enchondroma is its benign counterpart in small tubular bones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chondrosarcoma — adults, axial/pelvic location, chondroid matrix, atypical chondrocytes in lacunae, NO osteoid, chemotherapy resistant (surgery only). Second most common primary malignant bone tumor after osteosarcoma. Enchondroma → chondrosarcoma transformation risk is elevated in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome (IDH1/IDH2 mutations). COMLEX: Pelvic bone lesion with chondroid matrix in a middle-aged male = chondrosarcoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development and location","whyNot":{"A":"Osteosarcoma requires osteoid "}},{"id":"sdl54_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following histologic features is required to establish a definitive diagnosis of osteosarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Production of lacelike osteoid directly by malignant stromal cells is the single required histologic criterion for diagnosing osteosarcoma","B":"Periosteal elevation forming a Codman triangle is the definitive histologic criterion, as it reflects tumor expansion through the cortex to the periosteum","C":"Presence of small round blue cells arranged in sheets with Homer Wright pseudorosettes distinguishes osteosarcoma from other malignant bone tumors","D":"Identification of atypical chondrocytes within lacunae surrounded by a chondroid matrix is the pathognomonic finding required for osteosarcoma diagnosis","E":"High mitotic index greater than ten mitoses per ten high-power fields in a spindle cell neoplasm arising in bone establishes the diagnosis of osteosarcoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteosarcoma is defined histologically by the production of lacelike osteoid by malignant mesenchymal cells. This is the single required criterion — without osteoid production, the tumor cannot be classified as osteosarcoma regardless of other findings. The cells show marked pleomorphism, hyperchromatic nuclei, and abnormal mitoses. Codman triangle is a radiographic finding, not histologic. Small round blue cells with pseudorosettes describe Ewing sarcoma. Atypical chondrocytes in lacunae describe chondrosarcoma. High mitotic index in a spindle cell tumor without osteoid production would suggest fibrosarcoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The most testable osteosarcoma histology point — malignant cells producing LACELIKE OSTEOID is the REQUIRED histologic finding. Pleomorphism and mitoses are expected but not defining. Codman triangle and sunburst are radiographic, not histologic. COMLEX: Bone biopsy showing atypical spindle cells producing pink amorphous material = osteoid = osteosarcoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"},{"id":"sdl54_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl is newly diagnosed with osteosarcoma. Genetic testing reveals a germline mutation in the RB1 gene. Compared to patients with sporadic osteosarcoma, patients with germline RB1 mutations have which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Identical osteosarcoma risk to sporadic cases, because RB1 mutations only predispose to retinoblastoma and do not influence bone tumor formation","B":"Modestly elevated risk of about 5-fold compared to the general population, as RB1 loss alone is insufficient without additional oncogenic co-mutations","C":"Approximately 1000-fold increased lifetime risk of developing osteosarcoma compared to the general population due to loss of the RB1 tumor suppressor","D":"Reduced osteosarcoma risk compared to sporadic cases because germline mutations trigger p53-mediated apoptosis before malignant transformation occurs","E":"Elevated risk exclusively for the low-grade parosteal subtype of osteosarcoma rather than the conventional high-grade intramedullary variant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Germline RB1 mutations cause hereditary retinoblastoma and confer approximately a 1000-fold increased risk of osteosarcoma compared to the general population. RB1 mutations are found in approximately 70% of sporadic osteosarcomas as somatic events, making Rb pathway disruption central to osteosarcoma pathogenesis. The mechanism involves loss of G1/S checkpoint control. Other genetic associations include TP53 (Li-Fraumeni syndrome), CDKN2A (p16/p14ARF), and MDM2/CDK4 amplification in low-grade osteosarcomas on chromosome 12q14.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RB1 mutations — sporadic 70%, germline = 1000-fold osteosarcoma risk. TP53 mutations = Li-Fraumeni syndrome → osteosarcoma. MDM2/CDK4 amplification on chr 12q14 = low-grade osteosarcoma variant. COMLEX: Young patient with prior retinoblastoma develops bone pain near the knee in adolescence — screen for second primary osteosarcoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"},{"id":"sdl54_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the standard first-line treatment approach for high-grade osteosarcoma of the extremity, and what is the approximate 5-year survival without distant metastasis?","choices":{"A":"Immediate amputation followed by adjuvant radiation therapy is the standard approach, achieving approximately 40% five-year survival regardless of tumor extent","B":"Radiation therapy alone as the primary local control modality achieves approximately 60% five-year survival because osteosarcoma is highly radiosensitive","C":"Surgery alone without chemotherapy is appropriate for metaphyseal tumors under 5 cm and achieves approximately 85% five-year survival in this selected group","D":"Neoadjuvant chemotherapy alone without surgery is preferred in adolescents to preserve growth plate function, achieving 50% five-year survival in non-metastatic cases","E":"Neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by limb-salvage surgery and adjuvant chemotherapy achieves approximately 70% five-year survival in non-metastatic disease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"High-grade osteosarcoma is treated with a trimodal approach: neoadjuvant chemotherapy (typically MAP — methotrexate, doxorubicin, cisplatin) followed by limb-salvage surgery, then adjuvant chemotherapy. This approach achieves approximately 65-70% five-year survival in non-metastatic disease, a dramatic improvement over the pre-chemotherapy era. With pulmonary metastases present at diagnosis, five-year survival drops below 20%. The response to neoadjuvant therapy (assessed histologically as percent tumor necrosis in the resected specimen) is the strongest prognostic factor. Osteosarcoma is relatively radioresistant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteosarcoma = neoadjuvant chemo (MAP protocol) + limb-salvage surgery + adjuvant chemo. 5-year survival: ~70% without metastasis, <20% with lung mets. Osteosarcoma is RADIORESISTANT (unlike Ewing sarcoma which is radiosensitive). Histologic response to neoadjuvant chemo = strongest prognostic factor. COMLEX: Lung is the primary metastatic site (hematogenous spread via cortical veins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients","whyNot":{"B":"Osteosarcoma is relatively rad"}},{"id":"sdl54_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of dull aching in his right thigh that is worse at night. Vital signs are normal. Physical exam shows mild swelling over the mid-diaphysis of the femur. X-ray reveals a poorly defined lytic lesion with an \"onion-skin\" periosteal reaction and no Codman triangle. CBC shows leukocytosis and elevated ESR. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma, which typically arises in the metaphysis of long bones in adolescents and produces a sunburst pattern and Codman triangle rather than an onion-skin reaction","B":"Osteomyelitis, which can mimic Ewing sarcoma radiographically but typically presents with fever, localized warmth, and positive blood cultures in a shorter symptomatic course","C":"Ewing sarcoma, which characteristically arises in the diaphysis of long bones and flat bones, primarily in patients aged 10-20 years, with a classic onion-skin periosteal reaction","D":"Chondrosarcoma, which arises in the axial skeleton of middle-aged adults and produces a chondroid matrix on imaging rather than a pure lytic diaphyseal lesion","E":"Giant cell tumor, which arises in the epiphysis of skeletally mature patients as a soap-bubble lytic lesion and does not produce a periosteal reaction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ewing sarcoma is a small round blue cell tumor that arises in the diaphysis of long bones and flat bones (pelvis, ribs, vertebrae) and peaks in patients aged 10-20 years (Robbins Ch 26 table; FA p471: generally males under 15). The onion-skin periosteal reaction reflects successive layers of reactive bone laid down as the tumor elevates the periosteum. Systemic features (fever, leukocytosis, elevated ESR) are common and can mimic osteomyelitis. The EWSR1-FLI1 translocation (t(11;22)) is pathognomonic. Osteosarcoma is metaphyseal with a sunburst/Codman pattern. Chondrosarcoma and GCT are adult tumors at different locations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ewing sarcoma — diaphysis, 10-20 years, onion-skin periosteal reaction, small round blue cells, t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1 fusion. Radiosensitive (unlike osteosarcoma). Key differential: osteomyelitis (can look identical on imaging — biopsy required). COMLEX: Flat bone or diaphyseal bone lesion in a teenager with systemic symptoms — Ewing sarcoma until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development","whyNot":{"B":"Systemic features (fever, leuk","D":"Chondrosarcoma and GCT are adu","E":"Chondrosarcoma and GCT are adu"}},{"id":"sdl54_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman undergoes X-ray for a sprained wrist and is incidentally found to have a well-defined radiolucent lesion in the proximal phalanx of her ring finger. There is no periosteal reaction and no cortical destruction. She is asymptomatic. Biopsy shows bland chondrocytes in a chondroid matrix with no atypia. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and its associated genetic finding?","choices":{"A":"Enchondroma with IDH1 or IDH2 somatic mutations, which arise in the medullary cavity of small tubular bones and are most often asymptomatic and incidentally discovered","B":"Osteochondroma with EXT1 or EXT2 mutations, which arises at the metaphysis as an external exostosis rather than as an intramedullary lesion of the phalanges","C":"Chondrosarcoma with IDH1 mutations, which is the malignant transformation of this lesion and should be diagnosed based on the presence of bland chondrocytes without atypia","D":"Chondroblastoma with H3F3B mutations, which is an epiphyseal lesion of skeletally immature patients rather than a medullary lesion of the small bones of the hand","E":"Osteoid osteoma with no known recurrent mutation, which arises in the cortex of long bones and characteristically produces severe nocturnal pain relieved by aspirin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enchondromas are benign cartilage tumors that arise in the medullary cavity, most commonly in the small tubular bones of the hands and feet, and are often incidentally discovered. They harbor IDH1 or IDH2 somatic mutations. Histologically, bland chondrocytes without atypia are seen in a chondroid matrix. Multiple enchondromas characterize Ollier disease; when combined with spindle cell hemangiomas, this constitutes Maffucci syndrome, both of which carry elevated risk of chondrosarcoma transformation. Osteochondromas are external cortical lesions at the metaphysis with EXT1/EXT2 mutations. Chondroblastoma is epiphyseal in skeletally immature patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enchondroma — medullary cavity, small bones of hand, IDH1/IDH2 mutations, bland chondrocytes, incidental finding. Ollier disease = multiple enchondromas. Maffucci syndrome = enchondromas + spindle cell hemangiomas (both IDH-mutant). Malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma is a risk in both syndromes. COMLEX: Incidental radiolucent lesion in a finger phalanx in a young adult = enchondroma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development"},{"id":"sdl54_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between benign and malignant bone lesions and their location in bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old man presents with knee pain and swelling. X-ray shows a lytic, soap-bubble lesion in the distal femoral epiphysis that extends to the articular surface. There is no periosteal reaction. He is skeletally mature. Biopsy reveals osteoclast-like giant cells interspersed with mononuclear stromal cells. The mononuclear cells are RANKL-positive. Which of the following best describes this tumor's behavior?","choices":{"A":"Giant cell tumor of bone is universally benign with essentially zero malignant potential, and simple observation without surgical intervention is the standard of care for all cases","B":"Giant cell tumor of bone is a purely reactive lesion driven by inflammation and resolves spontaneously following NSAIDs and physiotherapy without surgical intervention","C":"Giant cell tumor of bone is best classified as a high-grade sarcoma at diagnosis because RANKL-positive mononuclear cells drive aggressive bone destruction from the outset","D":"Giant cell tumor of bone has a 40-50% rate of malignant transformation to osteosarcoma within 5 years, requiring neoadjuvant chemotherapy before any surgical planning","E":"Giant cell tumor of bone is locally aggressive and recurs locally in 40-60% of cases after curettage, although the uncommon lung metastases often regress or can be excised"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Giant cell tumor of bone is a locally aggressive benign tumor characterized by RANKL-expressing mononuclear stromal cells that recruit and activate osteoclast-like giant cells to drive bone destruction. It arises in the epiphysis of skeletally mature long bones, most commonly the distal femur, proximal tibia, and distal radius, and may extend to the articular surface. The soap-bubble appearance on X-ray is classic. Treatment is extended curettage with adjuvant therapy; 40% to 60% recur locally after curettage, and up to 4% metastasize to the lungs, where the deposits often regress or can be excised (Robbins Ch 26). Denosumab targets RANKL and is used for unresectable or recurrent disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giant cell tumor — EPIPHYSIS, skeletally MATURE, soap-bubble lytic lesion, osteoclast-like giant cells, RANKL-positive stromal cells. Locally aggressive but benign (40-60% local recurrence after curettage; up to 4% lung metastases, Robbins Ch 26). Denosumab (anti-RANKL) for unresectable disease. COMLEX: Knee pain + soap-bubble epiphyseal lytic lesion in a 25-35 year old = giant cell tumor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and remodeling as the cellular context for tumor development"},{"id":"sdl54_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 17-year-old male has a distal femoral osteosarcoma, and staging shows no metastases. His team plans chemotherapy before resection and again after surgery. Which of the following best explains why systemic chemotherapy is given even though no metastases were found?","choices":{"A":"Neoadjuvant chemotherapy is given mainly to shrink the tumor so the resection margin is cleaner, and it has no effect on overall survival","B":"Survival in osteosarcoma is set only by lung metastases visible at diagnosis, so chemotherapy is given just to slow local tumor growth","C":"Osteosarcomas are assumed to carry occult metastases at diagnosis, and chemotherapy around surgery has raised 5-year survival to 70%","D":"Osteosarcoma is intrinsically chemoresistant, so chemotherapy is given only to make the tumor sensitive to postoperative radiation","E":"Chemotherapy is used only to guide radiation planning and has no direct bearing on the regimen after surgery or on overall prognosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins Ch 26 (course reading for SDL 54–56): osteosarcomas \"are generally assumed to have occult metastases at the time of diagnosis\", so treatment typically consists of neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgery, and postoperative adjuvant chemotherapy. Chemotherapy has greatly improved prognosis, with 5-year survival reaching 70% in patients without overt metastases at diagnosis, while the outcome with clinically evident metastases, recurrent disease, or secondary osteosarcoma remains guarded. Chemotherapy therefore treats micrometastatic disease as well as the primary tumor: it is not only a margin or local-control measure, survival is not set only by visible lung metastases, and its role is not limited to radiation sensitization or radiation planning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteosarcoma — occult metastases are assumed at diagnosis, so treatment is neoadjuvant chemotherapy → resection → adjuvant chemotherapy; 5-year survival reaches about 70% without overt metastases (Robbins Ch 26). Spread is hematogenous to the lungs, bones and brain. COMLEX: Outcome stays guarded with clinically evident metastases, recurrence, or secondary osteosarcoma (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients","whyNot":{"A":"Chemotherapy therefore treats ","D":"Chemotherapy therefore treats ","E":"Chemotherapy therefore treats "}},{"id":"sdl54_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with a 15-year history of Paget's disease of the bone presents with a sudden worsening of his previously well-controlled thigh pain. X-ray shows a new lytic destructive lesion arising within a known pagetic focus of the proximal femur, with cortical breakthrough and an aggressive periosteal reaction. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of this secondary malignancy?","choices":{"A":"Bisphosphonate therapy used to treat Paget's disease directly causes osteosarcoma by inhibiting osteoblast apoptosis and promoting malignant transformation over time","B":"Secondary osteosarcoma in Paget's disease is caused by measles virus reactivation within osteoclasts, which then infects adjacent osteoblastic progenitors and initiates malignant change","C":"The osteosclerotic phase of Paget's disease physically compresses medullary stromal cells, causing ischemic necrosis followed by malignant repair in a process analogous to post-radiation sarcoma","D":"Chronically disordered bone remodeling in Paget's disease drives genomic instability in osteoblastic precursors, predisposing to secondary osteosarcoma development within pagetic bone","E":"Elevated alkaline phosphatase in Paget's disease directly acts as a mutagen on osteoblast DNA, with the risk of secondary osteosarcoma proportional to the alkaline phosphatase level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary osteosarcoma arising in pagetic bone is the most feared complication of Paget's disease, occurring in less than 1% of affected patients overall but in 5% to 10% of those with severe polyostotic disease (Robbins Ch 26). The pathophysiology involves chronically disordered bone remodeling — rapid alternating cycles of osteoclast-driven resorption and haphazard osteoblastic repair — which creates a microenvironment of ongoing genomic instability in osteoblastic precursors. Secondary osteosarcoma in Paget's disease has an extremely poor prognosis, being rapidly fatal (Robbins Ch 26), compared to approximately 70% five-year survival in primary osteosarcoma without metastases. Other secondary osteosarcoma risk factors include prior radiation exposure and bone infarcts. Bisphosphonate therapy is protective, not causative.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary osteosarcoma in Paget's disease — under 1% overall (5-10% with severe polyostotic disease), second smaller age peak in older adults, rapidly fatal. Other secondary causes: prior radiation, bone infarcts. Alkaline phosphatase is elevated in Paget's but is not directly mutagenic. COMLEX: Elderly patient with Paget's and sudden worsening of bone pain = rule out secondary osteosarcoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients"},{"id":"sdl54_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pathologic changes occurring with osteosarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with osteosarcoma of the proximal humerus undergoes staging CT. Imaging reveals two new pulmonary nodules consistent with metastatic disease. Which of the following best describes why pulmonary metastasis is specifically the predominant distant spread pattern in osteosarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma cells spread exclusively through lymphatic channels that drain the periosteal tissue and converge on hilar lymph nodes before seeding the lung parenchyma","B":"Osteosarcoma cells preferentially invade cortical Haversian venous channels and enter the systemic circulation, with the pulmonary capillary bed serving as the first vascular filter they encounter","C":"Osteosarcoma preferentially metastasizes to the lungs because CXCR4 chemokine receptor expression on tumor cells responds to CXCL12 gradients produced selectively by pulmonary endothelium","D":"Pulmonary metastasis in osteosarcoma results from retrograde venous flow through the inferior vena cava during Valsalva maneuvers, forcing tumor emboli into the pulmonary vasculature","E":"Osteosarcoma cells have a specific tropism for surfactant-producing type II pneumocytes, and this cell-to-cell adhesion interaction drives selective colonization of lung tissue over other organs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osteosarcoma metastasizes predominantly via hematogenous spread. The tumor invades cortical venous channels — Haversian system venous outflow — and enters the systemic circulation. Because the venous drainage of the extremities flows through the systemic veins to the right heart and then to the pulmonary circulation, the lungs are the first capillary bed encountered and trap tumor emboli. This explains the characteristic early pulmonary metastasis pattern. At diagnosis, some patients already have detectable lung metastases; occult micrometastases are present in most patients, which is why systemic chemotherapy is integral to treatment. Lymph node involvement is uncommon in osteosarcoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteosarcoma metastasizes HEMATOGENOUSLY — lungs first, because venous drainage of extremities passes through the pulmonary circulation. NOT lymphatic spread (unlike carcinomas). Presence of lung mets at diagnosis = <20% 5-year survival. COMLEX: Staging CT chest is mandatory in all osteosarcoma patients at diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 7 — Paget's disease of the bone as a differential for osteosarcoma in older patients","whyNot":{"A":"Lymph node involvement is unco"}}],"55":[{"id":"sdl55_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In bone remodeling, which molecular interaction directly initiates osteoclast precursor differentiation into mature osteoclasts?","choices":{"A":"OPG secreted by osteoblasts binds to RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and driving osteoclastogenesis","B":"Sclerostin produced by osteocytes binds to LRP5 and LRP6 on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and initiating osteoclast differentiation","C":"M-CSF produced by osteoclasts binds to RANKL on osteoblast precursors, activating beta-catenin signaling and promoting new bone matrix synthesis","D":"Wnt proteins secreted by osteoprogenitor cells bind to RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and driving osteoclastogenesis","E":"RANKL expressed on osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells binds to RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and driving osteoclastogenesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"RANKL is expressed on osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells and binds to its receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. This RANK signaling activates NF-kB, which is essential for osteoclast generation and survival. OPG is a decoy receptor also made by osteoblasts that binds RANKL and prevents it from interacting with RANK, thereby inhibiting osteoclastogenesis. Sclerostin inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin in osteoblasts. M-CSF from osteoblasts is also essential for osteoclast generation but acts through a separate receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANKL on osteoblasts/stromal cells → RANK on osteoclast precursors → NF-kB → osteoclast differentiation. OPG = decoy receptor made by osteoblasts → blocks RANKL → inhibits osteoclastogenesis. Denosumab mimics OPG (anti-RANKL antibody). COMLEX: RANK/RANKL/OPG axis is the molecular target of several rheumatologic and osteoporosis therapies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG are the molecular levers of BMU homeostasis and the pharmacologic targets in metabolic bone disease","whyNot":{"B":"Sclerostin inhibits Wnt/beta-c"}},{"id":"sdl55_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cell type produces sclerostin, and what is its effect on bone remodeling?","choices":{"A":"Osteocytes produce sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts, thereby reducing bone formation","B":"Osteoclasts produce sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts, thereby reducing bone formation","C":"Osteoblasts produce sclerostin, which activates RANKL expression on marrow stromal cells, thereby increasing osteoclast recruitment and bone resorption","D":"Marrow stromal cells produce sclerostin, which activates NF-kB in osteoclast precursors, thereby promoting osteoclast differentiation and bone turnover","E":"T lymphocytes produce sclerostin, which activates OPG expression on osteoblasts, thereby inhibiting osteoclastogenesis and promoting bone formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteocytes, the most abundant bone cells embedded within the mineralized matrix, produce sclerostin. Sclerostin binds to LRP5 and LRP6 co-receptors on osteoblasts and inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling, reducing osteoblast activity and bone formation. Romosozumab, an anti-sclerostin monoclonal antibody, is approved for osteoporosis treatment and works by removing this inhibitory brake on Wnt signaling, allowing increased bone formation. Osteoblasts produce OPG and RANKL; osteoclasts are derived from monocyte precursors and produce bone resorption enzymes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sclerostin = osteocyte-derived Wnt inhibitor → reduces osteoblast activity. Romosozumab = anti-sclerostin antibody → removes Wnt inhibition → anabolic effect. LRP5 loss-of-function mutations = osteoporosis; LRP5 gain-of-function = high bone density. COMLEX: Sclerostin connects mechanical load sensing (osteocytes) to bone formation regulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG are the molecular levers of BMU homeostasis and the pharmacologic targets in metabolic bone disease","whyNot":{"B":"Osteoblasts produce OPG and RA"}},{"id":"sdl55_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Peak bone mass is achieved in early adulthood. After the fourth decade, bone resorption slightly exceeds formation, resulting in an average skeletal mass decline of what percentage per year?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 5% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade, affecting women preferentially due to estrogen-independent osteoclast hyperactivity","B":"Approximately 0.1% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade, an amount insufficient to cause clinically significant osteoporosis without additional risk factors","C":"Approximately 2% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade in both sexes due to decreased vitamin D production in aged skin and reduced intestinal absorption","D":"Approximately 0.7% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade, affecting both sexes equally, independent of menopausal-related bone loss","E":"Approximately 10% skeletal mass is lost per year after the fourth decade, explaining why most adults develop fractures by the seventh decade without pharmacologic intervention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Once maximal skeletal mass is attained in early adulthood, bone resorption slightly exceeds formation at a rate of approximately 0.7% per year. This age-related bone loss affects both sexes equally and contributes to senile osteoporosis, which is a low-turnover form driven by reduced osteoblast proliferative and biosynthetic capacity. This is distinct from postmenopausal osteoporosis, which is a high-turnover form driven by estrogen deficiency and can cause up to 2% cortical bone and 9% cancellous bone loss per year in the first decade after menopause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age-related bone loss = 0.7%/year, both sexes, senile osteoporosis = LOW-turnover. Postmenopausal osteoporosis = HIGH-turnover, up to 2% cortical/9% cancellous/year in first decade. Peak bone mass factors: VDR polymorphisms, nutrition, physical activity, hormonal state. COMLEX: Resistance training > endurance exercise for building bone mass (load magnitude > load cycles).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG are the molecular levers of BMU homeostasis and the pharmacologic targets in metabolic bone disease"},{"id":"sdl55_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following sarcomas is best classified as a simple karyotype tumor, and what is its defining molecular feature?","choices":{"A":"Leiomyosarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by a single recurrent chromosomal translocation producing a chimeric fusion protein that drives constitutive growth factor signaling","B":"Undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by a limited number of recurrent chromosomal aberrations that can serve as diagnostic molecular markers","C":"Synovial sarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by a t(X;18) translocation producing SS18::SSX1 or SS18::SSX2 fusion proteins that dysregulate chromatin remodeling complexes","D":"Pleomorphic liposarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by MDM2 gene amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15 that drives proteasomal degradation of p53","E":"Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by a consistent t(2;13) translocation producing a PAX3::FOXO1 chimeric transcription factor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Simple karyotype sarcomas comprise approximately 20% of sarcomas and are characterized by euploid genomes with a single or limited number of recurrent chromosomal aberrations that are diagnostically specific. Synovial sarcoma carries t(X;18), producing SS18::SSX1, SS18::SSX2, or SS18::SSX4 fusion proteins that dysregulate chromatin remodeling. Other examples include Ewing sarcoma (EWSR1::FLI1), myxoid liposarcoma (FUS::DDIT3), and alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma (PAX3/7::FOXO1). These tumors are more common in younger patients with less pleomorphism. Leiomyosarcoma, undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma, and pleomorphic liposarcoma are complex karyotype tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simple karyotype (~20%) = euploid, single recurrent translocation, diagnostic marker, younger patients, less pleomorphic. Examples: Ewing t(11;22) EWSR1::FLI1, synovial sarcoma t(X;18) SS18::SSX, myxoid liposarcoma t(12;16) FUS::DDIT3, alveolar RMS PAX3::FOXO1. Complex karyotype (~80%) = aneuploid, multiple aberrations, adults, pleomorphic. COMLEX: Translocation-positive sarcoma in a teenager = simple karyotype.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 2 — Osteosarcoma exemplifies complex karyotype genomic instability with MDM2 and CDK4 amplification as parallel driver logic","whyNot":{"D":"Leiomyosarcoma, undifferentiat"}},{"id":"sdl55_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has a retroperitoneal mass. Biopsy reveals pleomorphic, anaplastic cells with numerous chromosomal gains and losses on karyotype analysis and no consistent chromosomal translocation. This tumor is best classified as which category, and what examples share this genetic profile?","choices":{"A":"Simple karyotype sarcoma, characterized by a single diagnostically specific fusion gene that acts as an oncogenic chimeric protein and serves as a biomarker for targeted therapy","B":"Reactive fibroblastic proliferation with clonal cytogenetic changes, as aneuploidy in soft tissue lesions is pathognomonic of self-limited processes such as nodular fasciitis","C":"Simple karyotype sarcoma, characterized by a single EWSR1 rearrangement that represents the most common recurrent translocation across sarcoma subtypes in middle-aged adults","D":"Hematologic malignancy with secondary bone marrow extension, as complex karyotype patterns are characteristic of leukemias and lymphomas rather than sarcomas of soft tissue","E":"Complex karyotype sarcoma, characterized by aneuploidy and multiple chromosomal aberrations from genomic instability; leiomyosarcoma and undifferentiated sarcoma are examples"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Complex karyotype sarcomas comprise approximately 80% of all sarcomas and are characterized by aneuploidy or polyploidy with multiple chromosomal gains and losses that reflect underlying genomic instability. Prototypic examples include leiomyosarcoma and undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma, which are both more common in adults and tend to be histologically pleomorphic. Pleomorphic liposarcoma is another example. Because there is no single diagnostic fusion protein, targeted therapy based on molecular markers is not available for most complex karyotype sarcomas. Simple karyotype sarcomas are more common in younger patients and carry specific recurrent translocations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complex karyotype (~80%) = aneuploid/polyploid, multiple gains/losses, genomic instability, adults, pleomorphic, no targeted therapy based on fusion gene. Leiomyosarcoma and undifferentiated soft tissue sarcoma are classic examples. Also: pleomorphic liposarcoma. Nodular fasciitis = clonal but self-limited (MYH9::USP6). COMLEX: Pleomorphic sarcoma in an older adult without recurrent translocation = complex karyotype.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 2 — Osteosarcoma exemplifies complex karyotype genomic instability with MDM2 and CDK4 amplification as parallel driver logic"},{"id":"sdl55_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion gene produced by t(12;16) translocation in myxoid liposarcoma encodes a chimeric DNA-binding protein. How does this oncoprotein drive tumorigenesis?","choices":{"A":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein arrests adipocyte differentiation by interfering with normal transcriptional programming, causing proliferation of primitive undifferentiated mesenchymal cells","B":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein constitutively activates the RAS/MAPK signaling cascade, driving cell cycle progression through unrestrained mitogenic signaling in committed adipocyte progenitors","C":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein promotes proteasomal degradation of p53 by binding to MDM2, thereby eliminating the primary checkpoint that prevents malignant transformation","D":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein activates NF-kB-dependent transcription of RANKL, creating an autocrine loop that sustains osteoclast-like resorptive activity within the tumor microenvironment","E":"The FUS::DDIT3 fusion protein amplifies the Wnt/beta-catenin pathway by inhibiting APC-mediated destruction complex activity, mimicking the mechanism seen in desmoid tumors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Myxoid liposarcoma is a simple karyotype sarcoma defined by t(12;16) producing the FUS::DDIT3 fusion gene (older terminology: TLS::CHOP). The chimeric DNA-binding protein arrests adipocyte differentiation through uncertain mechanisms, leading to proliferation of primitive mesenchymal cells at various early stages of adipocytic differentiation. This represents a common oncogenic mechanism in simple karyotype sarcomas where fusion proteins disrupt normal differentiation programs. Histologically, myxoid liposarcoma shows abundant basophilic extracellular matrix, arborizing capillaries, and primitive cells resembling fetal fat. MDM2 amplification is the mechanism in well-differentiated liposarcoma, not myxoid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myxoid liposarcoma = t(12;16), FUS::DDIT3, arrests adipocyte differentiation, myxoid matrix with \"chicken wire\" capillaries, thigh/leg, 30s, intermediate prognosis, unusual bone mets. Well-differentiated liposarcoma = MDM2 amplification at chr 12q13-q15. Pleomorphic liposarcoma = complex karyotype. COMLEX: Myxoid liposarcoma is unique among sarcomas for its predilection to metastasize to bone and other soft tissues rather than lung.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 2 — Osteosarcoma exemplifies complex karyotype genomic instability with MDM2 and CDK4 amplification as parallel driver logic","whyNot":{"C":"MDM2 amplification is the mech"}},{"id":"sdl55_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following soft tissue tumors is correctly matched with both its benign or malignant classification and its characteristic molecular or genetic finding?","choices":{"A":"Deep fibromatosis is a malignant mesenchymal tumor associated with FUS::DDIT3 fusion from t(12;16), conferring high rates of distant metastasis to the retroperitoneum and lungs","B":"Lipoma is a malignant adipose tumor associated with MDM2 amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15, causing p53 degradation and constitutive proliferation of mature adipocytes","C":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma is a benign skeletal muscle tumor associated with PAX3::FOXO1 fusion from t(2;13), found incidentally in the sinuses and genitourinary tract of children","D":"Leiomyosarcoma is a benign smooth muscle tumor associated with fumarate hydratase loss-of-function, which drives Krebs cycle disruption and metabolic transformation","E":"Nodular fasciitis is a benign clonal fibroblastic proliferation associated with a MYH9::USP6 fusion gene from t(17;22), which is self-limiting and may resolve spontaneously"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nodular fasciitis is a self-limited fibroblastic and myofibroblastic proliferation in young adults that harbors a t(17;22) translocation producing MYH9::USP6 fusion gene, establishing it as a clonal but self-limited neoplasm rather than a reactive process. It often regresses spontaneously. Deep fibromatosis is locally aggressive but does NOT metastasize and carries APC or CTNNB1 mutations. Lipoma is benign with HMGA2 rearrangements; MDM2 amplification defines well-differentiated liposarcoma. Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 is malignant. Leiomyosarcoma is malignant with complex karyotype; fumarate hydratase loss causes hereditary leiomyomas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nodular fasciitis = clonal (MYH9::USP6), self-limited, NOT reactive, young adults, forearm/chest/back, tissue culture pattern, mitoses without atypia, spontaneous regression. Deep fibromatosis = locally aggressive, NO mets, APC/CTNNB1, Gardner syndrome. COMLEX: Rapidly growing forearm mass in a 25-year-old with prior trauma history = nodular fasciitis (do not misdiagnose as sarcoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Benign versus malignant classification principles and bone location rules apply symmetrically to soft tissue tumors","whyNot":{"A":"Deep fibromatosis is locally a","B":"Lipoma is benign with HMGA2 re"}},{"id":"sdl55_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Liposarcoma is the most common sarcoma of adulthood. Which subtype is associated with MDM2 gene amplification and why does its location — extremity versus retroperitoneum — determine the clinical designation used?","choices":{"A":"Well-differentiated liposarcoma harbors MDM2 amplification at 12q13-q15; extremity tumors are called atypical lipomatous tumors because they behave indolently, while retroperitoneal tumors keep the liposarcoma name because of recurrence","B":"Myxoid liposarcoma harbors MDM2 amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15; extremity tumors are called atypical lipomatous tumors because MDM2 expression is suppressed in superficial locations by skin-derived factors","C":"Pleomorphic liposarcoma harbors MDM2 amplification at chromosome 12q13-q15; extremity tumors are called atypical lipomatous tumors because the complex karyotype is only expressed in deep retroperitoneal tumors","D":"Well-differentiated liposarcoma harbors FUS::DDIT3 from t(12;16); extremity tumors are called atypical lipomatous tumors due to indolent behavior, while retroperitoneal tumors retain the liposarcoma designation due to chemotherapy resistance","E":"Myxoid liposarcoma harbors FUS::DDIT3 from t(12;16); extremity tumors are labeled atypical lipomatous tumors because these arise from brown fat precursors that have a lower malignant potential than white fat precursors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Well-differentiated liposarcoma (WDLS) harbors amplification of the MDM2 gene at chromosome 12q13-q15. MDM2 is an oncoprotein that promotes p53 degradation. The same tumor is designated atypical lipomatous tumor (ALT) when it arises in the extremity, where complete surgical excision is feasible and behavior is indolent. In the retroperitoneum, where complete resection is often impossible, the WDLS designation is used because these tumors frequently recur locally and cause significant morbidity. This illustrates how tumor location influences not just treatment but nomenclature. Myxoid liposarcoma carries FUS::DDIT3 from t(12;16).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Well-differentiated liposarcoma = MDM2 amplification (12q13-q15), atypical spindle cells among mature adipocytes. Extremity = called ALT (indolent). Retroperitoneum = called WDLS (recurs, significant morbidity). Myxoid liposarcoma = FUS::DDIT3 t(12;16), chicken wire vessels, thigh, bone mets. Pleomorphic = complex karyotype, aggressive. COMLEX: Retroperitoneal adipose mass in 60-year-old = liposarcoma until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Benign versus malignant classification principles and bone location rules apply symmetrically to soft tissue tumors","whyNot":{"B":"Myxoid liposarcoma carries FUS","D":"Myxoid liposarcoma carries FUS"}},{"id":"sdl55_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood. The alveolar subtype carries PAX3::FOXO1 or PAX7::FOXO1 fusion genes. How do these fusion proteins drive malignant transformation?","choices":{"A":"PAX3 is a cell surface receptor for FGF; the PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein constitutively activates RAS/MAPK signaling independently of ligand binding, driving unrestrained mitogenic proliferation","B":"PAX3 is a transcription factor that normally initiates skeletal muscle differentiation; the PAX3::FOXO1 chimera interferes with this program, arresting cells in a proliferative undifferentiated state","C":"PAX3 is a tumor suppressor; the PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein acts as a dominant negative that silences PAX3 target genes through promoter hypermethylation, resulting in loss of cell cycle control","D":"PAX3 is a pro-apoptotic transcription factor; the PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein sequesters p53 in the cytoplasm, preventing its nuclear translocation and enabling evasion of apoptotic checkpoints","E":"PAX3 is a chromatin remodeling enzyme; the PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein dysregulates histone H3 methylation, creating bivalent chromatin domains that maintain the tumor in an epigenetically plastic state"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PAX3 is a transcription factor that initiates and regulates skeletal muscle differentiation. In alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma, the t(2;13) translocation produces a PAX3::FOXO1 chimeric fusion protein that interferes with normal myogenic differentiation, arresting cells in an undifferentiated, proliferative state. This is mechanistically analogous to transcription factor fusions in acute leukemias that block myeloid differentiation. The t(1;13) variant produces PAX7::FOXO1. Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma is more aggressive than the embryonal subtype. These tumors occur in sites that normally lack skeletal muscle (sinuses, genitourinary tract), underscoring that sarcomas arise from mesenchymal stem cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar RMS = PAX3::FOXO1 (t(2;13)) or PAX7::FOXO1 (t(1;13)), 5-15 years, dyscohesive round cells in alveolar pattern, more aggressive. Embryonal RMS = RAS pathway, 1-5 years, genitourinary tract, best prognosis variant = botryoid. Pleomorphic RMS = adults, complex karyotype. All require immunohistochemistry for myogenin to confirm skeletal muscle lineage. COMLEX: Bladder/vaginal mass in a toddler = embryonal RMS (sarcoma botryoides).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Benign versus malignant classification principles and bone location rules apply symmetrically to soft tissue tumors"},{"id":"sdl55_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman taking long-term prednisone for lupus presents with back pain. DEXA scan shows T-score of -2.8 at the lumbar spine. Her provider explains that glucocorticoids contribute to bone loss through multiple mechanisms. Which of the following best describes the primary skeletal mechanism by which glucocorticoids cause osteoporosis?","choices":{"A":"Glucocorticoids directly activate osteoclast differentiation by binding to RANK receptors on osteoclast precursors, bypassing the need for osteoblast-derived RANKL as an intermediate signal","B":"Glucocorticoids cause osteoporosis exclusively by increasing urinary calcium excretion, resulting in secondary hyperparathyroidism that drives osteoclast-mediated bone resorption","C":"Glucocorticoids accelerate osteocyte apoptosis, releasing sclerostin that activates Wnt/beta-catenin in osteoclast precursors and causes paradoxical stimulation of bone resorption","D":"Glucocorticoids inhibit vitamin D activation in the kidney by suppressing 1-alpha hydroxylase, creating a functional vitamin D deficiency that reduces calcium absorption from the gut","E":"Glucocorticoids increase RANKL expression on osteoblasts and suppress OPG, thereby tilting the RANK-to-OPG ratio toward bone resorption while also directly inhibiting osteoblast function and new bone formation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Glucocorticoids cause osteoporosis through multiple converging mechanisms. They increase RANKL expression on osteoblasts and decrease OPG production, increasing the RANK-to-OPG ratio and promoting osteoclastogenesis. Simultaneously, glucocorticoids directly suppress osteoblast proliferation and function, reducing bone matrix synthesis. They also antagonize vitamin D-stimulated intestinal calcium transport and stimulate renal calcium excretion, contributing to secondary hyperparathyroidism. Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis disproportionately affects cancellous bone of vertebral bodies and can cause fractures even at relatively preserved T-scores (fracture threshold is higher than in postmenopausal osteoporosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucocorticoids → increase RANKL, decrease OPG → osteoclastogenesis + directly inhibit osteoblasts. Affects cancellous bone (vertebrae) most. Fracture risk is higher at a given T-score than in postmenopausal osteoporosis. Bisphosphonates are first-line for glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis prevention. COMLEX: Any patient on chronic corticosteroids should be co-prescribed calcium, vitamin D, and a bisphosphonate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG as the molecular effectors linking hormonal and drug-induced bone loss"},{"id":"sdl55_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old male weightlifter has significantly higher bone density than age-matched sedentary controls. A 70-year-old man with paraplegia has severely reduced bone density in his lower extremities. Which cell type senses mechanical load and transduces signals to modulate osteoblast and osteoclast activity to explain both observations?","choices":{"A":"Osteoclasts are the primary mechanosensors; high mechanical load suppresses their bone resorption activity via direct cytoskeletal deformation, while immobilization removes this suppression and allows unchecked resorption","B":"Periosteal fibroblasts are the primary mechanosensors; they convert mechanical strain into prostaglandin synthesis that then activates osteoblast differentiation from marrow stromal cell precursors","C":"Osteoblasts are the primary mechanosensors; they directly increase matrix synthesis in response to compressive loads through integrin-mediated cytoskeletal tension without requiring any intermediary cell signaling","D":"Osteocytes are the mechanosensing cells of bone; they modulate osteoblast and osteoclast activity under load through sclerostin and RANKL, explaining denser bone with training and disuse osteoporosis","E":"Chondrocytes are the primary skeletal mechanosensors; they release nitric oxide in response to joint loading, which then diffuses into subchondral bone to regulate osteoblast and osteoclast activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Osteocytes — the most abundant bone cells, embedded within the mineralized matrix in lacunar spaces connected by canalicular networks — are the principal mechanosensors of bone. They respond to mechanical load by modulating sclerostin production (decreased under load, removing the Wnt inhibitory brake) and RANKL expression (adjusting osteoclast activity). Increased mechanical load (resistance training) reduces sclerostin → more Wnt/beta-catenin → more bone formation. Immobilization or microgravity increases sclerostin and promotes bone loss. The type of exercise matters: load magnitude (weight training) is more effective than load cycles (cycling) for increasing bone mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteocytes = mechanosensors. Mechanical load → decreased sclerostin → increased Wnt → bone formation. Immobilization → increased sclerostin → decreased bone formation + increased RANKL → bone loss. Resistance training > endurance exercise for bone health. COMLEX: Disuse osteoporosis (paralysis, bed rest, microgravity) is mediated by osteocyte mechanosensing → increased sclerostin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG as the molecular effectors linking mechanical and hormonal signals to bone cell activity"},{"id":"sdl55_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the homeostasis and remodeling of bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with multiple myeloma develops severe bone pain and lytic lesions throughout the axial skeleton. His oncologist explains that myeloma cells directly dysregulate the RANK/RANKL/OPG axis. Which of the following best explains the molecular mechanism by which multiple myeloma causes osteolytic bone destruction?","choices":{"A":"Myeloma cells directly differentiate into osteoclasts by expressing RANK on their surface, allowing them to perform autonomous bone resorption without requiring any osteoblast-derived RANKL signal","B":"Myeloma cells secrete factors that increase RANKL expression on marrow stromal cells and suppress OPG production, raising the RANKL-to-OPG ratio and driving osteoclast-mediated lytic bone lesions","C":"Myeloma cells suppress Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts through secretion of DKK-1, which directly activates RANK on osteoclast precursors and causes osteolysis independent of RANKL","D":"Myeloma cells secrete M-CSF that constitutively activates osteoclast formation, but the primary mechanism of bone destruction is direct tumor invasion rather than osteoclast-mediated resorption","E":"Myeloma cells activate the sclerostin pathway in osteocytes, which then suppresses both osteoblast formation and OPG production, resulting in secondary hyperparathyroidism that drives osteolysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multiple myeloma causes osteolytic bone disease primarily by dysregulating the RANKL/OPG axis. Myeloma cells and their stromal cell neighbors increase RANKL expression while suppressing OPG, dramatically increasing the RANK-to-OPG ratio and promoting osteoclastogenesis. Additionally, myeloma cells secrete DKK-1, which inhibits Wnt/beta-catenin signaling in osteoblasts, suppressing new bone formation and creating an uncoupling of resorption and formation (pure osteolysis without repair). Denosumab and bisphosphonates are used to mitigate myeloma bone disease by targeting the RANKL pathway. Myeloma bone lesions characteristically do not show increased uptake on bone scan because there is minimal osteoblastic activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myeloma bone disease = increased RANKL + decreased OPG + DKK-1 (inhibits Wnt/osteoblasts) = pure osteolysis with no repair. Bone scan NEGATIVE (no osteoblastic activity). Bisphosphonates/denosumab for bone protection. COMLEX: Lytic lesions + negative bone scan + plasma cell dyscrasia = multiple myeloma (contrast with metastatic prostate = sclerotic lesions, hot bone scan).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG as the molecular effectors of osteoclast activation in metabolic and neoplastic bone disease"},{"id":"sdl55_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy presents with a periorbital mass. MRI shows a lesion in the soft tissues adjacent to the orbit. Biopsy reveals uniform small round cells growing in an alveolar pattern, with fibrous septa creating aggregates and dyscohesive cells in the center. Cytogenetics shows t(2;13)(q35;q14). Which of the following best characterizes the prognosis and the molecular basis for this tumor's behavior relative to the embryonal subtype?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 has a more favorable prognosis than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma because the translocation allows precise targeted therapy that completely eliminates the tumor stem cell population","B":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 has an identical prognosis to embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma because the RAS pathway activated in embryonal disease is equally aggressive as the PAX3::FOXO1 transcription factor","C":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 has a more favorable prognosis than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma because the simple karyotype produces a more predictable tumor phenotype that is easier to stage and treat surgically","D":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 is more aggressive than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma; the chimeric transcription factor drives a highly active gene expression program that promotes invasion and resistance to differentiation-inducing therapy","E":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 only occurs in adults, while embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is the subtype seen in children under age 10, making the comparison between their prognoses clinically irrelevant for this patient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma with PAX3::FOXO1 (t(2;13)) or PAX7::FOXO1 (t(1;13)) is more aggressive than embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma. The PAX3::FOXO1 fusion protein functions as a potent transcriptional activator that drives invasion, metastasis, and resistance to apoptosis, and is associated with a worse prognosis. Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma harbors RAS pathway mutations and has a better overall prognosis, with the botryoid variant having the most favorable outlook. Both subtypes occur in children and adolescents. Treatment involves surgery, chemotherapy, and often radiation. The presence of a specific fusion gene (simple karyotype) does not inherently confer favorable prognosis — it confers diagnostic specificity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar RMS (PAX3::FOXO1) = WORSE prognosis than embryonal RMS (RAS mutations). Botryoid variant of embryonal = BEST prognosis among all subtypes. Pleomorphic RMS = adults = WORST prognosis. Both alveolar and embryonal occur in children. COMLEX: Head/neck or genitourinary soft tissue tumor in a child with round cells = rhabdomyosarcoma; t(2;13) = alveolar subtype = aggressive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 2 — Fusion protein-driven oncogenesis mirrors the simple karyotype mechanism in osteosarcoma driver mutations","whyNot":{"A":"The presence of a specific fus","E":"Both subtypes occur in childre"}},{"id":"sdl55_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Deep fibromatosis (desmoid tumor) harbors mutations in CTNNB1 or germline APC mutations. A 28-year-old woman with a large anterior abdominal wall mass is found to have a germline APC mutation. What syndrome does she have, and through what molecular mechanism do APC mutations lead to desmoid formation?","choices":{"A":"Gardner syndrome; loss of APC function prevents destruction of beta-catenin, leading to constitutive Wnt/beta-catenin signaling that drives fibroblast proliferation and desmoid tumor formation","B":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome; loss of APC function disrupts the spindle assembly checkpoint and causes chromosomal instability that drives fibroblast transformation into locally aggressive desmoid tumors","C":"Neurofibromatosis type 1; loss of APC function disrupts Ras GTPase activity by preventing NF1-mediated GAP regulation, leading to constitutive Ras/MAPK signaling in abdominal wall fibroblasts","D":"Lynch syndrome; loss of APC function impairs DNA mismatch repair in fibroblasts, creating microsatellite instability that accumulates secondary driver mutations leading to desmoid formation","E":"Weber-Osler-Rendu syndrome; loss of APC function disrupts TGF-beta receptor signaling in mesenchymal cells, causing unregulated fibroblastic proliferation in the mesentery and abdominal wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gardner syndrome is caused by germline APC mutations and is characterized by familial adenomatous polyposis (colon) plus extraintestinal manifestations including desmoid tumors, osteomas, and epidermoid cysts. APC is a key component of the beta-catenin destruction complex. Loss of APC prevents the phosphorylation and proteasomal degradation of beta-catenin, allowing it to accumulate and translocate to the nucleus, where it activates Wnt target genes that drive fibroblast proliferation. Sporadic desmoid tumors carry somatic CTNNB1 mutations that directly stabilize beta-catenin by preventing its phosphorylation. Both mutations converge on constitutive Wnt/beta-catenin activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Desmoid tumor (deep fibromatosis) = locally aggressive, NO metastasis, APC or CTNNB1 mutations, Wnt/beta-catenin pathway. Gardner syndrome = germline APC + FAP + desmoids + osteomas + epidermoid cysts. Gamma-secretase inhibitors slow progression. COMLEX: Young woman with abdominal wall fibromatosis + colon polyps history = Gardner syndrome; check for germline APC.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 2 — Tumor suppressor gene loss as a parallel driver mechanism to fusion protein oncogenesis in soft tissue tumors"},{"id":"sdl55_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the conceptual difference between Simple karyotype and Complex karyotype sarcomas.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pathologist orders FISH for a specific translocation on a monomorphic spindle cell sarcoma from a 30-year-old, but not on a highly pleomorphic sarcoma from a 75-year-old. Which concept about sarcoma genetics best justifies this approach?","choices":{"A":"Complex karyotype sarcomas carry one recurrent fusion gene that defines each tumor type","B":"Simple karyotype sarcomas are mostly pleomorphic tumors of older adults with poor prognosis","C":"Simple karyotype sarcomas carry reproducible chromosomal changes that have diagnostic utility","D":"Complex karyotype sarcomas are rare in adults, so testing them is not worth the cost","E":"Simple and complex karyotype sarcomas share the same translocations at different copy numbers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sarcomas with simple karyotypes have reproducible chromosomal and molecular abnormalities, such as recurrent translocations, that contribute to pathogenesis and are useful for diagnosis, so targeted testing such as FISH can confirm the tumor type. Most adult sarcomas are genetically heterogeneous with complex karyotypes, histologically pleomorphic, and have a poor prognosis; they lack a single defining fusion gene, so a translocation test adds little. Complex karyotype sarcomas are the common adult type, not rare, and they do not share the translocations of simple karyotype tumors. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, soft tissue tumors key concepts; leiomyosarcoma complex karyotype).","boardPrep":"Step 1: simple karyotype examples include synovial sarcoma with SS18::SSX and myxoid liposarcoma with FUS::DDIT3; leiomyosarcoma is a complex karyotype tumor (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: retroperitoneal leiomyosarcomas are often large, unresectable, and fatal (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 3 — benign versus malignant soft tissue tumors","whyNot":{"D":"Complex karyotype sarcomas are"}},{"id":"sdl55_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman presents with a large, painful retroperitoneal mass causing abdominal fullness. CT shows a heterogeneous mass with areas of myxoid change. Biopsy shows eosinophilic spindle cells with blunt-ended hyperchromatic nuclei in interweaving fascicles, with brisk mitotic activity. Immunostains are positive for smooth muscle actin and desmin. Which of the following best characterizes this tumor's prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma has an excellent prognosis with surgery alone because smooth muscle tumors respond well to complete excision and almost never metastasize from the retroperitoneal location","B":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma has an intermediate prognosis similar to well-differentiated liposarcoma because both tumors arise in the retroperitoneum and share similar molecular driver mechanisms","C":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma is classified as a benign leiomyoma variant because the retroperitoneum is a protected anatomical compartment that inhibits malignant transformation of smooth muscle cells","D":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma has a favorable prognosis when treated with neoadjuvant chemotherapy plus radiation, with 5-year survival rates comparable to extremity-based leiomyosarcomas","E":"Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma is usually large and unresectable at presentation and is frequently fatal due to local extension and distant metastatic spread, particularly to the lungs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leiomyosarcoma is a malignant smooth muscle tumor most commonly arising in the deep soft tissues of the extremities and retroperitoneum of older adults, with a female predominance. Retroperitoneal leiomyosarcomas are large and bulky, causing abdominal symptoms, and are frequently unresectable at presentation. They are often fatal due to local extension and distant metastases, particularly to the lungs. In contrast, superficial or cutaneous leiomyosarcomas are small and have a good prognosis. The histologic features — eosinophilic spindle cells in fascicles, brisk mitoses, blunt-ended nuclei — are characteristic, and smooth muscle actin, desmin, and caldesmon on immunostaining confirm diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leiomyosarcoma — malignant smooth muscle, retroperitoneum/thigh, complex karyotype, older adults, female predominance. Retroperitoneal = large, unresectable, fatal. Superficial = small, good prognosis. IHC: smooth muscle actin, desmin, caldesmon. Contrast with leiomyoma (benign, uterus, FH gene mutation in hereditary syndrome = Reed syndrome). COMLEX: Retroperitoneal spindle cell sarcoma with smooth muscle markers in middle-aged woman = leiomyosarcoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Location-dependent behavior parallels bone tumor principles: axial/retroperitoneal = poor prognosis, extremity = better prognosis"},{"id":"sdl55_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has had a slowly enlarging, firm, nodular thickening in the palm of his right hand for 3 years. The overlying skin is puckered and dimpled, and his ring finger now has a flexion contracture. Biopsy shows plump spindle cells in broad bundles and long sweeping fascicles surrounded by abundant dense collagen, without atypia. Which statement best characterizes this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Deep fibromatosis, a large infiltrative desmoid tumor that arises in the abdominal wall or limb girdles and often recurs after excision","B":"Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma, an anaplastic tumor of older adults with bizarre nuclei, atypical mitoses and necrosis","C":"Superficial fibromatosis, a benign fibroblastic lesion with an innocuous course; about half progress and the rest stabilize or regress","D":"Nodular fasciitis, a rapidly growing, self-limited clonal fibroblastic lesion of young adults that carries a MYH9::USP6 fusion","E":"Synovial sarcoma, a malignant tumor of young adults defined by an SS18::SSX fusion, with tight fascicles of uniform basophilic cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is Dupuytren contracture (palmar fibromatosis), a form of superficial fibromatosis. Superficial fibromatosis is a benign fibroblastic proliferation with an innocuous clinical course; its subtypes are Dupuytren contracture, Ledderhose disease (plantar) and Peyronie disease (penile). Dupuytren contracture is an irregular or nodular thickening of the palmar fascia whose incidence increases with age; attachment to the overlying skin causes puckering or dimpling and a slowly progressive flexion contracture. All three types are composed of plump spindle cells in broad bundles or long, sweeping fascicles surrounded by dense collagen. Palmar and plantar fibromatoses progress in about 50% of cases; the rest stabilize, and some resolve spontaneously. Deep fibromatosis (desmoid tumor) arises in the abdominal wall, limb girdles or mesentery, mostly in young people, and recurs often but does not metastasize. Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma shows anaplasia. Nodular fasciitis grows rapidly in young adults. Synovial sarcoma arises mainly in the thigh and leg of young adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Superficial fibromatoses (benign): Dupuytren (palmar), Ledderhose (plantar), Peyronie (penile). Deep fibromatosis (desmoid): infiltrative, recurs, does not metastasize; CTNNB1 or APC mutation (Gardner syndrome). COMLEX: Dupuytren contracture = palmar nodules, skin puckering and a slowly progressive finger flexion contracture, more common with age.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Benign versus malignant classification requires synthesis of location, age, histology, and molecular findings across soft tissue and bone tumors","whyNot":{"E":"Synovial sarcoma arises mainly"}},{"id":"sdl55_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between benign and malignant soft tissue tumors.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman presents with a painless, soft, mobile subcutaneous mass on her back that has been present for years. Excision reveals a well-encapsulated tumor of mature adipocytes that is histologically identical to normal fat. No atypia, mitoses, or lipoblasts are seen. Molecular testing reveals chromosomal rearrangement involving 12q with dysregulation of HMGA2. What is this tumor and what is its relationship to liposarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Well-differentiated liposarcoma — histologically identical to a lipoma in early stages; the presence of HMGA2 rearrangement rather than MDM2 amplification indicates the transition from benign lipoma to malignant liposarcoma has already occurred","B":"Lipoma, the most common soft tissue tumor in adults; histologically identical to normal fat and cured by excision, it lacks the atypical spindle cells of well-differentiated liposarcoma and has HMGA2 rather than MDM2 changes","C":"Atypical lipomatous tumor — a well-differentiated liposarcoma of the extremity; HMGA2 rearrangement is interchangeable with MDM2 amplification as a diagnostic marker distinguishing it from conventional lipoma","D":"Angiolipoma — a benign adipose tumor with a prominent vascular component; chromosomal rearrangements at 12q with HMGA2 dysregulation are pathognomonic for this vascular variant of lipoma","E":"Hibernoma — a benign tumor of brown fat that mimics the molecular profile of conventional lipoma; HMGA2 rearrangements are characteristic and distinguish it from the white fat origin of conventional lipoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lipoma is the most common soft tissue tumor in adults and is a benign tumor of mature adipocytes. Most solitary lipomas harbor chromosomal rearrangements involving 12q that dysregulate HMGA2, a chromatin structure regulator. This is distinct from well-differentiated liposarcoma, which harbors MDM2 gene amplification at 12q13-q15. The critical histologic distinction is that lipoma consists of entirely normal-appearing mature adipocytes without atypical spindle cells, lipoblasts, or atypia. Well-differentiated liposarcoma contains scattered atypical spindle cells among mature adipocytes. HMGA2 rearrangement in lipoma does not confer malignant potential; these are benign, cured by excision, and do not transform to liposarcoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lipoma = most common soft tissue tumor adults, HMGA2 rearrangement (12q), mature adipocytes, NO atypia, cured by excision, NO malignant potential. Well-differentiated liposarcoma = MDM2 amplification, atypical spindle cells admixed. KEY DISTINCTION: HMGA2 rearrangement in lipoma vs MDM2 amplification in liposarcoma. COMLEX: Painless mobile subcutaneous adipose mass in middle-aged adult = lipoma; simple excision curative.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Benign and malignant tumors of mesenchymal origin require molecular markers to distinguish histologically similar lesions","whyNot":{"A":"This is distinct from well-dif","C":"This is distinct from well-dif"}}],"56":[{"id":"sdl56_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Peak bone mass is reached in early adulthood after skeletal growth stops. Which inherited variation is named among the factors that determine this peak bone mass set point?","choices":{"A":"IGF-1 receptor gene polymorphisms that reduce growth hormone responsiveness in cortical bone osteoblasts during the pubertal growth spurt","B":"FGFR3 gain-of-function variants that slow chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate and shorten the long bones during skeletal growth","C":"RUNX2 transcription factor polymorphisms that reduce osteoblast lineage commitment from mesenchymal progenitors throughout skeletal development","D":"Fibrillin-1 gene polymorphisms that alter TGF-beta sequestration in bone matrix and reduce growth factor availability for osteoblast-driven bone formation","E":"Vitamin D receptor gene polymorphisms that alter intestinal calcium absorption efficiency and osteoblast responsiveness to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins (Ch 26) states that peak bone mass is achieved in early adulthood and that its set point is determined by several factors, including polymorphisms in the vitamin D receptor gene, nutrition and physical activity; muscle strength, diet and hormonal state also contribute. A separate point is often confused with this one: the genes most strongly linked to osteoporosis by genome-wide association studies are RANK, RANKL and OPG (key osteoclast regulators), the HLA locus and the estrogen receptor gene (Robbins Ch 26). FGFR3 gain-of-function mutations cause achondroplasia, a growth plate disorder, not variation in peak bone mass. The IGF-1 receptor, RUNX2 and fibrillin-1 options are not named among the determinants of peak bone mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peak bone mass is reached in early adulthood; the set point depends on vitamin D receptor polymorphisms, nutrition (calcium) and physical activity. After the peak, resorption slightly exceeds formation, giving about 0.7% bone loss per year (Robbins). GWAS osteoporosis genes: RANK, RANKL, OPG, HLA, estrogen receptor. COMLEX: Load magnitude matters more than the number of load cycles, so resistance training builds bone mass better than endurance exercise such as cycling (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and BMU remodeling cycles establish the skeletal set point that peak bone mass builds upon","whyNot":{"A":"The IGF-1 receptor, RUNX2 and ","C":"The IGF-1 receptor, RUNX2 and ","D":"The IGF-1 receptor, RUNX2 and "}},{"id":"sdl56_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"During puberty, sex steroids contribute significantly to peak bone mass accrual. What is the primary mechanism by which estrogen promotes net bone formation during skeletal growth?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen directly suppresses PTH gene transcription in parathyroid chief cells, reducing calcium mobilization from cortical bone during the pubertal growth phase","B":"Estrogen reduces osteoclast lifespan by promoting osteoclast apoptosis while enhancing osteoblast survival and activity, shifting the BMU balance toward net formation","C":"Estrogen stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity to increase circulating 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D levels and enhance intestinal calcium absorption for mineralization","D":"Estrogen inhibits sclerostin secretion from osteocytes, releasing the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts and driving periosteal bone apposition in long bones","E":"Estrogen upregulates calcitonin receptor expression on osteoclasts, amplifying calcitonin-mediated inhibition of acid secretion and preventing subperiosteal bone resorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Estrogen protects bone through multiple mechanisms. It directly reduces osteoclast lifespan by promoting osteoclast apoptosis and simultaneously increases OPG expression by osteoblasts, lowering the RANKL:OPG ratio and reducing osteoclastogenesis. Estrogen also promotes osteoblast survival. The net result is a shift toward bone formation during pubertal growth. Estrogen also closes the epiphyseal growth plates at the end of puberty — loss-of-function mutations in the estrogen receptor in males result in persistent growth plate activity and failure to close epiphyses, demonstrating the direct role of estrogen in both sexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen → increases OPG, decreases osteoclast lifespan, promotes osteoblast survival. Estrogen also closes epiphyseal plates — aromatase-deficient males have unfused epiphyses and osteopenia. Postmenopausal estrogen loss = high-turnover osteoporosis (increased osteoclasts). COMLEX: Estrogen deficiency is a named risk factor for osteoporosis on COMLEX Clinical Presentation 8.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 55 → Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and BMU remodeling cycles establish the skeletal set point that peak bone mass builds upon"},{"id":"sdl56_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Hereditary factors strongly influence peak bone mass, but single-gene defects explain few cases of osteoporosis. Genome-wide association studies have most strongly linked which group of genes to bone mass and osteoporosis?","choices":{"A":"FBN1, ELN, and TGFBR2, along with the lysyl oxidase gene","B":"FGFR3, SOX9, and RUNX2, along with the type X collagen gene","C":"HGPRT, PRPP synthetase, and xanthine oxidase, with URAT1","D":"RANK, RANKL, and OPG, with the HLA locus and estrogen receptor","E":"CFTR, HFE, and ATP7B, along with the transferrin receptor gene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peak bone mass is influenced by hereditary factors, especially polymorphisms in genes that affect bone metabolism. Single-gene defects account for only a small fraction of osteoporosis, but genome-wide association studies most strongly link RANK, RANKL, and OPG, which encode key osteoclast regulators, the HLA locus (for unknown reasons), and the estrogen receptor gene. FBN1, ELN, and TGFBR2 are connective tissue genes of Marfan-type disorders. FGFR3, SOX9, and RUNX2 regulate cartilage and bone development. HGPRT, PRPP synthetase, xanthine oxidase, and URAT1 belong to purine and urate handling. CFTR, HFE, and ATP7B cause cystic fibrosis, hemochromatosis, and Wilson disease. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, osteoporosis pathogenesis, genetic factors, p20–21).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peak bone mass is reached in young adulthood and depends on genes, physical activity, muscle strength, diet, and hormonal state (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: low dietary calcium in adolescence can reduce peak bone mass and raise later osteoporosis risk (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 3 — RANKL and OPG in bone metabolism"},{"id":"sdl56_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which molecular change in osteoblasts is the primary driver of increased osteoclastogenesis in postmenopausal women following estrogen withdrawal?","choices":{"A":"Decreased sclerostin production by osteocytes releases the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts, paradoxically stimulating osteoblast-derived RANKL secretion and osteoclast recruitment","B":"Decreased calcitonin secretion from parafollicular thyroid C cells removes the primary hormonal inhibition of osteoclast acid secretion and bone resorption","C":"Decreased OPG production by osteoblasts increases the RANKL-to-OPG ratio, allowing more RANKL to bind RANK on osteoclast precursors and amplify osteoclastogenesis","D":"Decreased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D synthesis from estrogen-dependent renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity impairs calcium absorption and drives secondary hyperparathyroidism","E":"Decreased M-CSF secretion by marrow stromal cells reduces osteoclast precursor survival and is replaced by IL-17-dependent osteoclastogenesis in estrogen deficiency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Estrogen maintains bone mass in part by stimulating OPG production in osteoblasts. OPG is a decoy receptor that binds RANKL and prevents it from interacting with its receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. After menopause, estrogen deficiency reduces OPG production, increasing the RANKL:OPG ratio. The excess unbound RANKL then binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, activating NF-kB signaling and driving osteoclastogenesis. This results in a high-turnover state with increased osteoclast number and activity. Simultaneously, estrogen deficiency elevates pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha) that further amplify RANKL expression and osteoclast survival.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menopause → estrogen → OPG → RANKL:OPG ratio → osteoclastogenesis. Also → IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha → further RANKL amplification. Result: high-turnover osteoporosis. Denosumab works by mimicking OPG — it is a RANKL-neutralizing antibody. COMLEX: Postmenopausal osteoporosis is a major COMLEX Clinical Presentation 8 condition; estrogen therapy is no longer first-line due to breast cancer and cardiovascular risks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha produced in estrogen deficiency directly amplify osteoclastogenesis via RANKL upregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which bone compartment undergoes the most rapid and pronounced loss in the first decade following menopause, and what is the approximate annual rate?","choices":{"A":"Cancellous bone, with its high surface-to-volume ratio providing extensive osteoclast access, loses up to 9% per year in the first decade after menopause","B":"Cortical bone of the femoral diaphysis, which provides the greatest total skeletal mass, loses up to 9% per year due to intracortical remodeling driven by PTH excess","C":"Subchondral bone in weight-bearing joints, which is most exposed to mechanical loading, loses up to 6% per year through estrogen-dependent osteoclast activation at joint surfaces","D":"Periosteal bone of the distal radius, which is metabolically active in younger women, loses up to 12% per year as periosteal osteoblast activity drops after estrogen withdrawal","E":"Endosteal cortical bone at the femoral neck, which is the most vascular skeletal region, loses up to 3% per year because of estrogen-dependent marrow vascularity changes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cancellous bone, also called trabecular or spongy bone, has a vastly higher surface-to-volume ratio than cortical bone, exposing far more bone surface to osteoclast-mediated resorption. In postmenopausal women, cancellous bone can lose up to 9% per year in the first decade after menopause, while cortical bone loses approximately 2% per year during this same accelerated phase. The cancellous-rich vertebral bodies and femoral neck are therefore the classic sites of postmenopausal fracture. After the first decade, the rate of loss decelerates as a new, lower steady state is established.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postmenopausal bone loss — cancellous up to 9%/year, cortical up to 2%/year in the first decade. Classic fracture sites: vertebral compression, Colles fracture of distal radius, femoral neck fracture. DEXA T-score of -2.5 or below = osteoporosis (FA p467). COMLEX: Vertebral compression fractures cause acute back pain, loss of height, and kyphosis — classic presentation on COMLEX clinical vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha produced in estrogen deficiency directly amplify osteoclastogenesis via RANKL upregulation","whyNot":{"B":"In postmenopausal women, cance"}},{"id":"sdl56_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the context of postmenopausal osteoporosis, which three pro-inflammatory cytokines are upregulated by estrogen withdrawal and directly amplify osteoclastogenesis and osteoclast survival?","choices":{"A":"IL-4, IL-10, and TGF-beta are elevated after menopause and stimulate osteoclast precursor proliferation through STAT6 and Smad3-dependent transcriptional activation","B":"IL-2, IFN-gamma, and TNF-beta are elevated in estrogen deficiency and drive osteoclast activation through T cell receptor signaling and MHC class I cross-presentation","C":"IL-8, IL-12, and IL-23 are upregulated by estrogen deficiency and recruit neutrophils and dendritic cells that amplify peritrabecular bone destruction at remodeling sites","D":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha are elevated after estrogen withdrawal and promote osteoclast precursor differentiation, prolong osteoclast survival, and amplify bone resorption","E":"IL-3, IL-5, and GM-CSF are produced by marrow stromal cells after estrogen withdrawal and drive mast cell degranulation and eosinophil recruitment near trabecular surfaces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Estrogen deficiency after menopause leads to elevated levels of IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. These cytokines directly promote osteoclastogenesis by increasing RANKL expression on osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells, and by prolonging osteoclast survival through anti-apoptotic signaling. IL-1 and TNF-alpha are particularly potent amplifiers of RANKL-driven osteoclast differentiation. This cytokine-driven amplification of the RANKL pathway explains why the postmenopausal period is characterized by high-turnover bone remodeling with markedly elevated markers such as urinary N-telopeptide and serum C-telopeptide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menopause cytokine triad = IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha. These same cytokines drive bone erosion in RA (periarticular erosions = RANKL amplification at synovial pannus). High-turnover markers: elevated urinary NTx, CTx, and serum bone-specific ALP. COMLEX: RANKL inhibitors like denosumab work both in osteoporosis and in cancer-related bone metastases by targeting the same pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha produced in estrogen deficiency directly amplify osteoclastogenesis via RANKL upregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"OPG is a key molecular regulator of osteoclastogenesis. On which cell type is OPG primarily produced, and what is its mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"OPG is a membrane-bound co-receptor expressed on osteoclast precursors that competes with RANK for RANKL binding and reduces osteoclast activation when RANKL is limited","B":"OPG is a soluble decoy receptor secreted by osteoblasts that binds RANKL, preventing it from interacting with RANK on osteoclast precursors and inhibiting osteoclastogenesis","C":"OPG is a matrix metalloproteinase inhibitor secreted by marrow stromal cells that blocks collagen degradation during osteoclast-driven resorption lacunae formation","D":"OPG is a serine kinase receptor expressed on osteocytes that activates Wnt signaling downstream of LRP5 and promotes osteoblast progenitor differentiation from marrow stroma","E":"OPG is a cytokine receptor expressed on T lymphocytes that suppresses IL-6 and IL-1 release from macrophages and limits osteoclast-promoting inflammation in bone marrow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"OPG, or osteoprotegerin, is a soluble decoy receptor secreted primarily by osteoblasts and marrow stromal cells. It acts by binding RANKL in the extracellular space, preventing RANKL from engaging its functional receptor RANK on osteoclast precursors. Because OPG occupies RANKL without triggering any downstream signaling, it effectively neutralizes RANKL and inhibits osteoclastogenesis. The RANKL:OPG ratio is a key determinant of osteoclast number and activity. When OPG is high relative to RANKL, bone resorption is suppressed. When the ratio tips in favor of RANKL (such as in estrogen deficiency or inflammatory states), osteoclastogenesis increases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANKL/RANK/OPG axis: RANKL on osteoblasts and stromal cells binds RANK on osteoclast precursors → NF-kB → osteoclastogenesis. OPG = decoy receptor, binds RANKL, blocks RANK interaction. OPG is the prototype for denosumab. Mutations in RANK, RANKL, OPG → severe disruption of bone metabolism. COMLEX: The RANKL-OPG axis is also active in lymph node development and immune regulation, making it a cross-system high-yield target.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid arthritis amplify RANKL expression on synoviocytes, driving periarticular bone erosion"},{"id":"sdl56_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"When RANKL binds to RANK on osteoclast precursors, which intracellular signaling pathway is primarily activated to drive differentiation into mature osteoclasts?","choices":{"A":"NF-kB signaling is primarily activated by RANK-RANKL interaction and is essential for osteoclast generation, survival, and the maintenance of bone resorption capacity","B":"JAK-STAT signaling is activated by RANK-RANKL binding through associated JAK2 kinase, leading to STAT3-dependent transcription of osteoclast cathepsin K and acid pump genes","C":"Wnt/beta-catenin signaling is activated by RANK-RANKL binding through a RANK-LRP5 co-receptor complex that drives nuclear beta-catenin translocation in osteoclast precursors","D":"PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling is the primary pathway activated by RANK-RANKL binding, promoting osteoclast precursor survival and protein synthesis through mTORC1 activation","E":"MAPK-ERK signaling is the primary pathway activated by RANK-RANKL binding, driving cyclin D1 expression and entry of osteoclast precursors into the cell cycle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RANK belongs to the TNF receptor superfamily. Upon RANKL binding, RANK recruits TRAF adapter proteins (especially TRAF6) which activate NF-kB signaling. NF-kB translocates to the nucleus and drives transcription of genes essential for osteoclast differentiation, including NFATc1, the master regulator of osteoclastogenesis. NF-kB signaling is also required for osteoclast survival. This pathway is the reason why therapeutic RANKL inhibition with denosumab potently suppresses osteoclastogenesis — blocking RANKL prevents the RANK-TRAF6-NF-kB cascade from initiating. Wnt signaling and PI3K-AKT are important in osteoblast biology, not osteoclast differentiation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANK-RANKL → TRAF6 → NF-kB → NFATc1 = master osteoclast transcription factor. Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates act differently, inhibiting farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase to trigger osteoclast apoptosis. Denosumab upstream blocks RANKL before it ever reaches RANK. COMLEX: NF-kB is a convergent pathway for both inflammatory cytokines and RANKL — explaining why RA (IL-1, TNF-alpha-driven) causes the same periarticular bone erosion pattern as primary osteoclast activation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid arthritis amplify RANKL expression on synoviocytes, driving periarticular bone erosion","whyNot":{"C":"Wnt signaling and PI3K-AKT are"}},{"id":"sdl56_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which drug directly mimics the function of OPG by binding RANKL in the extracellular space and preventing osteoclast activation?","choices":{"A":"Alendronate, a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate that is internalized by osteoclasts during bone resorption and inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase to induce apoptosis","B":"Romosozumab, a monoclonal antibody directed against sclerostin that removes the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts and simultaneously suppresses osteoclast activity","C":"Teriparatide, recombinant PTH 1-34 that activates PTH-1 receptors on osteoblasts at intermittent doses to drive anabolic bone formation beyond resorption-coupled rates","D":"Denosumab, a human monoclonal antibody directed against RANKL, binds RANKL with high affinity and prevents it from interacting with RANK on osteoclast precursors","E":"Raloxifene, a selective estrogen receptor modulator that acts as an estrogen agonist in bone tissue and reduces osteoclast-mediated resorption through genomic ER signaling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Denosumab is a fully human IgG2 monoclonal antibody that binds RANKL with very high affinity and specificity, acting as an OPG mimetic. By neutralizing RANKL, denosumab prevents it from binding RANK on osteoclast precursors, thereby blocking osteoclastogenesis. Unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab does not integrate into the bone matrix and its effect is fully reversible upon discontinuation — stopping denosumab abruptly can cause rebound osteoclastogenesis and rapid bone loss. Given subcutaneously every 6 months, denosumab is approved for postmenopausal osteoporosis, glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis, and bone metastases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Denosumab = anti-RANKL monoclonal antibody, mimics OPG, given SC every 6 months. Critical: unlike bisphosphonates, must transition to antiresorptive when stopping denosumab or rebound bone loss and fractures occur. Also used in giant cell tumor of bone. COMLEX: Denosumab represents a biologic drug in the same conceptual class as biologic DMARDs — targeted neutralization of a specific ligand.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory cytokines in rheumatoid arthritis amplify RANKL expression on synoviocytes, driving periarticular bone erosion","whyNot":{"A":"Unlike bisphosphonates, denosu"}},{"id":"sdl56_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which histologic lesion is considered the hallmark of severe primary hyperparathyroidism in bone, and what accounts for the characteristic color of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Mosaic lamellar bone with irregular cement lines filling trabecular spaces and a patchwork of old and new bone matrix characterizes severe hyperparathyroid bone pathology","B":"Widened unmineralized osteoid seams accumulating on trabecular surfaces and staining orange-red on Goldner trichrome represent hyperparathyroid bone disease","C":"Cortical tunneling by osteoclasts forming intracortical canals that are later lined by fibrous tissue and hemosiderin deposits characterizes osteitis fibrosa of the diaphysis","D":"Subperiosteal erosions producing a lacy irregular cortical border on the radial surface of the middle phalanges represent the earliest radiographic sign of hyperparathyroid bone","E":"Brown tumors are osteoclast-filled bone cysts in which microfractures and hemorrhage cause hemosiderin deposition, imparting the characteristic brown discoloration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown tumors are the hallmark lesion of severe primary hyperparathyroidism. They are not true neoplasms but rather osteoclast-filled bone cysts that form as excess PTH drives intense cortical resorption. As osteoclasts enlarge these cavities, microfractures occur and secondary hemorrhage deposits hemosiderin within the lesion, giving it the characteristic brown color. Widespread development of brown tumors represents osteitis fibrosa cystica — the generalized severe form of hyperparathyroid bone disease. Modern diagnosis of primary hyperparathyroidism at early stages means osteitis fibrosa cystica is now rarely seen in clinical practice.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brown tumor = osteoclast-filled cystic lesion with hemosiderin; brown color from hemosiderin. Generalized form = osteitis fibrosa cystica. Salt-and-pepper skull and subperiosteal resorption of radial middle phalanges are classic X-ray findings. Primary HPT: high Ca, high PTH, low PO4. COMLEX: Primary HPT classic triad = Stones (nephrolithiasis), Bones (bone pain, brown tumors), Groans (GI — constipation, nausea, peptic ulcers).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition is a recognized complication of primary hyperparathyroidism-driven metabolic dysregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Excess PTH in primary hyperparathyroidism drives osteoclastic resorption at a specific bone surface that produces the most pathognomonic radiographic finding. Which surface is this?","choices":{"A":"The subperiosteal surface of the radial aspect of the middle phalanges shows the most prominent resorption, producing a lacy irregular cortical border on plain radiograph","B":"The trabecular surfaces of the lumbar vertebral bodies show preferential osteoclast erosion, producing vertical corduroy striations due to selective horizontal trabecular resorption","C":"The endochondral growth plate of long bones shows the most prominent PTH-driven osteoclast activity, producing metaphyseal widening and flaring on plain films","D":"The articular cartilage of metacarpophalangeal joints undergoes osteoclast-mediated erosion, producing the joint space narrowing pattern that mimics rheumatoid arthritis","E":"The periosteal surface of the outer table of the skull shows preferential osteoclast activity, producing the ground glass pattern that overlaps with fibrous dysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PTH-driven osteoclastic resorption most prominently occurs at the subperiosteal cortical bone surface, particularly the radial aspect of the middle phalanges of the hand. On plain radiograph, this appears as a lacy, irregular cortical border — a pathognomonic finding of hyperparathyroidism. Radiographically, the skull may show a salt-and-pepper pattern from diffuse osteoclastic activity. The classic clinical teaching is that subperiosteal resorption of the middle phalanges is the most specific radiographic finding for hyperparathyroidism among all bone diseases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH effects on bone = increases osteoclast activity at subperiosteal surfaces; specifically radial aspect of middle phalanges on hand X-ray. Also skull = salt-and-pepper pattern. Biochemistry: primary HPT = high Ca, high PTH, low PO4, elevated ALP. COMLEX: PTH acts on bone through osteoblasts (which express PTH-1R) to upregulate RANKL, then osteoclasts respond — PTH has no direct receptor on osteoclasts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition is a recognized complication of primary hyperparathyroidism-driven metabolic dysregulation","whyNot":{"E":"Radiographically, the skull ma"}},{"id":"sdl56_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which set of laboratory values best distinguishes primary hyperparathyroidism from secondary hyperparathyroidism caused by chronic renal failure?","choices":{"A":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium, while secondary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated calcium and elevated alkaline phosphatase","B":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated PTH with low phosphate and normal calcium, while secondary shows low PTH with high phosphate and low calcium","C":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows low 25-hydroxyvitamin D with elevated calcium, while secondary shows normal vitamin D with elevated calcium and elevated PTH","D":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated serum calcium with elevated PTH, while secondary hyperparathyroidism shows low serum calcium with elevated PTH driven by hypocalcemia","E":"Primary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated calcium with suppressed PTH, while secondary hyperparathyroidism shows elevated calcium with proportionally elevated PTH"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In primary hyperparathyroidism, an autonomous parathyroid adenoma secretes excess PTH independent of serum calcium levels, resulting in hypercalcemia with inappropriately elevated (or non-suppressed) PTH. In secondary hyperparathyroidism from chronic renal failure, the failing kidney cannot activate vitamin D or excrete phosphate; the resulting hypocalcemia is the stimulus for compensatory PTH secretion. Therefore, secondary hyperparathyroidism is characterized by low serum calcium with elevated PTH. Both forms produce elevated PTH and increased osteoclastic bone resorption, but the calcium level differentiates them and guides treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary HPT = high Ca + high PTH + low PO4. Secondary HPT from CKD = LOW Ca + high PTH + high PO4. PTH in primary = autonomous; in secondary = compensatory response to hypocalcemia. Both increase osteoclast activity. Tertiary HPT = CKD patient develops autonomous PTH secretion after prolonged secondary HPT → high Ca + very high PTH. COMLEX: CKD sequence: decreased 1,25-OH2D → low Ca → high PTH → secondary HPT → renal osteodystrophy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition is a recognized complication of primary hyperparathyroidism-driven metabolic dysregulation"},{"id":"sdl56_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the primary pathologic mechanism by which vitamin D deficiency causes osteomalacia in adults?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin D deficiency directly triggers osteoclast overactivation through RANKL upregulation on marrow stromal cells, causing high-turnover resorption of already-mineralized trabecular bone","B":"Vitamin D deficiency inhibits osteoblast differentiation from mesenchymal progenitors by blocking Wnt/beta-catenin signaling, reducing type I collagen synthesis and osteoid production","C":"Vitamin D deficiency induces osteocyte apoptosis by depriving them of anti-apoptotic 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D receptor signaling, releasing mineral-binding proteins into the lacunar space","D":"Vitamin D deficiency reduces synthesis of bone morphogenetic proteins in osteoblasts, preventing hydroxyapatite nucleation even when calcium and phosphate are present in normal concentrations","E":"Vitamin D deficiency impairs intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption, reducing their serum availability and preventing adequate mineralization of newly synthesized osteoid matrix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D deficiency causes osteomalacia (in adults) and rickets (in children) primarily by impairing calcium and phosphate absorption from the intestine. 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is essential for inducing intestinal calcium transport proteins (calbindin and TRPV6). Without adequate calcium and phosphate in the serum, newly synthesized osteoid cannot undergo normal mineralization, leading to accumulation of unmineralized osteoid seams. On bone biopsy, widened osteoid seams are the diagnostic finding. Normal bone mineralization depends on both adequate substrate (calcium and phosphate) and functional alkaline phosphatase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D deficiency → impaired Ca and PO4 absorption → unmineralized osteoid accumulates → osteomalacia in adults, rickets in children. Labs: low Ca, low PO4, elevated PTH (secondary HPT), elevated ALP, low 25-OH vitamin D. Bone biopsy: widened osteoid seams on tetracycline labeling. COMLEX: Osteomalacia causes bone pain (from periosteal irritation by unmineralized osteoid) and proximal muscle weakness — a key clinical clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Vitamin D deficiency impairs immune tolerance and is linked to autoimmune disease susceptibility through type III and IV hypersensitivity"},{"id":"sdl56_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In children with rickets, which radiographic finding at the growth plate best reflects failure of provisional cartilage calcification at the zone of hypertrophic chondrocytes?","choices":{"A":"Chalk-stick transverse fractures through the diaphysis of long bones reflecting brittle, disorganized woven bone replacing the normal lamellar structure in rickets","B":"Dense metaphyseal bands reflecting calcium precipitation lines at the zone of provisional calcification that appear as lead lines in vitamin D-deficient growth plates","C":"Periosteal elevation along the femoral and tibial diaphyses reflecting subperiosteal hemorrhage from scurvy, which must be excluded in the differential diagnosis of rickets","D":"Vertical striations in the vertebral bodies reflecting the preferential cancellous bone resorption pattern in calcium-deficient rickets from inadequate dietary intake","E":"Metaphyseal cupping and fraying with a widened growth plate reflects accumulation of uncalcified hypertrophic chondrocytes in vitamin D-deficient endochondral ossification"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In normal endochondral ossification, hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate undergo apoptosis and their lacunae become calcified at the zone of provisional calcification, allowing vascular invasion and replacement by woven bone. In vitamin D deficiency, calcium and phosphate availability is reduced, preventing mineralization of this zone. Hypertrophic chondrocytes accumulate, the zone of provisional calcification widens and becomes irregular, and the metaphysis shows cupping and fraying on plain radiograph. Additional pediatric findings include bowing of weight-bearing legs, rachitic rosary from costochondral junction widening, and craniotabes from soft calvaria. Periosteal elevation from subperiosteal hemorrhage is a sign of scurvy, not of failed calcification in rickets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rickets X-ray = metaphyseal cupping and fraying + widened irregular growth plate. Also: bow legs, rachitic rosary, craniotabes, frontal bossing. Vs. scurvy = subperiosteal hemorrhage and periosteal elevation, scorbutic zone (radiolucent metaphyseal line). COMLEX: Rickets from nutritional vitamin D deficiency has a different presentation from X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets, which involves FGF23 excess causing phosphate wasting — treatment includes phosphate supplementation plus calcitriol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Vitamin D deficiency impairs immune tolerance and is linked to autoimmune disease susceptibility through type III and IV hypersensitivity"},{"id":"sdl56_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In addition to impaired bone mineralization, what secondary endocrine complication of vitamin D deficiency further accelerates bone loss in patients with osteomalacia?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin D deficiency triggers primary hyperthyroidism through downregulation of vitamin D receptor in thyroid follicular cells, increasing metabolic rate and bone resorption","B":"Vitamin D deficiency causes autonomous insulin hypersecretion from pancreatic beta cells, leading to reactive hypoglycemia that impairs osteoblast energy metabolism","C":"Hypocalcemia from impaired intestinal calcium absorption stimulates secondary hyperparathyroidism, which increases osteoclast-driven bone resorption beyond the primary mineralization defect","D":"Vitamin D deficiency directly stimulates FGF23 overproduction from osteocytes independent of phosphate levels, causing renal phosphate wasting and further depleting mineralization substrates","E":"Vitamin D deficiency activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, increasing distal tubular calcium excretion and creating a positive feedback loop of worsening hypocalcemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In vitamin D deficiency, reduced intestinal calcium absorption causes hypocalcemia, which is a potent stimulus for PTH secretion by the parathyroid glands. This secondary hyperparathyroidism adds a resorptive component to the bone disease of vitamin D deficiency: PTH drives osteoclast-mediated cortical and trabecular bone resorption on top of the failure of osteoid mineralization. Clinically, labs in osteomalacia show low calcium, low phosphate, elevated PTH, elevated ALP, and low 25-hydroxyvitamin D. Secondary hyperparathyroidism can partially restore serum calcium at the expense of skeletal mineral content.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D deficiency → low Ca → secondary PTH elevation → bone resorption amplified on top of impaired mineralization. Key lab pattern: low Ca + low PO4 + HIGH PTH + HIGH ALP + low 25-OH-D. Contrast with primary HPT: HIGH Ca + HIGH PTH + low PO4 + HIGH ALP. Osteomalacia treatment = replete vitamin D + calcium. COMLEX: Proximal muscle weakness and bone pain are classic clinical clues for vitamin D deficiency/osteomalacia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Vitamin D deficiency impairs immune tolerance and is linked to autoimmune disease susceptibility through type III and IV hypersensitivity"},{"id":"sdl56_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In chronic kidney disease, which biochemical step in vitamin D metabolism is impaired by the failing kidney, resulting in reduced active vitamin D levels?","choices":{"A":"The failing kidney develops vascular calcification that obstructs tubular blood flow, selectively ischemic the proximal tubule cells responsible for vitamin D hydroxylation","B":"The failing kidney excessively excretes 25-hydroxyvitamin D-binding protein into the urine, depleting circulating vitamin D metabolite reservoir and reducing substrate availability","C":"The failing kidney accumulates uremic toxins that inhibit hepatic 25-hydroxylase activity, blocking the first step of vitamin D activation from cholecalciferol to calcidiol","D":"The failing kidney produces excess FGF23 independently of phosphate, driving 24-hydroxylase upregulation that catabolizes 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D before it can act on target tissues","E":"The failing kidney loses 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, preventing conversion of 25-hydroxyvitamin D to the active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D that is required for intestinal calcium absorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D undergoes two sequential hydroxylation steps for full activation. The first (25-hydroxylation) occurs in the liver, producing 25-hydroxyvitamin D. The second (1-alpha-hydroxylation) occurs primarily in the proximal tubule of the kidney, producing the biologically active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. In chronic kidney disease, the progressive loss of functional proximal tubular mass impairs 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity. Without adequate 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, intestinal calcium absorption falls, serum calcium drops, and secondary hyperparathyroidism develops. This is why standard vitamin D supplements are insufficient in CKD — patients require exogenous active vitamin D analogs such as calcitriol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D activation pathway: skin/diet → D3 → liver 25-hydroxylation → 25-OH-D → kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylation → 1,25-OH2-D (calcitriol, active form). CKD blocks step 2. Treatment with calcitriol bypasses the impaired kidney step. FGF23 (from bone) inhibits renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase — elevated in CKD as a compensatory but ultimately counterproductive response. COMLEX: CKD patients require calcitriol (active analog) not ergocalciferol (D2) or cholecalciferol (D3) because the kidney cannot hydroxylate them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Routine lab panels in chronic renal disease reveal hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH as markers of renal osteodystrophy"},{"id":"sdl56_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In renal osteodystrophy, how does hyperphosphatemia from the failing kidney contribute to worsening secondary hyperparathyroidism?","choices":{"A":"Hyperphosphatemia directly stimulates parathyroid chief cell proliferation by binding a phosphate-sensing receptor that activates the calcium-sensing receptor signaling cascade","B":"Hyperphosphatemia inhibits calcitonin secretion from parafollicular thyroid C cells, removing the negative feedback signal that normally suppresses PTH gene transcription","C":"Hyperphosphatemia lowers ionized calcium through calcium-phosphate complexation and suppresses renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, removing two key inhibitors of PTH secretion simultaneously","D":"Hyperphosphatemia causes phosphaturia-driven metabolic alkalosis in the proximal tubule that sensitizes parathyroid cell calcium-sensing receptors to small decreases in ionized calcium","E":"Hyperphosphatemia suppresses FGF23 secretion from osteocytes, which increases renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity and raises circulating calcitriol levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperphosphatemia from renal failure impairs PTH regulation through two main mechanisms. First, high phosphate complexes with calcium in the circulation, lowering free ionized calcium. The parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor detects this drop in ionized calcium and increases PTH secretion. Second, hyperphosphatemia (and the elevated FGF23 that follows) inhibits renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, further reducing 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D levels. 1,25-OH2D normally suppresses PTH synthesis at the gene level, so its deficiency removes this brake. Phosphate also directly stimulates PTH secretion and parathyroid hyperplasia (First Aid), but not by activating the calcium-sensing receptor: activation of that receptor suppresses PTH, so a phosphate signal working through it could not drive hyperparathyroidism. FGF23 rises, not falls, with hyperphosphatemia, and it suppresses rather than increases 1-alpha-hydroxylase. The combined effect is persistent, driven secondary hyperparathyroidism that causes osteitis fibrosa cystica as part of renal osteodystrophy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKD cascade — low GFR → high PO4 and low 1,25-OH2D → low Ca → high PTH. Also elevated FGF23 worsens 1-alpha-hydroxylase suppression. Manage with phosphate binders, dietary phosphate restriction, calcitriol, and calcimimetics (cinacalcet) to suppress PTH. COMLEX: Rugger jersey spine on plain film = dense osteosclerotic bands at vertebral endplates alternating with less dense central bone — classic renal osteodystrophy finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Routine lab panels in chronic renal disease reveal hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH as markers of renal osteodystrophy","whyNot":{"E":"FGF23 rises, not falls, with h"}},{"id":"sdl56_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Renal osteodystrophy encompasses multiple skeletal pathologies. Which of the following best describes the full spectrum of bone changes that can occur in this condition?","choices":{"A":"Renal osteodystrophy produces exclusively osteosclerotic changes throughout the skeleton from calcium phosphate precipitation into bone matrix driven by hyperphosphatemia","B":"Renal osteodystrophy produces only high-turnover osteoclastic changes identical to primary hyperparathyroidism, with no component of impaired mineralization or adynamic disease","C":"Renal osteodystrophy consists solely of avascular necrosis at the femoral head and distal femoral condyles from vascular calcification obstructing subchondral blood supply","D":"Renal osteodystrophy encompasses osteomalacia, secondary hyperparathyroid bone changes, osteosclerosis of vertebral endplates, and adynamic bone disease with very low turnover","E":"Renal osteodystrophy causes periosteal new bone formation along long bone diaphyses driven by FGF23-mediated osteoblast activation independent of PTH or vitamin D changes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Renal osteodystrophy is a collective term for the skeletal changes occurring in chronic renal failure, reflecting multiple simultaneous pathologic processes. These include: (1) osteomalacia from reduced 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and impaired mineralization; (2) secondary hyperparathyroidism causing osteitis fibrosa cystica with subperiosteal resorption; (3) osteosclerosis, particularly at vertebral endplates giving the rugger jersey spine appearance; and (4) adynamic bone disease, particularly in dialysis patients who are over-treated with vitamin D analogs causing PTH to fall too low, leaving bone with virtually no remodeling capacity and reduced ability to repair microdamage. Growth retardation is a fifth component in skeletally immature patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal osteodystrophy = four-component disease: osteomalacia + osteitis fibrosa (secondary HPT) + osteosclerosis + adynamic bone. Rugger jersey spine on lateral plain film is pathognomonic. Adynamic bone from over-suppressed PTH is paradoxically associated with high fracture risk despite low turnover. COMLEX: Dialysis patients need careful PTH monitoring to keep bone turnover adequate without causing osteitis fibrosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Routine lab panels in chronic renal disease reveal hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH as markers of renal osteodystrophy","whyNot":{"A":"These include: (1) osteomalaci","B":"These include: (1) osteomalaci","C":"These include: (1) osteomalaci"}},{"id":"sdl56_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the hallmark microscopic finding in Paget disease of bone, and which phase of the disease does it represent?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse osteoid accumulation with widened unmineralized seams on all trabecular surfaces and osteoblasts embedded in uncalcified matrix characterizes the active mixed phase of Paget disease","B":"Peritrabecular fibrosis with osteoclast-filled cysts containing hemosiderin deposits and reactive woven bone formation characterizes the lytic phase of Paget disease","C":"Infiltration of marrow by atypical osteoblasts forming haphazard new woven bone in sheets with entrapped osteoclasts is the histologic hallmark of pagetic bone remodeling","D":"Dense fibrous replacement of marrow spaces by spindle cells with interspersed woven bone trabeculae and peripheral rimming osteoblasts characterizes the sclerotic phase of Paget disease","E":"A mosaic pattern of woven and lamellar bone in chaotic juxtapositions separated by irregular cement lines is the hallmark of the mixed phase of Paget disease and persists into the burned-out sclerotic stage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The histologic hallmark of Paget disease is the mosaic pattern — a patchwork of woven and lamellar bone in chaotic juxtaposition, separated by irregular scalloped cement lines that represent the boundaries of successive waves of resorption and deposition. This pattern arises because pagetic osteoclasts resorb bone abnormally rapidly, and osteoblasts deposit new bone in a disorganized fashion, producing architecturally inferior woven bone rather than organized lamellar bone. The three phases are: (1) lytic — predominantly osteoclastic; (2) mixed — concurrent osteoclastic and osteoblastic; (3) sclerotic — burnt-out, dense but structurally weak. The mosaic cement-line pattern is present throughout phases 2 and 3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease hallmark = mosaic pattern of woven and lamellar bone with irregular cement lines. Three phases: lytic (osteoclastic), mixed (concurrent), sclerotic (burned out). ALP is markedly elevated in active disease (osteoblastic activity); serum Ca and PO4 are NORMAL (unlike hyperparathyroidism). Serum ALP is the best marker of disease activity. COMLEX: Paget disease increases blood flow through hyperemic pagetic bone — can cause high-output heart failure in polyostotic disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Paget disease of bone carries a low but real risk of secondary osteosarcoma, the most feared malignant complication of pagetic remodeling"},{"id":"sdl56_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 75-year-old man with known Paget disease has markedly elevated alkaline phosphatase but normal serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH. Which best explains why calcium remains normal in active Paget disease despite intense bone remodeling?","choices":{"A":"In Paget disease, the parathyroid glands detect excess calcium released from resorbing pagetic bone and maximally suppress PTH, driving renal calcium excretion to maintain normal serum levels","B":"In Paget disease, resorption and deposition are coupled within focal pagetic bone, so calcium liberated from resorption is locally reused in new bone and serum calcium remains balanced","C":"In Paget disease, calcitonin is autonomously secreted from C cells in response to pagetic bone hypervascularity, continuously suppressing serum calcium toward normal ranges","D":"In Paget disease, the renal tubules dramatically increase calcium excretion as a homeostatic response to the large flux of calcium entering the circulation from pagetic resorption sites","E":"In Paget disease, osteoclast activity is inhibited by intracellular signaling defects in pagetic cells, so net resorption does not exceed formation despite elevated ALP and intense histologic activity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Paget disease is characterized by coupled but chaotic and accelerated remodeling within focal pagetic lesions. Osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic new bone formation occur simultaneously within the same pagetic foci, so the calcium liberated from resorption is quickly taken up by osteoblasts in the formation phase. The net effect is that serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH remain normal — a key feature distinguishing Paget disease from primary hyperparathyroidism. The biochemical hallmark is an isolated markedly elevated ALP (bone fraction) reflecting the intense osteoblastic response. Immobilization of a patient with Paget disease can uncouple this balance and cause hypercalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease labs = markedly elevated ALP (bone fraction), NORMAL Ca, NORMAL PO4, NORMAL PTH. Contrast with primary HPT: high Ca + high PTH. Immobilization in Paget disease can cause hypercalcemia by uncoupling resorption from formation. Treatment: bisphosphonates (reduce osteoclast activity). Also calcitonin in refractory cases. COMLEX: Paget disease skull involvement causes hearing loss from cochlear compression; hat size increase from skull enlargement is a classic clinical vignette detail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Paget disease of bone carries a low but real risk of secondary osteosarcoma, the most feared malignant complication of pagetic remodeling","whyNot":{"A":"The net effect is that serum c"}},{"id":"sdl56_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which rare but serious neoplastic complication occurs in fewer than 1% of all patients with Paget disease but in 5 to 10% of those with severe polyostotic involvement?","choices":{"A":"Secondary osteosarcoma arising within pagetic bone represents the most feared malignant complication, occurring in 5 to 10 percent of patients with severe polyostotic Paget disease","B":"High-output cardiac failure from arteriovenous shunting within hyperemic polyostotic pagetic bone can progress to dilated cardiomyopathy and requires urgent antiresorptive treatment","C":"Basilar invagination from skull base pagetic remodeling compresses the brainstem and produces cranial nerve palsies and ataxia requiring surgical decompression","D":"Chalk-stick transverse fractures through pagetic long bones result in permanent nonunion due to structurally abnormal pagetic bone callus with deficient osteoblast bridging","E":"Secondary amyloidosis from systemic chronic inflammation in polyostotic Paget disease causes progressive nephrotic syndrome requiring early bone marrow biopsy for diagnosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secondary osteosarcoma is the most feared and clinically important complication of Paget disease. While it occurs in fewer than 1% of all Paget disease patients overall, the rate rises to 5-10% in patients with severe polyostotic disease. The osteosarcoma typically arises within pagetic bone and carries a very poor prognosis — worse than primary osteosarcoma in younger patients. Clinically, it presents as sudden worsening of bone pain, rapid expansion of a known pagetic lesion, or an elevated ALP that escalates further. TP53 and RB gene inactivation are the most commonly identified drivers. High-output heart failure and fractures are also complications but are not neoplastic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease complications: (1) Secondary osteosarcoma — less than 1% overall, 5-10% with severe polyostotic disease; worst prognosis of all osteosarcomas. (2) High-output CHF from AV shunting in hyperemic pagetic bone. (3) Chalk-stick transverse fractures. (4) Hearing loss from cochlear/ossicular involvement. (5) Basilar invagination. COMLEX: Sudden worsening pain or rapidly rising ALP in a known Paget disease patient = rule out sarcomatous transformation immediately.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 54 → Obj 1 — Paget disease of bone carries a low but real risk of secondary osteosarcoma, the most feared malignant complication of pagetic remodeling","whyNot":{"B":"High-output heart failure and ","D":"High-output heart failure and "}},{"id":"sdl56_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Bisphosphonates are the most widely prescribed class of drugs for osteoporosis. What is the mechanism by which nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates inhibit osteoclast function?","choices":{"A":"Bisphosphonates bind to RANK receptors on osteoclast precursors and competitively block RANKL from initiating differentiation, acting upstream of the NF-kB signaling pathway","B":"Bisphosphonates bind hydroxyapatite and are internalized by resorbing osteoclasts, where they inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase and induce osteoclast apoptosis","C":"Bisphosphonates mimic calcitonin by binding to calcitonin receptors on osteoclasts and activating adenylyl cyclase, suppressing osteoclast acid secretion and ruffled border formation","D":"Bisphosphonates inhibit cathepsin K activity in osteoclast lysosomes after direct binding, preventing collagenolysis and dissolution of the organic component of bone matrix","E":"Bisphosphonates block PTH-1 receptor signaling in osteoblasts, reducing RANKL expression and indirectly decreasing osteoclast recruitment and activation at remodeling sites"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronate) are pyrophosphate analogs that bind tightly to hydroxyapatite in bone mineral. When osteoclasts resorb pagetic or osteoporotic bone, they ingest bisphosphonate-laden mineral. Intracellularly, the bisphosphonate inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate cholesterol synthesis pathway, preventing the prenylation of small GTPases such as Rac, Rho, and Ras that are required for osteoclast cytoskeletal function and survival. The result is osteoclast apoptosis. Because bisphosphonates are tightly bound to bone mineral, they have a prolonged skeletal residence time, which is why drug holidays are possible after long-term use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bisphosphonates = pyrophosphate analogs; bind hydroxyapatite; internalized by osteoclasts; inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in mevalonate pathway; prevent GTPase prenylation; cause osteoclast apoptosis. Key adverse effects: esophagitis (must stay upright 30-60 min), osteonecrosis of jaw (ONJ), atypical subtrochanteric femur fractures. Zoledronate = IV, given once yearly. COMLEX: Bisphosphonates also used for Paget disease, hypercalcemia of malignancy, osteogenesis imperfecta, and bone metastases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — Denosumab mirrors biologic DMARD logic by using a monoclonal antibody to neutralize a specific cytokine ligand in the RANKL pathway"},{"id":"sdl56_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which osteoporosis medication uniquely combines both anabolic and antiresorptive mechanisms simultaneously, distinguishing it from bisphosphonates and teriparatide?","choices":{"A":"Denosumab, a monoclonal antibody against RANKL, inhibits osteoclastogenesis while upregulating OPG in osteoblasts through a secondary feedback loop that promotes bone formation","B":"Raloxifene, a selective estrogen receptor modulator, acts as an estrogen agonist in bone tissue to increase osteoblast activity while antagonizing estrogen in breast and uterine tissue","C":"Teriparatide, recombinant PTH 1-34, increases bone formation predominantly at intermittent doses and also has a secondary antiresorptive effect through direct calcitonin stimulation","D":"Alendronate, a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate, inhibits osteoclast farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase while simultaneously stimulating osteoblast differentiation through BMP pathway activation","E":"Romosozumab, an antibody against sclerostin, removes the Wnt brake to increase bone formation while suppressing osteoclast activity, producing a dual anabolic and antiresorptive effect"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody against sclerostin, the Wnt signaling inhibitor produced by osteocytes. By neutralizing sclerostin, romosozumab removes the inhibitory brake on osteoblasts, leading to markedly increased bone formation. Additionally, in the first months of treatment, romosozumab also suppresses osteoclast activity — a dual mechanism not shared by bisphosphonates (antiresorptive only) or teriparatide (primarily anabolic with a secondary resorption increase at high doses). Romosozumab is approved for a maximum of 12 months, after which transition to an antiresorptive agent (bisphosphonate or denosumab) is recommended. It carries an FDA boxed warning for increased cardiovascular risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Romosozumab = anti-sclerostin antibody → dual mechanism: anabolic (removes Wnt inhibition) + antiresorptive (suppresses osteoclasts). Approved for 1 year only, then switch to antiresorptive. FDA boxed warning: increased MI and stroke risk — avoid in patients with recent cardiovascular events. COMLEX: Romosozumab vs. teriparatide: both are for severe osteoporosis; romosozumab is preferred when cardiovascular risk is low, teriparatide when cardiovascular risk is elevated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — Denosumab mirrors biologic DMARD logic by using a monoclonal antibody to neutralize a specific cytokine ligand in the RANKL pathway","whyNot":{"C":"Additionally, in the first mon","D":"Additionally, in the first mon"}},{"id":"sdl56_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Teriparatide and abaloparatide are anabolic agents approved for osteoporosis. What is the maximum approved duration of use for each agent, and what drug class is recommended to follow this therapy?","choices":{"A":"Both teriparatide and abaloparatide are approved for one year of use due to osteosarcoma risk in rat studies, after which transition to calcitonin therapy is required to consolidate gains","B":"Teriparatide is approved for up to three years in glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis and five years in postmenopausal osteoporosis, with no recommended transition therapy required","C":"Teriparatide and abaloparatide are each approved for at most two years, after which patients should transition to a bisphosphonate or denosumab to preserve the bone gains","D":"Teriparatide is approved for indefinite use as long as bone mineral density continues to increase on serial DEXA scans and serum calcium remains within the normal reference range","E":"Both teriparatide and abaloparatide are approved for a maximum of six months, reflecting the short anabolic window before osteoblast PTH receptor desensitization limits further response"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Teriparatide (recombinant PTH 1-34) and abaloparatide (a PTHrP 1-34 analog) are each approved for a maximum of two years of use. The two-year limit derives from rat studies showing osteosarcoma development with prolonged high-dose PTH-1R stimulation, though this has not been observed in human clinical trials. After completing teriparatide or abaloparatide, patients must be transitioned to an antiresorptive agent (bisphosphonate or denosumab) to preserve the bone mineral density gained. Without this transition, much of the gain is rapidly lost as osteoclast activity rebounds. Abaloparatide is given as a daily 80-mcg subcutaneous injection, while teriparatide is given as 20 mcg subcutaneously daily.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Teriparatide = PTH 1-34, 20 mcg SC daily, max 2 years. Abaloparatide = PTHrP analog, 80 mcg SC daily, max 2 years. Mechanism: intermittent PTH-1R stimulation → preferential osteoblast activation over osteoclast. Adverse effects: transient hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria, orthostatic hypotension after first dose. Both are contraindicated in Paget disease and prior bone radiation. COMLEX: Teriparatide is the only anabolic option for glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis where bisphosphonates have failed or are contraindicated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — Denosumab mirrors biologic DMARD logic by using a monoclonal antibody to neutralize a specific cytokine ligand in the RANKL pathway"},{"id":"sdl56_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old female competitive runner presents with a tibial stress fracture. DEXA shows bone density 1.8 standard deviations below age-matched peers. She reports amenorrhea for 9 months and consumes fewer than 1200 kcal/day with minimal dairy intake. Her coach notes she trains 25 hours per week. Serum estradiol is undetectable and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 14 ng/mL. Which combination of physiologic deficits most directly accounts for her failure to accrue expected peak bone mass?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive tibial microtrauma from high-volume training stimulates RANKL overexpression at remodeling sites, causing localized osteoclast overshoot that exceeds osteoblast-driven periosteal repair capacity","B":"Elevated cortisol from chronic training-related stress suppresses IGF-1 receptor expression in osteoblasts, reducing insulin-like growth factor-mediated matrix synthesis in cortical bone diaphyses","C":"Physical activity-induced FGF23 excess from osteocyte mechanosensory overload promotes renal phosphate wasting that impairs hydroxyapatite deposition in newly formed woven bone","D":"Excessive mechanical loading of immature cortical bone directly inhibits sclerostin in osteocytes, paradoxically downregulating Wnt signaling and impairing stress-induced bone formation","E":"Estrogen deficiency from hypothalamic amenorrhea reduces OPG expression in osteoblasts, increasing osteoclastogenesis, while inadequate calcium intake and low vitamin D further impair osteoid mineralization"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has the female athlete triad: energy deficiency, functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, and low bone density. Hypothalamic amenorrhea suppresses estrogen, which normally maintains bone by increasing OPG production in osteoblasts to keep the RANKL:OPG ratio low. Without estrogen, OPG falls, RANKL dominates, and osteoclastogenesis accelerates. Concurrently, her very low calcium intake and vitamin D deficiency reduce substrate availability for osteoid mineralization. Peak bone mass requires adequate calcium intake, vitamin D, physical activity (which is present), and sex steroids — she is deficient in three of these four pillars: calcium, vitamin D, and sex steroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Female athlete triad = energy deficiency + functional hypothalamic amenorrhea + low BMD. Peak bone mass requires: VDR-mediated calcium absorption, adequate calcium intake, sex steroids, weight-bearing activity. Loss of any combination reduces accrual. COMLEX: The broader updated framework, relative energy deficiency in sport (RED-S), extends the triad's energy-deficiency concept to other body systems. Stress fractures in young female athletes should prompt evaluation of menstrual status and nutritional intake.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis and BMU remodeling: osteocyte mechanosensory network couples mechanical load to formation, but cannot compensate for estrogen and nutrient deficiency"},{"id":"sdl56_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl who trains as a competitive cyclist and swimmer asks how she can build the highest possible peak bone mass before adulthood. Her diet, calcium intake and menstrual cycles are normal. Which change to her training would most effectively increase her bone mass?","choices":{"A":"Adding resistance exercise such as weight training, because load magnitude affects bone density more than the number of load cycles","B":"Adding more hours of cycling, because the number of repeated load cycles is the main mechanical stimulus for the formation of new bone","C":"Adding more endurance training of any kind, because total weekly training time is what determines how much new bone is formed","D":"Reducing all of her training, because intense exercise lowers estrogen and so reduces peak bone mass even when menses are normal","E":"Adding long-distance running, because high weekly mileage stimulates osteocytes more strongly than brief heavy loading does"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peak bone mass is reached in young adulthood. Its magnitude depends on hereditary factors and on physical activity, muscle strength, diet and hormonal state. Osteocytes sense mechanical load and stimulate or inhibit osteoblasts and osteoclasts accordingly; this explains bone loss in an immobilized limb or in the microgravity of space and the higher bone density of athletes. The type of exercise matters: load magnitude influences bone density more than the number of load cycles, so resistance exercise such as weight training is a more effective stimulus than repetitive endurance activity such as bicycling. Extra cycling, more endurance time or more running mileage adds load cycles rather than load magnitude. Her menses are normal, so there is no exercise-related estrogen deficiency to correct, and cutting her training would remove a mechanical stimulus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peak bone mass depends on genes plus physical activity, muscle strength, diet (adolescent calcium intake) and hormones; after it is reached, bone mass falls about 0.7% per year. COMLEX: Advise weight-bearing and resistance exercise with adequate calcium and vitamin D; disuse and microgravity cause bone loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis: the balance between osteoblast-driven formation and osteoclast-driven resorption within BMUs determines whether bone mass is maintained or progressively lost","whyNot":{"B":"Extra cycling, more endurance ","C":"Extra cycling, more endurance ","E":"Extra cycling, more endurance "}},{"id":"sdl56_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the factors that contribute to the achievement of peak bone mass.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old perimenopausal woman is found to have osteopenia on DEXA with a T-score of -1.8 at the lumbar spine. She has adequate weight-bearing activity, a BMI of 20, and consumes 650 mg of calcium daily. Genetic testing performed as part of a research study reveals she carries a vitamin D receptor gene polymorphism associated with reduced efficiency of calcium absorption at normal dietary intakes. Which intervention most directly compensates for the impact of this polymorphism on her skeleton?","choices":{"A":"Adding resistance exercise training three times per week activates the osteocyte mechanosensory network and upregulates Wnt/beta-catenin signaling to drive osteoblast-mediated periosteal apposition independent of calcium","B":"Initiating prophylactic bisphosphonate therapy preserves current bone density by inhibiting osteoclast farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase and preventing resorption that would further unmask the calcium deficit","C":"Starting a SERM such as raloxifene increases OPG expression in osteoblasts and compensates for the VDR polymorphism by reducing RANKL-driven osteoclastogenesis through estrogen receptor agonism","D":"Increasing dietary calcium intake combined with vitamin D3 supplementation to keep 25-hydroxyvitamin D in the sufficient range saturates absorption pathways to offset the reduced efficiency from the VDR polymorphism","E":"Prescribing calcitriol directly bypasses VDR binding requirements entirely and delivers active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D to intestinal cells through a non-genomic membrane receptor mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"VDR gene polymorphisms reduce the efficiency of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D-mediated calcium transport across intestinal epithelium, meaning less calcium is absorbed per unit of oral intake. The compensatory strategy is to increase both the substrate (dietary calcium) and the signal (vitamin D to produce 1,25-OH2D) to saturate absorption even with reduced per-receptor efficiency. Katzung (SDL 56 course reading) advises calcium supplements that bring total intake up to 1500 mg/day after menopause; it gives 20 ng/mL as a sufficient 25(OH)D level (National Academy of Medicine) and 30 ng/mL as the lower limit preferred by the Endocrine Society. Resistance exercise is important but addresses mechanical bone formation rather than calcium absorption. Bisphosphonates address resorption but do not correct the mineralization substrate deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VDR polymorphisms are among the factors that set peak bone mass; the genes most strongly linked to osteoporosis by genome-wide association studies are RANK, RANKL, OPG, HLA and the estrogen receptor gene (Robbins Ch 26). Calcium and vitamin D supplementation remain the foundational intervention for all osteoporosis prevention. Katzung: calcium to a total of 1500 mg/day after menopause; 25(OH)D sufficient at 20 ng/mL (NAM) or 30 ng/mL (Endocrine Society). COMLEX: Low dairy intake, dark skin, limited sun exposure, and northern latitude are the main risk factors for vitamin D insufficiency — all reduce substrate for 1,25-OH2D production.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Cytokine functions: 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D modulates cytokine production in immune cells, connecting calcium metabolism to immune regulation beyond its skeletal role","whyNot":{"A":"Resistance exercise is importa","B":"Bisphosphonates address resorp"}},{"id":"sdl56_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman, 12 years past menopause, has a vertebral compression fracture after lifting a bag of groceries. A bone biopsy is taken from the collapsed vertebral body during kyphoplasty. Serum calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase are normal. Which histologic finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Wide seams of unmineralized osteoid covering the trabecular surfaces, with a normal total volume of bone matrix","B":"Normally mineralized bone reduced in amount, with thin, perforated trabecular plates that have lost connections","C":"A mosaic of lamellar bone units joined by prominent, haphazard cement lines, with fibrovascular marrow tissue","D":"Osteoclasts tunneling along the centers of trabeculae, with the adjacent marrow replaced by fibrovascular tissue","E":"Increased bone mass with thick, disorganized trabeculae because the osteoclasts are unable to resorb bone matrix"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The hallmark of osteoporosis is histologically normal bone that is decreased in quantity. Postmenopausal osteoporosis mainly affects bone with a large surface area, such as the cancellous compartment of the vertebral bodies. The trabecular plates become perforated and thinned and lose their interconnections, which leads to microfractures and eventually vertebral collapse; collapsed vertebrae show loss of horizontal trabeculae with thickened vertical trabeculae. In senile osteoporosis the cortex is thinned instead, by subperiosteal and endosteal resorption, with widened Haversian systems. Serum calcium, phosphorus and alkaline phosphatase are usually normal. Unmineralized osteoid is osteomalacia. A mosaic pattern with prominent cement lines is Paget disease. Osteoclasts tunneling through trabeculae (dissecting osteitis) is hyperparathyroidism. Dense, brittle bone from failed osteoclast function is osteopetrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteoporosis = normal mineralization, decreased bone quantity, normal serum Ca, PO4 and ALP; postmenopausal type hits cancellous bone (vertebral compression fractures), senile type thins cortical bone. COMLEX: Plain films miss osteoporosis until 30% to 40% of bone mass is lost, so screen with DEXA (T-score of -2.5 or lower).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Homeostasis and remodeling of bone","whyNot":{"D":"Osteoclasts tunneling through ","E":"Dense, brittle bone from faile"}},{"id":"sdl56_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman who reached menopause 3 years ago has rapidly falling bone density. Her markers show increased bone resorption and also increased bone formation. She asks why her bone density is falling when her bone-forming cells are more active. Which statement best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen loss lowers the proliferative capacity of osteoblasts, producing low-turnover loss in which formation markers fall","B":"Estrogen loss blocks mineralization of new osteoid, so the extra matrix the osteoblasts make never becomes mineralized bone","C":"Estrogen loss raises formation markers by raising PTH, which builds cortical bone while it dissolves the cancellous bone","D":"Estrogen loss increases both bone formation and resorption, but resorption dominates, producing high-turnover bone loss","E":"Estrogen loss raises formation markers because new bone is laid down as disorganized woven bone, as in Paget disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Estrogen deficiency is the major cause of the rapid bone loss of the first decade after menopause. Although decreased estrogen increases both bone formation and bone resorption, resorption dominates, so postmenopausal osteoporosis is a high-turnover form. Menopause raises secretion of IL-1, IL-6 and TNF by innate immune cells and IL-17 by Th17 cells; the resulting rise in RANKL and fall in OPG increase osteoclast recruitment and activity. Reduced proliferative and biosynthetic capacity of aging osteoblasts describes senile osteoporosis, the low-turnover form. Osteoporosis is a loss of normally mineralized bone, not a mineralization defect; that is osteomalacia. Serum calcium and PTH are normal in postmenopausal osteoporosis. Woven bone in disordered remodeling describes the mixed phase of Paget disease; osteoporotic bone is histologically normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postmenopausal osteoporosis = high turnover (formation up, resorption up more; IL-1, IL-6, TNF, IL-17 raise RANKL and lower OPG); senile osteoporosis = low turnover (aging osteoblasts). COMLEX: Up to 2% of cortical and 9% of cancellous bone can be lost per year in the decade after menopause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Homeostasis and remodeling of bone","whyNot":{"B":"Osteoporosis is a loss of norm","C":"Serum calcium and PTH are norm"}},{"id":"sdl56_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the physiologic factors leading to menopausal bone loss.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old man has a wrist fracture after a minor fall, and DEXA shows markedly low bone density. Radiographs show that his epiphyses have not closed. His serum testosterone is normal, but he cannot convert androgens to estrogen because of aromatase deficiency. Which conclusion about bone physiology does his case best support?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen is needed for bone mass and epiphyseal closure even in men, which fits its central role in postmenopausal bone loss","B":"Testosterone fuses the growth plates directly, so his open epiphyses must reflect a separate excess of growth hormone instead","C":"Estrogen protects bone by activating renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so his low bone density must reflect a deficiency of calcitriol","D":"Bone loss without estrogen requires an excess of PTH, so his low bone density must reflect an unrecognized parathyroid adenoma","E":"Estrogen protects bone in women but not in men, so his low bone density must reflect a low calcium intake during adolescence"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Estrogen receptors are present in bone, and estrogen has direct effects on bone remodeling. Case reports of men who lack the estrogen receptor or who cannot make estrogen because of aromatase deficiency describe marked osteopenia and failure of the epiphyses to close, showing that estrogen is essential for bone development even in men; this man has normal testosterone, yet his bone is not protected. Estrogen promotes bone density by inhibiting osteoblast apoptosis, stimulating osteoclast apoptosis and suppressing RANKL production, which is why its loss at menopause causes rapid bone loss. Katzung notes that estrogens have no direct effect on 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D production in vitro. His findings are explained by the missing estrogen, so neither growth hormone excess, a parathyroid adenoma nor low calcium intake is needed to account for them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen closes epiphyses and preserves bone mass in both sexes; aromatase or estrogen-receptor deficiency in men gives osteopenia with open epiphyses. Estrogen loss raises RANKL, lowers OPG and lengthens osteoclast survival. COMLEX: Estrogen therapy prevents early postmenopausal bone loss but has fallen out of favor for long-term use because of adverse effects; SERMs aim to keep the bone benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Homeostasis and remodeling of bone","whyNot":{"C":"Katzung notes that estrogens h"}},{"id":"sdl56_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with postmenopausal osteoporosis and a T-score of -3.1 at the hip has been taking denosumab every 6 months for 3 years with excellent bone density response. Due to a change in insurance coverage she abruptly discontinues treatment without transitioning to another agent. Six months later she sustains three new vertebral fractures and repeat DEXA shows rapid bone density loss. Which pharmacologic property of denosumab best explains this rebound phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Denosumab permanently depletes osteoclast precursors from the bone marrow during treatment, causing compensatory hyperproliferation of precursor pools when the drug is discontinued","B":"Denosumab activates OPG gene expression in osteoblasts during therapy, and discontinuation removes this stimulus so osteoblasts dramatically reduce OPG production below pre-treatment baseline levels","C":"Denosumab does not incorporate into the bone mineral matrix the way bisphosphonates do, so when it is cleared the RANKL pathway is no longer blocked and osteoclastogenesis rapidly rebounds","D":"Denosumab has a half-life of less than 24 hours requiring daily dosing, so missing even a single scheduled dose allows rapid and complete recovery of RANKL-driven osteoclast activity","E":"Denosumab cross-reacts with sclerostin during therapy and its discontinuation simultaneously restores both RANKL activity and sclerostin-mediated Wnt inhibition, causing synergistic bone loss"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike bisphosphonates, which bind tightly to hydroxyapatite in bone mineral and remain in the skeleton for years, denosumab circulates as a monoclonal antibody and does not integrate into the bone matrix. Its antiresorptive effect depends entirely on sustained RANKL neutralization while the drug is present. Once denosumab is cleared, RANKL is no longer blocked and osteoclastogenesis rebounds rapidly and fully. This rebound can be so severe that it causes multiple vertebral fractures within months. All patients stopping denosumab must be transitioned to a bisphosphonate or another antiresorptive to maintain the bone density gains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Denosumab discontinuation = rebound osteoclastogenesis → rapid bone loss → multiple vertebral fractures. Mechanism: denosumab does NOT bind bone mineral; effect is fully reversible when drug is cleared. Contrast: bisphosphonates bind bone and retain activity for years after stopping (drug holiday possible). Critical: transition to bisphosphonate when stopping denosumab. COMLEX: This rebound phenomenon is a major COMLEX high-yield clinical pearl — abrupt denosumab discontinuation is potentially catastrophic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Biologic DMARD discontinuation produces analogous disease flare and cytokine rebound when the targeted ligand or receptor is no longer neutralized by the antibody"},{"id":"sdl56_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In a research model, transgenic mice engineered to overexpress OPG throughout their lifespan develop dense, heavy bones that fracture easily when subjected to mechanical testing, despite showing no abnormalities in calcium, phosphate, or PTH. Histology shows obliterated marrow spaces and absent osteoclasts. Which of the following best explains why excess OPG produces brittle bones rather than strong ones?","choices":{"A":"OPG overexpression activates Wnt/beta-catenin signaling excessively in osteoblasts, causing unlimited matrix deposition that becomes brittle due to inadequate cross-linking of rapidly produced collagen","B":"OPG overexpression inhibits calcitonin receptor signaling on osteoclasts through a competitive receptor binding mechanism, paradoxically increasing non-calcitonin-dependent osteoclast activity","C":"OPG overexpression drives FGF23 secretion from osteocytes by relieving RANKL-mediated suppression, causing phosphate wasting and defective hydroxyapatite stoichiometry in new bone","D":"OPG overexpression completely abolishes osteoclast formation by sequestering all available RANKL, preventing the resorption required to remodel disorganized woven bone into load-bearing lamellar architecture","E":"OPG overexpression causes osteoblast apoptosis through a RANK-reverse signaling mechanism that removes the formation component of remodeling while preserving residual osteoclast activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bone strength requires more than density — it requires normal microarchitecture. Osteoclast-driven resorption is not just destructive; it is necessary for replacing old, fatigued, or architecturally disorganized bone with new, well-organized lamellar bone through the bone remodeling unit (BMU) cycle. When OPG is overexpressed and RANKL is entirely sequestered, osteoclastogenesis is abolished and no resorption-based remodeling can occur. Bone accumulates as primary woven bone with disorganized collagen orientation and accumulated microdamage — structurally inferior and brittle despite being dense. This is the cellular basis of osteopetrosis, the genetic equivalent of excess OPG signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteopetrosis = excess bone density with brittle fractures, obliterated marrow (→ anemia, infections, cranial nerve palsies). Mechanism: absent osteoclast function (carbonic anhydrase II mutation, ClC-7 mutation). OPG overexpression = functional osteopetrosis. Key teaching: bone strength requires BOTH formation AND resorption for architectural renewal. COMLEX: Osteopetrosis causes \"marble bone disease\" — stone-hard but fragile bones, pancytopenia from marrow obliteration, hearing loss from cranial foraminal narrowing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Bone homeostasis: osteoclast-driven resorption is an essential partner to osteoblast-driven formation; abolishing either half of the BMU cycle produces pathologic bone"},{"id":"sdl56_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the function of RANK Ligand and OPG in bone metabolism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A research team develops a novel recombinant protein that selectively stimulates RANKL secretion from marrow stromal cells in mice while leaving OPG production unchanged. After 8 weeks of daily administration, microCT imaging and bone histomorphometry are performed. Which skeletal finding would most likely be observed?","choices":{"A":"Increased osteoclast number and activity driven by excess RANKL-RANK-NF-kB signaling would produce accelerated trabecular bone resorption, widened resorption lacunae, and reduced bone mineral density","B":"Increased Wnt/beta-catenin activation through RANKL reverse signaling on LRP5 and LRP6 co-receptors on osteoblasts would paradoxically stimulate new bone formation and increase bone mineral density","C":"Compensatory upregulation of OPG by osteoblasts in response to excess RANKL would neutralize the added RANKL and maintain a normal RANKL-to-OPG ratio without skeletal change","D":"Sclerostin suppression through RANKL feedback on osteocytes would remove the Wnt inhibitory brake on osteoblasts, shifting the net balance toward bone formation and increased density","E":"Neutral effect on bone density because RANKL alone is insufficient to drive osteoclastogenesis without concurrent M-CSF binding to c-Fms on osteoclast precursors in the same marrow niche"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RANKL binding to RANK on osteoclast precursors activates NF-kB, which drives transcription of NFATc1 — the master regulator of osteoclast differentiation. Increasing RANKL without increasing OPG raises the RANKL:OPG ratio and drives excess osteoclastogenesis. In 8 weeks, this would produce increased osteoclast number, deeper and more numerous resorption lacunae on trabecular surfaces, thinning of trabeculae, and reduced bone mineral density — an osteoporosis-like phenotype. This is the mechanism exploited by tumors that secrete RANKL (e.g., multiple myeloma causing lytic lesions) and by estrogen-deficient states that reduce OPG relative to RANKL.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Excess RANKL → excess osteoclastogenesis → bone resorption → osteoporosis/lytic lesions. Multiple myeloma plasma cells produce RANKL (and DKK-1 to inhibit Wnt), causing the lytic bone lesions characteristic of myeloma. Bisphosphonates and denosumab are used in myeloma bone disease for this reason. COMLEX: Malignancy-associated bone loss (metastases, myeloma) uses the same RANKL pathway as postmenopausal osteoporosis — denosumab is approved for both indications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — RA synoviocytes and infiltrating T cells overexpress RANKL in response to TNF-alpha and IL-1, replicating the effect of excess RANKL on periarticular bone and causing erosive joint disease"},{"id":"sdl56_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with long-standing, untreated primary hyperparathyroidism (calcium 12.0 mg/dL, PTH 190 pg/mL) has an iliac crest bone biopsy. Which finding in the trabecular bone is most characteristic of her disease?","choices":{"A":"Wide seams of unmineralized osteoid covering trabecular surfaces, with delayed mineralization of new matrix","B":"A jigsaw mosaic of lamellar bone units joined by prominent cement lines in coarse, thickened trabeculae","C":"Thick trabeculae that retain cores of calcified cartilage because osteoclasts are unable to resorb bone","D":"Osteoclasts tunneling into the centers of trabeculae in a railroad-track pattern, with fibrovascular marrow","E":"Curved trabeculae of woven bone without osteoblast rimming, set in fibrous tissue, from a GNAS mutation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hyperparathyroidism most prominently enhances osteoclast activity within cortical bone, at the subperiosteal and endosteal surfaces, but cancellous bone is also affected. There, osteoclasts may tunnel into and dissect centrally along the length of the trabeculae, and the adjacent marrow spaces are replaced by fibrovascular tissue. This creates a railroad track-like appearance called dissecting osteitis, a misnomer because the lesion is not inflammatory. Increased bone cell activity, peritrabecular fibrosis and cystic brown tumors together make up osteitis fibrosa cystica, the hallmark of severe hyperparathyroidism. Wide unmineralized osteoid seams describe osteomalacia. A mosaic of lamellar bone with prominent cement lines is the hallmark of Paget disease. Retained calcified cartilage from failed osteoclast resorption describes osteopetrosis. Disorganized woven bone in fibrous tissue from a GNAS mutation is fibrous dysplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperparathyroid bone disease: subperiosteal resorption (radial side of the middle phalanges), dissecting osteitis (railroad-track tunneling), brown tumors; together these form osteitis fibrosa cystica. COMLEX: PTH acts on osteoblasts (PTH1R) to raise RANKL; osteoclasts carry no PTH receptor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 3 — RANK ligand and OPG in bone metabolism"},{"id":"sdl56_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has hypercalcemia, an elevated PTH and a single parathyroid adenoma on imaging. Radiographs show subperiosteal resorption of the phalanges and a generalized decrease in bone density. She asks what will happen to her bones if the adenoma is removed. Which response is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The bone changes are permanent because PTH has destroyed the osteoblast precursors, so lifelong anabolic therapy is needed","B":"The bone changes will progress to brown tumors anyway, because osteoclast activity continues once it has been triggered","C":"Removing the autonomously functioning gland can completely reverse the skeletal changes caused by the excess PTH","D":"The bone changes reverse when calcitriol is added, because removal of the adenoma causes permanent vitamin D deficiency","E":"The bone changes will turn into Paget disease, because the sudden fall in PTH uncouples bone resorption from formation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Symptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism can cause osteoporosis (most severe in the phalanges, vertebrae and proximal femur), brown tumors and osteitis fibrosa cystica. Robbins states that surgical removal of the autonomously functioning parathyroid tissue, typically an adenoma, can completely reverse the bone changes. Osteitis fibrosa cystica is now uncommon because hyperparathyroidism is usually found on routine blood tests and treated early; asymptomatic patients are followed with serum calcium, creatinine and bone density. Brown tumors form when ongoing PTH-driven resorption causes microfractures, hemorrhage and fibrous repair, so removing the PTH source removes the drive. Nothing in the sources describes destruction of osteoblast precursors or permanent vitamin D deficiency after parathyroidectomy. Paget disease has its own genetic and environmental causes, such as SQSTM1 mutations, and is not triggered by a fall in PTH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary hyperparathyroidism (usually an adenoma): high Ca, high PTH, low PO4; bone shows osteoporosis, subperiosteal resorption, brown tumors and osteitis fibrosa cystica. COMLEX: Parathyroidectomy can fully reverse the skeletal changes; secondary hyperparathyroidism causes similar but milder changes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Bone lesion classification: brown tumors must be distinguished from giant cell tumors and other benign-appearing lytic lesions using clinical context including serum calcium and PTH","whyNot":{"B":"Brown tumors form when ongoing"}},{"id":"sdl56_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the process resulting in bone density occurring with hyperparathyroidism.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with CKD stage 4 is found to have a PTH of 420 pg/mL, calcium of 8.0 mg/dL, and phosphate of 5.8 mg/dL on routine labs. Her nephrologist diagnoses secondary hyperparathyroidism and initiates phosphate binders and calcitriol. She asks how her bone disease differs from that of her friend who had a parathyroid adenoma removed last year. Which of the following best distinguishes the skeletal picture of her secondary hyperparathyroidism from her friend's primary hyperparathyroidism?","choices":{"A":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism causes exclusively osteosclerotic changes throughout the skeleton from calcium phosphate deposition, while primary hyperparathyroidism produces only osteolytic lesions","B":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD co-occurs with osteomalacia from impaired vitamin D activation and osteosclerosis (rugger jersey spine), a mixed picture not seen in primary hyperparathyroidism","C":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism produces a higher frequency of brown tumor formation and pathologic fractures than primary hyperparathyroidism because the PTH elevation is more extreme and sustained","D":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is distinguished by autonomous parathyroid gland enlargement that does not respond to calcium supplementation, while primary hyperparathyroidism shows normal gland anatomy","E":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism causes preferential cortical bone loss at the femoral neck and distal radius, while primary hyperparathyroidism causes exclusively cancellous vertebral bone loss"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In secondary hyperparathyroidism from CKD, the bone disease is far more complex than primary hyperparathyroidism. In addition to PTH-driven osteoclastic bone changes (osteitis fibrosa cystica), the failing kidney also produces inadequate 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, causing concurrent osteomalacia from impaired mineralization. Very high PTH also drives osteoblastic new bone at the vertebral endplates, producing osteosclerosis (rugger jersey spine). Adynamic bone disease from over-suppressed PTH may also be present. Primary hyperparathyroidism presents only with excess osteoclastic activity in the setting of high calcium; the renal and vitamin D components of renal osteodystrophy are absent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal osteodystrophy = mixed disease: osteitis fibrosa (secondary HPT) + osteomalacia (low 1,25-OH2D) + osteosclerosis (rugger jersey spine) + adynamic bone. Primary HPT = isolated PTH excess, high calcium, normal vitamin D, osteitis fibrosa only. Rugger jersey spine = dense sclerotic bands at vertebral endplates on lateral X-ray, pathognomonic for renal osteodystrophy. COMLEX: Cinacalcet (calcimimetic) activates calcium-sensing receptors on parathyroid cells to suppress PTH — used for secondary HPT in CKD on dialysis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Lab panel interpretation: the combination of low calcium, high phosphate, elevated PTH, low 1,25-OH2D, and elevated ALP distinguishes secondary HPT from primary and guides management","whyNot":{"A":"In addition to PTH-driven oste"}},{"id":"sdl56_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old housebound woman with severe vitamin D deficiency and a 70-year-old woman with postmenopausal osteoporosis both have low bone density and vertebral fractures. Which statement correctly contrasts their bone at biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Both show normally mineralized bone, and they differ in the rate of bone loss","B":"The vitamin D-deficient bone shows a chaotic mosaic of woven and lamellar bone with cement lines","C":"Vitamin D-deficient bone is undermineralized; osteoporotic bone is mineralized but reduced in mass","D":"The vitamin D-deficient bone shows dense sclerosis, while the osteoporotic bone shows lytic cysts","E":"Both show widened osteoid seams, and they differ in the serum PTH level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Osteomalacia, the adult form of impaired mineralization usually caused by abnormal vitamin D metabolism or deficiency, leaves bone formed during remodeling undermineralized and prone to fracture. Osteoporosis contrasts with it: mineralization is normal, but bone mass is decreased. Normally mineralized bone in both patients does not fit osteomalacia, and an undermineralized pattern in both does not fit osteoporosis, whatever the PTH level. A mosaic of woven and lamellar bone with cement lines is Paget disease. Dense sclerosis and lytic cysts are not the contrast between these two disorders. Sources: course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, osteomalacia and rickets, p24; osteoporosis, p20).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rickets is impaired mineralization in children, affecting bone deposition at the growth plates; osteomalacia is the adult counterpart (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: vitamin D deficiency lowers calcium and phosphate absorption and triggers secondary hyperparathyroidism (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 56 → Obj 2 — postmenopausal osteoporosis"},{"id":"sdl56_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a 20-year history of Crohn disease involving the terminal ileum has undergone two bowel resections totaling 80 cm. She presents with diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, and a waddling gait. Serum labs: calcium 7.8 mg/dL, phosphate 2.2 mg/dL, PTH 145 pg/mL, ALP 380 U/L, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 9 ng/mL. Bone biopsy shows widened osteoid seams and reduced tetracycline double-label width. Which of the following best explains why this patient's bowel disease predisposed her to this bone disorder?","choices":{"A":"Resection of the terminal ileum disrupts the enterohepatic circulation of bile acids required for fat absorption, impairing absorption of fat-soluble vitamin D from both dietary sources and oral supplements","B":"Resection of the terminal ileum eliminates the primary site of active transcellular calcium transport that is mediated by calbindin-D9k, bypassing 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D-dependent intestinal calcium uptake","C":"Resection of the terminal ileum depletes intrinsic factor stores critical for cobalamin absorption, causing cobalamin deficiency that directly impairs osteoblast DNA synthesis and bone matrix production","D":"Resection of the terminal ileum allows bacterial overgrowth that produces urease, generating ammonia that directly inhibits 25-hydroxylase activity in hepatocytes and blocks vitamin D activation","E":"Resection of the terminal ileum causes chronic diarrhea that accelerates renal calcium excretion, creating negative calcium balance that depletes skeletal mineral stores through secondary PTH activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin that requires micellar solubilization by bile acids for intestinal absorption. The terminal ileum is the primary site of bile acid reabsorption (enterohepatic circulation). After terminal ileum resection, bile acids are lost into the colon rather than being recycled, reducing the bile acid pool available for fat-soluble vitamin absorption. The result is malabsorption of vitamins D, A, E, and K. Chronic vitamin D malabsorption leads to osteomalacia — characterized by widened osteoid seams, low calcium, low phosphate, elevated PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism), and elevated ALP. Tetracycline-double labeling showing reduced inter-label width confirms slow mineralization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption in ileal disease/resection = vitamins A, D, E, K. Vitamin D malabsorption → osteomalacia. Labs: low Ca + low PO4 + high PTH + high ALP + low 25-OH-D + widened osteoid seams on biopsy. Key clinical clue: proximal muscle weakness + bone pain = osteomalacia until proven otherwise. COMLEX: Crohn disease and celiac disease are the two most common GI causes of vitamin D malabsorption — both are tested as board triggers for metabolic bone disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Metabolic panel in osteomalacia: low Ca + low PO4 + elevated PTH + elevated ALP + low 25-OH-D forms a recognizable biochemical pattern distinguishable from osteoporosis (normal labs) and primary HPT (high Ca)"},{"id":"sdl56_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — List the bone changes related to vitamin D deficiency.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed osteomalacia from nutritional vitamin D deficiency is started on high-dose vitamin D3 and calcium supplementation. Her baseline labs showed calcium 7.6 mg/dL, phosphate 2.4 mg/dL, PTH 188 pg/mL, and ALP 312 U/L. After 3 months of therapy, which set of laboratory changes best confirms a meaningful therapeutic response?","choices":{"A":"Serum ALP falling to undetectable levels within the first month confirms complete remineralization of all osteoid, and urinary calcium dropping to zero confirms that no further calcium supplementation is required","B":"Serum calcium rising toward normal with a fall in PTH confirms that the secondary hyperparathyroidism is resolving, and eventual narrowing of osteoid seams on follow-up biopsy confirms remineralization","C":"Serum sclerostin falling to below-normal levels confirms that the Wnt signaling inhibitory brake has been completely removed and osteoblast-driven mineralization is proceeding at maximum capacity","D":"Serum phosphate rising above the upper limit of normal combined with urinary hydroxyproline increasing above baseline confirms that excess remineralization is occurring at an appropriate therapeutic rate","E":"Serum calcitonin rising above the upper reference limit confirms that osteoclast-mediated bone resorption has been fully suppressed and the anabolic mineralization phase is underway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In osteomalacia, vitamin D deficiency causes hypocalcemia, which drives secondary hyperparathyroidism (elevated PTH). As vitamin D is repleted and calcium absorption normalizes, serum calcium rises, and the PTH falls toward normal (secondary hyperparathyroidism resolves). This normalizing PTH is an early and reliable marker of response. ALP may transiently rise as remineralization accelerates (the hungry bone phenomenon) before eventually returning to normal. Confirmation of successful bone remineralization ultimately requires a follow-up bone biopsy showing narrowing of osteoid seams, though this is not routinely performed in clinical practice. Serum 25-OH-D rising into the sufficient range (20 ng/mL per the National Academy of Medicine, 30 ng/mL per the Endocrine Society; Katzung) and normalization of calcium, phosphate, and PTH are the practical clinical endpoints.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Treatment response markers in osteomalacia: PTH falls as Ca normalizes; ALP may initially rise (hungry bone), then fall; serum 25-OH-D rises above 20 ng/mL (NAM) or 30 ng/mL (Endocrine Society). Hungry bone syndrome after parathyroid surgery = rapid calcium influx into newly mineralizing bone → hypocalcemia (the inverse scenario). COMLEX: In osteomalacia from nutritional vitamin D deficiency, calcium supplementation must accompany vitamin D or remineralization will fail even with adequate vitamin D levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — PTH normalization after vitamin D repletion correlates with reduction in IL-6-driven osteoclastogenesis, connecting calcium homeostasis restoration to cytokine-mediated inflammatory pathway normalization","whyNot":{"A":"ALP may transiently rise as re"}},{"id":"sdl56_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with end-stage renal disease on hemodialysis for 9 years presents with worsening bone pain and two pathologic rib fractures. Labs: calcium 7.6 mg/dL, phosphate 6.8 mg/dL, PTH 1100 pg/mL, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D undetectable. Lateral thoracic spine X-ray shows alternating dense and less dense horizontal bands across the vertebral bodies. Which of the following best explains this characteristic radiographic finding?","choices":{"A":"Calcium phosphate precipitation along vertebral endplates from hyperphosphatemia-driven metastatic calcification in the soft tissue adjacent to endplates produces the dense band pattern","B":"Alternating zones of osteolytic brown tumor formation and calcified fibrous tissue that develop along the vertebral endplates in renal osteodystrophy produce the banded pattern","C":"Dense periosteal new bone formation stimulated by FGF23-dependent osteoblast activation along the superior and inferior endplates creates the alternating density pattern on plain film","D":"Dense osteosclerosis at vertebral endplates driven by PTH-stimulated osteoblasts contrasts with less dense central cancellous bone, producing the rugger jersey spine","E":"Symmetric collapse of the superior and inferior endplates from osteomalacic bone softening alternating with preserved central cortical bone produces the banded appearance in uremic patients"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rugger jersey spine is a classic radiographic finding of renal osteodystrophy with secondary hyperparathyroidism. Markedly elevated PTH drives intense osteoblastic new bone formation preferentially at the vertebral endplates (subendplate regions), where the bone is most metabolically active. This creates zones of dense osteosclerosis at the endplates that contrast with the less dense central cancellous bone of the vertebral body. The resulting alternating dense-lucent-dense horizontal banding on lateral spine X-ray resembles the horizontal stripes of a rugby jersey. It is pathognomonic for secondary hyperparathyroidism in the context of chronic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rugger jersey spine = alternating dense endplates and lucent central vertebral bodies on lateral X-ray. Pathognomonic for secondary HPT in CKD. Mechanism: PTH-driven osteoblast hyperactivity at endplates produces subendplate sclerosis. COMLEX: Other classic renal osteodystrophy X-ray findings: subperiosteal resorption of radial phalanges, periarticular calcifications from calcium-phosphate deposits, and arterial calcifications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Lab panel in ESRD: PTH above 500 pg/mL + low Ca + high PO4 + undetectable 1,25-OH2D = severe secondary HPT driving the osteosclerosis and osteitis fibrosa components of renal osteodystrophy"},{"id":"sdl56_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with end-stage renal disease on peritoneal dialysis for 6 years has been aggressively managed with high-dose calcitriol and calcium-based phosphate binders. Her current PTH is 52 pg/mL. Bone biopsy shows markedly reduced osteoid thickness, absent double tetracycline label, and obliterated remodeling activity. Despite having a T-score of -1.4, she sustains a hip fracture from a minor fall. Which of the following best explains her elevated fracture risk despite relatively preserved bone density?","choices":{"A":"Her fracture risk is paradoxically low because absent osteoclast activity preserves bone mass and microarchitecture, and her hip fracture must reflect an unrelated traumatic mechanism","B":"Adynamic bone disease with absent remodeling prevents repair of accumulated microcrack damage, causing progressive mechanical brittleness that increases fracture risk independent of bone mineral density","C":"Her low PTH suppresses osteoblast-derived growth factors including TGF-beta and IGF-1 that are required for collagen maturation, causing a qualitative collagen defect that weakens bone independent of density","D":"Her fracture risk is entirely explained by osteomalacia from incomplete remineralization of osteoid, which softens bones and reduces their resistance to the compressive forces of daily activity","E":"Her fracture risk is driven by calcitriol toxicity causing hypervitaminosis D that activates osteoclasts autonomously through 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D receptor-independent mechanisms in bone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adynamic bone disease is a low-turnover form of renal osteodystrophy in which bone remodeling is markedly suppressed — often from over-treatment with calcitriol and/or calcium-based binders that suppress PTH too aggressively (PTH suppressed too low in dialysis patients). Without active osteoclast-driven resorption followed by osteoblast-driven renewal, microdamage from daily mechanical loading accumulates without repair. This leads to progressive brittleness and increased fracture risk despite relatively preserved bone mineral density on DEXA. Both extremes of bone turnover — too high (secondary HPT) and too low (adynamic) — increase fracture risk in CKD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adynamic bone disease = absent remodeling in CKD, often from PTH over-suppression. Biopsy: absent osteoid, no tetracycline labeling. Paradox: preserved BMD on DEXA but elevated fracture risk from microdamage accumulation. PTH in dialysis should be kept high enough to maintain adequate but not excess bone turnover. COMLEX: Over-suppressed PTH in dialysis = adynamic bone disease risk; markedly high PTH = high-turnover osteitis fibrosa risk. Both increase fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 1 — Bone architectural integrity requires active remodeling; adynamic bone disease and pagetic bone share the failure to renew microarchitecture despite opposite mechanisms of turnover dysregulation","whyNot":{"A":"This leads to progressive brit"}},{"id":"sdl56_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the bone changes associated with chronic renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has a serum bicarbonate of 15 mEq/L from impaired tubular acid handling, and his DEXA shows progressive osteopenia. Apart from changes in calcium, phosphate and vitamin D, which mechanism links his acidosis directly to bone loss?","choices":{"A":"Acidosis raises renal Klotho expression, so FGF-23 causes phosphate wasting and a rickets-like failure of mineralization","B":"Acidosis makes osteoblasts secrete more OPG, which speeds the maturation of osteoclast precursors into resorbing cells","C":"Acidosis precipitates calcium phosphate in the marrow, which forms the sclerotic bands seen at the vertebral endplates","D":"Acidosis suppresses PTH secretion, producing adynamic bone with very little osteoclastic or osteoblastic activity","E":"Acidosis increases RANKL expression on osteoblasts, which raises osteoclast activity and the resorption of bone matrix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kidney disease damages the skeleton through several linked mechanisms. Tubular dysfunction causes tubular and systemic acidosis, and acidosis increases RANKL expression on osteoblasts; the rise in osteoclast activity causes matrix resorption and osteopenia. Separately, secondary hyperparathyroidism develops from hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia, which stem from decreased renal expression of Klotho (the receptor for the phosphaturic hormone FGF-23) and from decreased renal production of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D; the net effect also includes diminished calcification of matrix (osteomalacia). Klotho falls rather than rises in kidney disease, and the result is phosphate retention, not wasting. OPG is a decoy receptor that blocks RANKL, so more OPG would inhibit osteoclast formation. The endplate sclerosis of rugger-jersey spine reflects PTH-driven bone formation, not calcium phosphate precipitation. Adynamic bone is a low-turnover pattern; the sources do not attribute it to acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal osteodystrophy: acidosis (RANKL up, resorption, osteopenia); low Klotho and low 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D give hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia, secondary hyperparathyroidism and osteomalacia; growth retardation in children. COMLEX: Bone histology in end-stage renal failure ranges from high-turnover to low-turnover (adynamic) and mixed disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 56 Obj 3 — RANK ligand and OPG in bone metabolism","whyNot":{"B":"OPG is a decoy receptor that b"}},{"id":"sdl56_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 76-year-old man presents with a 4-year history of progressive skull enlargement requiring larger hat sizes, bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, and increasing frontal headaches. His ALP is 920 U/L while serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH are all normal. Skull X-ray shows a mixture of lytic areas and dense sclerotic patches described as a cotton wool appearance. Which phase of Paget disease does this presentation represent, and what is happening histologically at this stage?","choices":{"A":"This represents the lytic phase, in which osteoclasts are resorbing bone rapidly without any compensatory osteoblastic response, producing purely lucent areas on skull radiographs and low ALP","B":"This represents the burned-out sclerotic phase, in which all osteoclast activity has ceased and dense disorganized lamellar bone remains without active remodeling or biochemical marker elevation","C":"This represents the mixed phase, in which simultaneous osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic bone formation produce the mosaic cement-line pattern and the cotton wool radiographic appearance","D":"This represents the malignant transformation phase, in which osteoclasts have undergone sarcomatous change and are now invading the skull base and producing the radiographic density changes","E":"This represents the inflammatory phase, in which viral inclusion bodies in pagetic osteoclasts trigger macrophage recruitment and granulomatous inflammation creating the mixed radiographic appearance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paget disease progresses through three phases: the lytic phase (predominantly osteoclastic, seen as a flame-shaped or wedge-shaped lucency on X-ray), the mixed phase (concurrent osteoclastic and osteoblastic activity, markedly elevated ALP, cotton wool appearance from patches of sclerosis against a background of residual lucency), and the sclerotic/burned-out phase (predominantly dense disorganized bone, mildly elevated ALP). This patient has markedly elevated ALP with the cotton wool appearance, indicating active mixed-phase disease. Skull involvement causes hearing loss from compression of the cochlea or ossicles, and the expanding skull creates the characteristic hat-size increase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease phases: lytic (osteoclastic, flame-shaped lucency), mixed (cotton wool, markedly elevated ALP), sclerotic (burned out, dense bone, modest ALP). Labs: markedly elevated ALP; NORMAL Ca, PO4, PTH — key differentiator from HPT. Skull complications: hearing loss (cochlea/ossicle compression), hat size increase, cranial nerve palsies, high-output CHF. COMLEX: Treat active symptomatic Paget disease with bisphosphonates (zoledronate preferred) or calcitonin to reduce osteoclast activity and lower ALP.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 54 Obj 2 — Secondary osteosarcoma arising in Paget disease is driven by TP53 and RB inactivation in osteoblast lineage cells, the same molecular drivers as high-grade primary osteosarcoma"},{"id":"sdl56_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with longstanding polyostotic Paget disease involving the femur, pelvis, and skull presents with sudden severe thigh pain after standing from a chair. X-ray shows a transverse fracture through the mid-diaphysis of the femur without significant trauma. The radiologist notes that the affected femur is diffusely thickened, bowed anteriorly, and shows the mixed sclerotic and lytic density changes of pagetic bone. Which of the following best explains why structurally thicker pagetic bone fractures with minimal force?","choices":{"A":"Pagetic bone is hypervascular from arteriovenous shunting, and the resulting increase in intraosseous hydraulic pressure generates fatigue stress on the cortex that produces spontaneous transverse fractures","B":"Pagetic bone is mechanically inferior despite increased bulk because the chaotic mosaic of woven and lamellar bone produces disorganized collagen fiber orientation with poor tensile strength and resistance to bending forces","C":"Pagetic bone develops stress-shielding from abnormal bowing geometry that concentrates compressive forces on the concave side, causing cortical disuse osteoporosis that predisposes to fracture at the convex surface","D":"Pagetic bone accumulates calcium phosphate deposits in haversian canals that block normal osteocyte canalicular signaling, preventing mechanosensory-driven microcrack repair and causing brittle fracture","E":"Pagetic bone develops subperiosteal hemorrhage from hypervascularity that creates stress risers in the cortex, propagating cracks transversely through the diaphysis under physiologic bending loads"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pagetic bone is dense but architecturally inferior. The hallmark of Paget disease — the mosaic pattern of alternating woven and lamellar bone separated by irregular cement lines — produces bone with disorganized collagen fiber orientation. Normal cortical bone derives its tensile and bending strength from precisely oriented Type I collagen fibers within osteons. Pagetic bone lacks this ordered architecture, making it brittle despite its bulk, much like disorganized concrete. The result is the chalk-stick transverse fracture, where the bone snaps cleanly across the diaphysis under bending or torsional loads that normal bone would easily withstand. The bowing of the femur in Paget disease further concentrates stress.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pagetic bone = dense but brittle from disorganized collagen architecture. Chalk-stick fracture = transverse diaphyseal fracture characteristic of pagetic long bones. Also: bowing of the tibia (saber shin) is classic for tibial involvement. Treatment: bisphosphonates reduce osteoclast activity, lower ALP, and may reduce fracture risk. Surgical fixation of pagetic fractures is challenging due to hard, bleeding bone. COMLEX: In polyostotic Paget, the combination of high-output heart failure (AV shunting), hearing loss, and chalk-stick fractures covers the major systemic complications to know.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 3 — Soft tissue sarcomas with complex karyotypes also arise in pre-existing bone lesions, sharing the principle that architectural disorganization of matrix predisposes to genomic instability and malignant transformation"},{"id":"sdl56_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the abnormal pathophysiology resulting in Paget's disease of the bone.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man of English descent has polyostotic Paget disease; his father and an uncle had the same disease. Biopsy of a lytic lesion in the femur shows numerous abnormally large osteoclasts, some with about 100 nuclei. Genetic testing finds a germline mutation shared by the affected relatives. Which mechanism best links the mutation to his disease?","choices":{"A":"An FBN1 mutation releases excess TGF-beta from the bone matrix, and the TGF-beta recruits giant osteoclasts","B":"A COL1A1 mutation impairs triple-helix formation, so osteoclasts resorb the fragile bone after microfractures","C":"A RANK-inactivating mutation slows osteoclast differentiation, so the few osteoclasts that form grow oversized","D":"An OPG-activating mutation traps RANKL in the marrow, so the rare osteoclasts that do form fuse into giant cells","E":"An SQSTM1 mutation increases NF-kappa-B activity in the osteoclast lineage, so osteoclast activity is increased"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cause of Paget disease is uncertain, but there are genetic and environmental contributions. About 60% of familial cases and 10% of sporadic cases carry germline mutations in SQSTM1, which encodes sequestosome-1. These mutations appear to increase NF-kappa-B activity and thus osteoclast activity. The initial lytic phase is marked by numerous abnormally large osteoclasts, which may have 100 or more nuclei. Some cases of juvenile Paget disease are caused by activating mutations in RANK and inactivating mutations in OPG, the opposite of the mutations offered; both raise, rather than lower, osteoclast formation. The geographic pattern (common in the United Kingdom, central Europe and Greece, rare in Scandinavia, China, Japan and Africa) suggests an environmental factor, and infection of osteoclast precursors by measles or other RNA viruses has been proposed but not established. FBN1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome, and COL1A1 mutations cause osteogenesis imperfecta.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paget disease: SQSTM1 mutations (about 60% familial, 10% sporadic) raise NF-kappa-B and osteoclast activity; lytic, mixed and sclerotic phases; mosaic lamellar bone; high ALP with normal Ca and PO4. COMLEX: About 85% of cases are polyostotic and many are found incidentally on imaging; secondary osteosarcoma is rare.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 55 Obj 1 — Homeostasis and remodeling of bone","whyNot":{"A":"FBN1 mutations cause Marfan sy","B":"FBN1 mutations cause Marfan sy","D":"Some cases of juvenile Paget d"}},{"id":"sdl56_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman with severe postmenopausal osteoporosis, T-score -3.8 at the hip, and three vertebral fractures in the past 2 years is started on teriparatide 20 mcg subcutaneously daily. Her physician explains that the drug works anabolically at this intermittent dose. She asks why the same PTH hormone that causes bone loss in hyperparathyroidism is now being given to build bone. Which of the following best explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Intermittent PTH-1R stimulation from a daily subcutaneous dose preferentially drives osteoblast differentiation and survival, whereas sustained PTH elevation upregulates RANKL and drives osteoclastic resorption","B":"Intermittent dosing allows teriparatide to accumulate in bone mineral over time where it inhibits osteoclast H-ATPase, while in hyperparathyroidism the continuous PTH instead activates calcitonin secretion","C":"Intermittent dosing selectively phosphorylates sclerostin at a PTH-dependent site, permanently inactivating it and sustaining Wnt-driven bone formation independent of ongoing PTH-1R stimulation","D":"Intermittent dosing generates calcitonin co-secretion from parafollicular C cells that suppresses osteoclast activity between PTH pulses, while chronic hyperparathyroidism lacks this co-secretory protective response","E":"Intermittent dosing upregulates OPG expression in osteoblasts during the drug-free period, shifting the RANKL-to-OPG ratio toward antiresorption in the hours between once-daily doses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The anabolic vs. catabolic paradox of PTH is resolved by the concept of intermittent vs. continuous receptor stimulation. PTH-1R on osteoblasts signals through different downstream pathways depending on duration of activation. Brief daily peaks (as with subcutaneous teriparatide) preferentially activate osteoblast differentiation from progenitors, suppress osteoblast apoptosis, and drive sclerostin suppression — collectively increasing bone formation above resorption. Sustained PTH receptor stimulation (as in primary hyperparathyroidism) instead chronically upregulates RANKL on osteoblasts, driving dominant osteoclastogenesis and cortical bone loss. The net result of identical receptor but different signaling duration is opposite skeletal outcomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Teriparatide intermittent = anabolic (osteoblast differentiation, sclerostin suppression, IGF-1). Continuous PTH = catabolic (RANKL upregulation, osteoclastogenesis). Max 2 years; follow with antiresorptive. SE: transient hypercalcemia, dizziness after first dose. Contraindications: Paget disease, prior bone radiation, unexplained elevated ALP, pediatric patients with open epiphyses. COMLEX: Teriparatide is the first-choice anabolic for severe glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis and for patients who have failed antiresorptive therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Teriparatide as a recombinant peptide hormone illustrates the biologic drug principle: the target receptor, dose, and signaling duration determine whether the pharmacologic effect is anabolic or catabolic"},{"id":"sdl56_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with osteoporosis is prescribed once-weekly alendronate 70 mg and given standard discharge instructions to take it on an empty stomach in the morning. Two weeks later she calls reporting severe pain with swallowing and a burning sensation behind her sternum that started the day after her first dose. Endoscopy reveals an esophageal ulceration at the gastroesophageal junction. Which specific counseling point, if provided at initiation, most likely would have prevented this complication?","choices":{"A":"Instructing the patient to take alendronate with a full glass of milk would have buffered its acidic properties and neutralized any caustic effect on the esophageal mucosa before the tablet dissolved","B":"Instructing the patient to crush the alendronate tablet and dissolve it in warm water would have eliminated the solid tablet contact with the esophageal epithelium during transit","C":"Instructing the patient to take alendronate with antacids would have neutralized its local acidity and formed a mucosal protective layer against direct esophageal irritation","D":"Instructing the patient to take alendronate at bedtime rather than in the morning would have allowed gravity-independent transit and reduced the duration of esophageal exposure during sleep","E":"Instructing the patient to stay upright for at least 30 minutes after taking alendronate with a full glass of water would have allowed esophageal clearance and prevented mucosal contact"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bisphosphonate-induced esophageal injury results from direct prolonged contact of the tablet or its contents with the esophageal mucosa, causing chemical irritation and ulceration. The critical preventive measures are: (1) swallow with a full glass (8 oz) of plain water — dairy and antacids impair absorption and must be avoided; (2) take on an empty stomach first thing in the morning; (3) remain upright (sitting or standing) for at least 30-60 minutes after taking the dose to ensure esophageal clearance by gravity and prevent reflux of the drug back into the esophagus. Lying down or taking alendronate with insufficient water allows the tablet to sit in the esophagus or reflux back. Bedtime dosing would be the worst option as lying prone prevents clearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bisphosphonate instructions: empty stomach, full glass of plain water, remain upright 30-60 minutes, no food for 30-60 minutes. SE: esophagitis and esophageal ulceration (must stay upright), osteonecrosis of jaw (especially with dental procedures), atypical subtrochanteric femur fracture (with long-term use). IV zoledronate avoids GI side effects. COMLEX: Osteonecrosis of the jaw with bisphosphonates is most common with IV formulations used for bone metastases at high doses — less common with oral doses for osteoporosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — Adverse effect profiles by class: GI toxicity is a major limitation of oral disease-modifying agents including bisphosphonates and NSAIDs; switching to parenteral formulations can mitigate this class effect","whyNot":{"A":"The critical preventive measur","C":"The critical preventive measur","D":"Bedtime dosing would be the wo"}},{"id":"sdl56_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the classes and mechanisms of action in medications approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with postmenopausal osteoporosis and a T-score of -3.5 at the hip is referred for consideration of romosozumab therapy. Her endocrinologist reviews her history and notes she had a myocardial infarction 6 months ago, currently managed with dual antiplatelet therapy and a statin. She also has well-controlled hypertension. Before prescribing romosozumab, which of the following statements about its safety profile is most accurate and clinically important?","choices":{"A":"Romosozumab carries no cardiovascular contraindications and is the preferred first-line anabolic agent for all patients with severe osteoporosis regardless of prior cardiac or cerebrovascular history","B":"Romosozumab increases cardiovascular risk only in patients with uncontrolled hypertension, and her well-controlled blood pressure with medication means the cardiovascular boxed warning does not apply to her","C":"Romosozumab carries an FDA boxed warning for increased risk of myocardial infarction and stroke and should not be used in patients at risk of coronary or cerebrovascular events, making her an unsuitable candidate","D":"Romosozumab is safe to use in patients on dual antiplatelet therapy because the mechanism of cardiovascular risk involves arterial inflammation rather than platelet-mediated thrombus formation","E":"Romosozumab cardiovascular risk is limited to the first injection and is fully mitigated by pretreatment with aspirin and a loading dose of a P2Y12 inhibitor before initiation of therapy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Romosozumab carries a boxed warning for increased risk of myocardial infarction and stroke; it should not be used in patients with risks of stroke or coronary vascular disease (Katzung Ch 42, SDL 56 reading). This patient had an MI 6 months ago, so she falls in that group. Alternatives for her would include teriparatide or abaloparatide (anabolics without the cardiovascular warning) as initial therapy, with transition to an antiresorptive when complete. Romosozumab is approved for a maximum of 12 months of therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Romosozumab = anti-sclerostin antibody, dual anabolic and antiresorptive, approved 12 months only, then transition to antiresorptive. FDA boxed warning: increased MI and stroke risk — do NOT use in patients with recent MI or stroke or high coronary or stroke risk. Contrast with teriparatide and abaloparatide, which carry an osteosarcoma-in-rats warning but no cardiovascular boxed warning. COMLEX: For a patient with recent MI and severe osteoporosis, teriparatide or abaloparatide is the appropriate anabolic choice; defer romosozumab until cardiovascular risk window has passed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — FDA boxed warnings in biologic agents reflect class-specific serious adverse effects that require mandatory risk-benefit counseling — romosozumab cardiovascular risk parallels the infection risk warnings on TNF inhibitors"}]};
